{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014bE86dtw8W89Bn6TJdPx4V","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tCITATION RULES — GAP-FOCUSED TOKEN DOCUMENTS (ENHANCE)\n2\t\n3\tGoal\n4\t- Define how enhance session outputs represent and use citations when operating over `<id>.compile.review.md`.\n5\t- Ensure that no content previously audited as FALSE is promoted as evidence, and that all new/corrected claims are backed by explicit external sources.\n6\t\n7\tCitation Representation (MANDATORY)\n8\t- Enhance sessions use TOKEN citations; do NOT use [@Key] here.\n9\t- Supported tokens:\n10\t  - Knowledge Base / compiled review documents: `[KB:filename.md]` (including `<id>.compile.review.md` and any underlying KB `.md` files when they act as context).\n11\t  - External web sources: `[WEB:https://full-url]`.\n12\t  - ArXiv preprints: `[ARXIV:paper-id]`.\n13\t  - DOI-identified works: `[DOI:10.0000/xyz123]`.\n14\t  - Optional Zotero items: `[ZOTERO:item_key]` when a Zotero MCP is used.\n15\t- Do **not** convert token citations to [@Key] in enhance workflows.\n16\t- Do **not** remove any valid citation tokens that appear in the draft.\n17\t\n18\tPlacement Rules\n19\t- Place citations immediately after the sentence they support.\n20\t- Multiple citations are allowed, e.g.:\n21\t  - `... [KB:doc1.md][WEB:https://...]`.\n22\t  - `... [KB:hazard.compile.review.md][DOI:10.0000/xyz123]`.\n23\t- Never place citation tokens inside Markdown code blocks (fenced or indented). If you need to cite evidence about code, cite in prose outside the code block.\n24\t- Make clear, across the document, which claims are grounded in the audited KB and which in external sources.\n25\t\n26\tEvidence Policy (ENHANCE-SPECIFIC)\n27\t- `<id>.compile.review.md` provides the starting point and context for enhance sessions, but it is **not** sufficient on its own as supporting evidence.\n28\t- Paragraphs whose audit Rationales (or legacy `Verdict:` labels) indicate that the KB paragraph is **factually wrong or contradicted by evidence** MUST NOT be treated as positive evidence for any claim.\n29\t- Paragraphs whose Rationales describe uncertainty or mixed/insufficient evidence, or whose `Confidence` is LOW, may be used only to frame uncertainty, never as sole support for a factual statement.\n30\t- For sections whose audit Rationales state that the KB paragraph is well supported and whose `Confidence` is HIGH (including any legacy `Verdict: TRUE` labels):\n31\t-  - You may treat the audited text as KB-style context (`[KB:...]`).\n32\t-  - Any **new or expanded** claims beyond what was already audited as well supported with HIGH confidence MUST be backed by explicit external citations `[WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:/ZOTERO:]`.\n33\t- For gaps (FALSE, UNCERTAIN, LOW confidence, missing or partially covered objectives):\n34\t  - All corrective or gap-filling paragraphs MUST include external citations.\n35\t  - When several external sources disagree, cite the most authoritative ones and reflect the remaining uncertainty in the audit footnote.\n36\t\n37\tCoverage Rules\n38\t- For every major enhancement area (objective or gap), ensure that at least one external citation is present in the paragraphs that close that gap.\n39\t- Keep `[KB:...]` tokens for:\n40\t  - Referring back to specific reviewed sections in `<id>.compile.review.md`.\n41\t  - Marking when you reuse or paraphrase TRUE/HIGH content.\n42\t- Do **not** rely solely on `[KB:...]` in new paragraphs; at least one external token should appear wherever you introduce new factual material.\n43\t\n44\tDeduplication\n45\t- Within a paragraph, avoid redundant repetition of the same token unless it improves clarity.\n46\t- Across the document:\n47\t  - Reuse the same token when referring to the same underlying source.\n48\t  - Prefer a small number of strong sources over many weak or redundant WEB links.\n49\t\n50\tInteraction with Audit Rules\n51\t- Every paragraph in `<id>.enhance.md` will receive a footnote per `rules/audit.md`.\n52\t- When writing the audit Rationale, refer back to the same tokens you used in the paragraph body:\n53\t-  - Example: \"Supported by `[DOI:10.0000/xyz123]` and `[WEB:https://...]`; previous KB paragraph was judged incorrect due to outdated values.\"\n54\t- If evidence remains genuinely insufficient, explain this clearly in the Rationale and choose a cautious `Confidence` level instead of over-stating certainty.\n55\t\n56\tOutput Requirements\n57\t- Enhance session outputs MUST:\n58\t  - Keep all citations in TOKEN form only.\n59\t  - Use `[KB:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:/ZOTERO:]` consistently according to this policy.\n60\t  - Never treat previously FALSE-audited content as standalone evidence.\n61\t  - Provide enough external citations that compile-enhance can later map tokens to `[@Key]` and generate a complete `biblatex.bib`.\n62\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bf7093-818d-4370-9615-a0aeec356215","uuid":"b4931483-65a6-4d4a-a272-774aa124000f","timestamp":"2026-03-30T01:27:58.144Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/usr/local/libexec/tito/rules/citations.enhance.md","content":"CITATION RULES — GAP-FOCUSED TOKEN DOCUMENTS (ENHANCE)\n\nGoal\n- Define how enhance session outputs represent and use citations when operating over `<id>.compile.review.md`.\n- Ensure that no content previously audited as FALSE is promoted as evidence, and that all new/corrected claims are backed by explicit external sources.\n\nCitation Representation (MANDATORY)\n- Enhance sessions use TOKEN citations; do NOT use [@Key] here.\n- Supported tokens:\n  - Knowledge Base / compiled review documents: `[KB:filename.md]` (including `<id>.compile.review.md` and any underlying KB `.md` files when they act as context).\n  - External web sources: `[WEB:https://full-url]`.\n  - ArXiv preprints: `[ARXIV:paper-id]`.\n  - DOI-identified works: `[DOI:10.0000/xyz123]`.\n  - Optional Zotero items: `[ZOTERO:item_key]` when a Zotero MCP is used.\n- Do **not** convert token citations to [@Key] in enhance workflows.\n- Do **not** remove any valid citation tokens that appear in the draft.\n\nPlacement Rules\n- Place citations immediately after the sentence they support.\n- Multiple citations are allowed, e.g.:\n  - `... [KB:doc1.md][WEB:https://...]`.\n  - `... [KB:hazard.compile.review.md][DOI:10.0000/xyz123]`.\n- Never place citation tokens inside Markdown code blocks (fenced or indented). If you need to cite evidence about code, cite in prose outside the code block.\n- Make clear, across the document, which claims are grounded in the audited KB and which in external sources.\n\nEvidence Policy (ENHANCE-SPECIFIC)\n- `<id>.compile.review.md` provides the starting point and context for enhance sessions, but it is **not** sufficient on its own as supporting evidence.\n- Paragraphs whose audit Rationales (or legacy `Verdict:` labels) indicate that the KB paragraph is **factually wrong or contradicted by evidence** MUST NOT be treated as positive evidence for any claim.\n- Paragraphs whose Rationales describe uncertainty or mixed/insufficient evidence, or whose `Confidence` is LOW, may be used only to frame uncertainty, never as sole support for a factual statement.\n- For sections whose audit Rationales state that the KB paragraph is well supported and whose `Confidence` is HIGH (including any legacy `Verdict: TRUE` labels):\n-  - You may treat the audited text as KB-style context (`[KB:...]`).\n-  - Any **new or expanded** claims beyond what was already audited as well supported with HIGH confidence MUST be backed by explicit external citations `[WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:/ZOTERO:]`.\n- For gaps (FALSE, UNCERTAIN, LOW confidence, missing or partially covered objectives):\n  - All corrective or gap-filling paragraphs MUST include external citations.\n  - When several external sources disagree, cite the most authoritative ones and reflect the remaining uncertainty in the audit footnote.\n\nCoverage Rules\n- For every major enhancement area (objective or gap), ensure that at least one external citation is present in the paragraphs that close that gap.\n- Keep `[KB:...]` tokens for:\n  - Referring back to specific reviewed sections in `<id>.compile.review.md`.\n  - Marking when you reuse or paraphrase TRUE/HIGH content.\n- Do **not** rely solely on `[KB:...]` in new paragraphs; at least one external token should appear wherever you introduce new factual material.\n\nDeduplication\n- Within a paragraph, avoid redundant repetition of the same token unless it improves clarity.\n- Across the document:\n  - Reuse the same token when referring to the same underlying source.\n  - Prefer a small number of strong sources over many weak or redundant WEB links.\n\nInteraction with Audit Rules\n- Every paragraph in `<id>.enhance.md` will receive a footnote per `rules/audit.md`.\n- When writing the audit Rationale, refer back to the same tokens you used in the paragraph body:\n-  - Example: \"Supported by `[DOI:10.0000/xyz123]` and `[WEB:https://...]`; previous KB paragraph was judged incorrect due to outdated values.\"\n- If evidence remains genuinely insufficient, explain this clearly in the Rationale and choose a cautious `Confidence` level instead of over-stating certainty.\n\nOutput Requirements\n- Enhance session outputs MUST:\n  - Keep all citations in TOKEN form only.\n  - Use `[KB:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:/ZOTERO:]` consistently according to this policy.\n  - Never treat previously FALSE-audited content as standalone evidence.\n  - Provide enough external citations that compile-enhance can later map tokens to `[@Key]` and generate a complete `biblatex.bib`.\n","numLines":62,"startLine":1,"totalLines":62}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01LckWSHBCEUiM7NHHN8zJqx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SgVbYJHj74TwXkfDmBGcpa","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/usr/local/libexec/tito/rules/audit.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":29880,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":29880,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":0,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bf7093-818d-4370-9615-a0aeec356215","uuid":"f5d2dcc9-ebc9-4071-bbbd-28b81aec1fa9"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SgVbYJHj74TwXkfDmBGcpa","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AUDIT RULES — Paragraph-level veracity verification (CORE)\n2\t\n3\tPurpose: Define the universal structure and taxonomy for audit footnotes across all workflows that require veracity checking.\n4\t\n5\t## Scope\n6\tThis rule file contains workflow-agnostic elements:\n7\t- Footnote format specification\n8\t- Hallucination taxonomy\n9\t- Confidence level definitions\n10\t- Enforcement policy (no pass-through)\n11\t\n12\tEvidence policies (target scope, fetch policy, token requirements) are workflow-specific and declared inline in each workflow file.\n13\t\n14\t## Enforcement policy\n15\t- No pass-through: Always perform the per-paragraph footnote audit; never skip based on perceived document quality.\n16\t- Audit timing: Perform audit AFTER draft assembly and formatting, as final post-processing step.\n17\t- Apply audit to every paragraph in the output document.\n18\t\n19\t**PARAGRAPH DEFINITION** (for audit purposes):\n20\tA **paragraph** is a block of text separated by blank lines in Markdown. Each blank-line-separated block requires its own audit footnote, regardless of:\n21\t- Section headers (##, ###) - sections may contain multiple paragraphs\n22\t- Conceptual grouping - each text block is audited independently\n23\t- List items - each numbered/bulleted item that forms a paragraph needs audit\n24\t\n25\tCount paragraphs by counting text blocks separated by blank lines, NOT by counting sections or concepts.\n26\t\n27\t**CODE BLOCKS (fenced or indented) are NOT paragraphs for audit purposes.**\n28\t- Treat any code block as an **opaque, atomic block**.\n29\t- You MUST NOT insert audit footnotes inside a code block.\n30\t- If a workflow requires an audit judgment about a code block (for example, to flag that code is likely incorrect), place a single audit footnote **immediately after the closing fence** (or after the indented block ends) on the next line.\n31\t- A code block may contain blank lines; these must not be treated as paragraph boundaries.\n32\t\n33\t**DISPLAY MATH BLOCKS (for example standalone `$$...$$`, `\\[...\\]`, or equation environments) are NOT paragraphs for audit purposes.**\n34\t- Treat a standalone display-math block as a non-prose block, analogous to a code block.\n35\t- Do NOT require or insert an audit footnote on the math block itself.\n36\t- Any prose paragraph that introduces, interprets, or follows the equation is still a paragraph and MUST keep its own audit footnote.\n37\t- Do NOT assume that a footnote after a display equation covers an earlier prose paragraph separated by a blank line.\n38\t\n39\t## Footnote format (mandatory, exact)\n40\tFor each paragraph, append exactly one audit footnote at the end in this exact format:\n41\t\n42\t```\n43\t^[Confidence: HIGH|MEDIUM|LOW, Rationale: explanatory note]\n44\t```\n45\t\n46\tRequirements:\n47\t- Place at paragraph end (after all sentences and inline citations).\n48\t- Do NOT place audit metadata (`Confidence:`, `Rationale:`) inline in prose; use footnotes only.\n49\t- Must include both components: `Confidence` and `Rationale`.\n50\t- Rationale must be specific and evidence-grounded. By default it should give a detailed explanation referencing concrete evidence and issues found, but individual workflows may further constrain length or style (for example, short 1–2 sentence rationales) as long as they still explain why the confidence level and rationale are appropriate.\n51\t\n52\t## Interpreting audit judgments (Rationale replaces structured Verdict)\n53\t\n54\tThis specification no longer defines a structured `Verdict` field. All judgments about whether a paragraph is supported, unsupported, or uncertain MUST be expressed in natural language inside the `Rationale`, using the workflow's own evidence policy.\n55\t\n56\tGuidance:\n57\t- When the available evidence **supports** the paragraph according to the workflow's evidence policy, the rationale should say this explicitly (for example, that the claims are well supported and consistent with the cited sources).\n58\t- When the evidence **contradicts** the paragraph or reveals clear factual errors, the rationale should state that the paragraph is likely incorrect and explain why.\n59\t- When evidence is **insufficient, ambiguous, or mixed**, the rationale should explain the uncertainty and, where relevant, which parts are supported and which are not.\n60\t\n61\tHistorical note:\n62\t- Older outputs may include a structured prefix like `Verdict: TRUE|FALSE|UNCERTAIN` inside the footnote. This text is now considered diagnostic only. New workflows MUST NOT rely on it for selection or filtering, and MUST NOT introduce a `Verdict:` field as part of the contract.\n63\t\n64\t## Confidence levels\n65\t\n66\t### HIGH\n67\t- Strong, consistent evidence from multiple authoritative sources\n68\t- Clear consensus in cited materials\n69\t- No contradictions or ambiguities detected\n70\t\n71\t### MEDIUM\n72\t- Evidence from credible sources with minor gaps or limitations\n73\t- Some inconsistencies that do not fundamentally undermine the claim\n74\t- Partial verification possible\n75\t\n76\t### LOW\n77\t- Limited evidence available\n78\t- Significant gaps or ambiguities\n79\t- Single source or questionable source reliability\n80\t- Claims at the edge of available evidence\n81\t\n82\t## Hallucination taxonomy (apply via rationale)\n83\t\n84\tWhen assessing paragraphs, check for these common hallucination types and reflect findings in the rationale (and, implicitly, in the chosen confidence level):\n85\t\n86\t1. **Unsupported quantitative claims**: Specific numbers, percentages, measurements, or statistics without citation support.\n87\t\n88\t2. **Internal contradictions**: Paragraph contradicts itself or contradicts other paragraphs in the document.\n89\t\n90\t3. **Equations inconsistent with cited sources**: Mathematical expressions, formulas, or models that do not match the notation, parameters, or structure in cited sources.\n91\t\n92\t4. **Terminology misuse**: Technical terms, definitions, or classifications used incorrectly relative to cited definitions or domain-standard meanings.\n93\t\n94\t5. **Chronological inconsistencies**: Dates, timelines, or temporal sequences that conflict with cited sources or known facts within the document.\n95\t\n96\t## Workflow-specific overrides\n97\t\n98\tEach workflow that uses this rule MUST specify inline, in its own workflow file:\n99\t- **Target scope**: What is being audited (generated output vs. input documents under review).\n100\t- **Evidence rule**: What evidence sources are acceptable for audit judgments.\n101\t- **Fetch policy**: Whether new source fetching is allowed/required during audit.\n102\t- **Token requirements**: Which citation token types are expected (`[KB:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:/ZOTERO:]`).\n103\t- **Audit semantics**: How `Confidence` and `Rationale` should be interpreted for that workflow (for example, veracity vs external sources, translation fidelity vs source text, coverage vs KB, etc.).\n104\t\n105\tFor current workflows, see in particular: `workflows/research.session.md`, `workflows/review.session.md`, `workflows/enhance.session.md`, `workflows/translate.session.md`, and `workflows/synthesis.session.md`. These specializations MUST remain consistent with the base semantics defined in this file.\n106\t\n107\tAdditional design or analysis documents may exist in `docs/`, but the only normative contract for audit footnote structure and taxonomy is this file.\n108\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bf7093-818d-4370-9615-a0aeec356215","uuid":"b2a5e25f-7a2a-4df6-b4e7-02866c8d9079","timestamp":"2026-03-30T01:27:58.174Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/usr/local/libexec/tito/rules/audit.md","content":"# AUDIT RULES — Paragraph-level veracity verification (CORE)\n\nPurpose: Define the universal structure and taxonomy for audit footnotes across all workflows that require veracity checking.\n\n## Scope\nThis rule file contains workflow-agnostic elements:\n- Footnote format specification\n- Hallucination taxonomy\n- Confidence level definitions\n- Enforcement policy (no pass-through)\n\nEvidence policies (target scope, fetch policy, token requirements) are workflow-specific and declared inline in each workflow file.\n\n## Enforcement policy\n- No pass-through: Always perform the per-paragraph footnote audit; never skip based on perceived document quality.\n- Audit timing: Perform audit AFTER draft assembly and formatting, as final post-processing step.\n- Apply audit to every paragraph in the output document.\n\n**PARAGRAPH DEFINITION** (for audit purposes):\nA **paragraph** is a block of text separated by blank lines in Markdown. Each blank-line-separated block requires its own audit footnote, regardless of:\n- Section headers (##, ###) - sections may contain multiple paragraphs\n- Conceptual grouping - each text block is audited independently\n- List items - each numbered/bulleted item that forms a paragraph needs audit\n\nCount paragraphs by counting text blocks separated by blank lines, NOT by counting sections or concepts.\n\n**CODE BLOCKS (fenced or indented) are NOT paragraphs for audit purposes.**\n- Treat any code block as an **opaque, atomic block**.\n- You MUST NOT insert audit footnotes inside a code block.\n- If a workflow requires an audit judgment about a code block (for example, to flag that code is likely incorrect), place a single audit footnote **immediately after the closing fence** (or after the indented block ends) on the next line.\n- A code block may contain blank lines; these must not be treated as paragraph boundaries.\n\n**DISPLAY MATH BLOCKS (for example standalone `$$...$$`, `\\[...\\]`, or equation environments) are NOT paragraphs for audit purposes.**\n- Treat a standalone display-math block as a non-prose block, analogous to a code block.\n- Do NOT require or insert an audit footnote on the math block itself.\n- Any prose paragraph that introduces, interprets, or follows the equation is still a paragraph and MUST keep its own audit footnote.\n- Do NOT assume that a footnote after a display equation covers an earlier prose paragraph separated by a blank line.\n\n## Footnote format (mandatory, exact)\nFor each paragraph, append exactly one audit footnote at the end in this exact format:\n\n```\n^[Confidence: HIGH|MEDIUM|LOW, Rationale: explanatory note]\n```\n\nRequirements:\n- Place at paragraph end (after all sentences and inline citations).\n- Do NOT place audit metadata (`Confidence:`, `Rationale:`) inline in prose; use footnotes only.\n- Must include both components: `Confidence` and `Rationale`.\n- Rationale must be specific and evidence-grounded. By default it should give a detailed explanation referencing concrete evidence and issues found, but individual workflows may further constrain length or style (for example, short 1–2 sentence rationales) as long as they still explain why the confidence level and rationale are appropriate.\n\n## Interpreting audit judgments (Rationale replaces structured Verdict)\n\nThis specification no longer defines a structured `Verdict` field. All judgments about whether a paragraph is supported, unsupported, or uncertain MUST be expressed in natural language inside the `Rationale`, using the workflow's own evidence policy.\n\nGuidance:\n- When the available evidence **supports** the paragraph according to the workflow's evidence policy, the rationale should say this explicitly (for example, that the claims are well supported and consistent with the cited sources).\n- When the evidence **contradicts** the paragraph or reveals clear factual errors, the rationale should state that the paragraph is likely incorrect and explain why.\n- When evidence is **insufficient, ambiguous, or mixed**, the rationale should explain the uncertainty and, where relevant, which parts are supported and which are not.\n\nHistorical note:\n- Older outputs may include a structured prefix like `Verdict: TRUE|FALSE|UNCERTAIN` inside the footnote. This text is now considered diagnostic only. New workflows MUST NOT rely on it for selection or filtering, and MUST NOT introduce a `Verdict:` field as part of the contract.\n\n## Confidence levels\n\n### HIGH\n- Strong, consistent evidence from multiple authoritative sources\n- Clear consensus in cited materials\n- No contradictions or ambiguities detected\n\n### MEDIUM\n- Evidence from credible sources with minor gaps or limitations\n- Some inconsistencies that do not fundamentally undermine the claim\n- Partial verification possible\n\n### LOW\n- Limited evidence available\n- Significant gaps or ambiguities\n- Single source or questionable source reliability\n- Claims at the edge of available evidence\n\n## Hallucination taxonomy (apply via rationale)\n\nWhen assessing paragraphs, check for these common hallucination types and reflect findings in the rationale (and, implicitly, in the chosen confidence level):\n\n1. **Unsupported quantitative claims**: Specific numbers, percentages, measurements, or statistics without citation support.\n\n2. **Internal contradictions**: Paragraph contradicts itself or contradicts other paragraphs in the document.\n\n3. **Equations inconsistent with cited sources**: Mathematical expressions, formulas, or models that do not match the notation, parameters, or structure in cited sources.\n\n4. **Terminology misuse**: Technical terms, definitions, or classifications used incorrectly relative to cited definitions or domain-standard meanings.\n\n5. **Chronological inconsistencies**: Dates, timelines, or temporal sequences that conflict with cited sources or known facts within the document.\n\n## Workflow-specific overrides\n\nEach workflow that uses this rule MUST specify inline, in its own workflow file:\n- **Target scope**: What is being audited (generated output vs. input documents under review).\n- **Evidence rule**: What evidence sources are acceptable for audit judgments.\n- **Fetch policy**: Whether new source fetching is allowed/required during audit.\n- **Token requirements**: Which citation token types are expected (`[KB:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:/ZOTERO:]`).\n- **Audit semantics**: How `Confidence` and `Rationale` should be interpreted for that workflow (for example, veracity vs external sources, translation fidelity vs source text, coverage vs KB, etc.).\n\nFor current workflows, see in particular: `workflows/research.session.md`, `workflows/review.session.md`, `workflows/enhance.session.md`, `workflows/translate.session.md`, and `workflows/synthesis.session.md`. These specializations MUST remain consistent with the base semantics defined in this file.\n\nAdditional design or analysis documents may exist in `docs/`, but the only normative contract for audit footnote structure and taxonomy is this file.\n","numLines":108,"startLine":1,"totalLines":108}}}
