{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EPhRKUbFgFNKJwUu9wMDq9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## MCER and ELastic Design Spectra (ASCE) {#sec-mcer}\n2\t\n3\tSeismic demand for all Risk Categories I–IV was derived in accordance with **ASCE/SEI 7-22**—as adopted by **IBC 2021**—using the site-specific uniform-hazard spectrum (UHS) established in the preceding chapter. Chapter 11 of ASCE 7 permits replacement of mapped spectral accelerations by values obtained from a probabilistic hazard analysis coupled with site-response modelling, provided the procedures of §11.4.7 are satisfied [@ASCE722 §11.4.7]. ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: ASCE/SEI 7-22 is the current edition of the standard and IBC 2021 does adopt ASCE 7-22 for seismic design requirements. Section 11.4.7 of ASCE 7-22 explicitly permits site-specific procedures as an alternative to mapped spectral accelerations provided the requirements of Chapter 21 are satisfied [WEB:https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s11]. The description of the UHS-based approach is accurate and the procedure is well-established. The claim is well-supported.]\n4\t\n5\tChapter §11.4.7 indicate that to perform a site response analysis and a ground motion hazard analysis to determine ground motions for any structure procedures of sections §21.1 and §21.2 must be applied. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The procedural requirement to use §21.1 (probabilistic ground motion hazard analysis) and §21.2 (deterministic ground motion analysis) when conducting site-specific analyses under §11.4.7 is consistent with the structure of ASCE 7-22 Chapter 21 [WEB:https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s21]. The paragraph contains a minor grammatical error: \"indicate\" should be \"indicates\" (subject-verb agreement with the singular antecedent \"Chapter §11.4.7\"). This error does not affect the technical accuracy of the statement. Confidence is MEDIUM rather than HIGH because the paragraph paraphrases normative language without direct quotation, and the grammatical error slightly reduces precision.]\n6\t\n7\tWhen the procedures of either Section §21.1 or §21.2 are used, the design response spectrum shall be determined in accordance with Section §21.3, the design acceleration parameters shall be determined in accordance with Section §21.4. ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: This paragraph accurately describes the procedural chain required by ASCE 7-22: site-specific ground motions are developed via §21.1 or §21.2, the design response spectrum is derived per §21.3, and design acceleration parameters $S_{DS}$ and $S_{D1}$ are determined per §21.4 [WEB:https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s21]. This sequence is a direct and accurate reflection of the normative structure of ASCE 7-22 Chapter 21.]\n8\t\n9\tThe probabilistic spectral response accelerations shall be taken as the spectral response accelerations in the direction of maximum horizontal response represented by a 5% damped acceleration response spectrum that is expected to achieve a 1% probability of collapse within a 50-year period. If the spectral response accelerations predicted by the ground motion models do not represent the maximum response in the horizontal plane, then the response spectral accelerations computed from the hazard analysis shall be scaled by factors to increase the motions to the maximum response. If the ground motion models predict the geometric mean or similar metric of the two horizontal components, then the scale factors shall be 1.2 for periods less than, or equal to, 0.2 s, 1.25 for a period of 1.0 s, and 1.3 for periods greater than or equal to 10 s. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The definition of MCER as a 5% damped spectrum targeting a 1% probability of collapse in 50 years is consistent with the risk-targeted ground motion framework introduced in ASCE 7-10 and retained in ASCE 7-22 [WEB:https://www.asce.org/publications-and-news/codes-and-standards/asce-sei-7-22]. The scale factors for converting geometric-mean (RotD50) predictions to the maximum horizontal component (RotD100) are specified in ASCE 7-22 §21.1 and are consistent with published research on the RotD100/RotD50 ratio [WEB:https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s21]. The stated values of 1.2 at $T \\leq 0.2$ s, 1.25 at $T = 1.0$ s, and 1.3 at $T \\geq 10$ s are plausible period-anchored values consistent with the ASCE 7-22 framework; however, direct normative text confirmation of the precise endpoint value of 1.3 at 10 s could not be independently verified from publicly accessible sources without access to the full standard text. The paragraph does not specify that log-log interpolation is used between anchored period values, which is a relevant omission for practitioners applying the scaling. Confidence is MEDIUM on account of this gap.]\n10\t\n11\tConsistent with §21.3, the **design-level spectral accelerations** are obtained by scaling the MCER ordinates by the factor $2/3$ [@ASCE722 Eq. 21.3-1]: ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The two-thirds scaling relationship $S_a = (2/3)\\,S_{aM}$ from MCER to design-level spectral acceleration is the standard relationship codified since ASCE 7-10 and retained in ASCE 7-22 as Equation 21.3-1 [WEB:https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s21][WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. The claim is unambiguous and fully supported.]\n12\t\n13\t$$\n14\tS_{a}=\\frac{2}{3}S_{aM}\n15\t$$\n16\t\n17\tWhen a site-specific procedure is used to determine the design ground motion the design acceleration parameters are calculated as [@ASCE722 §21.4]: ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The formulas for $S_{DS}$ and $S_{D1}$ using 0.9-scaled spectral envelope values over defined period ranges reflect provisions introduced in ASCE 7-16 §21.4 and carried forward into ASCE 7-22 [WEB:https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s21]. The $T^*$ threshold distinguishing a 2 s upper limit (for $\\bar{v}_s > 442$ m/s) from a 5 s upper limit (for $\\bar{v}_s \\leq 442$ m/s) is consistent with published descriptions of the site-specific $S_{D1}$ procedure [WEB:https://shop.iccsafe.org/media/wysiwyg/material/9011S181-Sample.pdf]. However, the display math that follows embeds the non-English word \"con\" (Spanish for \"with\") as a $\\text{con}$ label within the equation environment. This constitutes a language inconsistency in an English-language document and likely reflects adaptation from a Spanish-language source. The label should read \"with\" or \"where\" to conform to the language of the document. Confidence is MEDIUM because the 0.9 factors and $T^*$ definition cannot be fully confirmed from open-access normative sources alone.]\n18\t$$\n19\t  S_{DS} = 0.9 \\max_{T\\,\\in\\,[0.2,\\,5\\,\\text{s}]} S_a(T)\n20\t  $$ $$\n21\t  S_{D1} = \\max\\!\\left(0.9\\max_{T\\,\\in\\,[1,\\,T^*]\\,\\text{s}} \\left[T \\cdot S_a(T)\\right],\\quad\n22\t  S_a(1\\,\\text{s})\\right)\n23\t  \\quad \\text{con} \\quad\n24\t  T^* = \\begin{cases} 2\\,\\text{s} & \\bar{v}_s > 442\\,\\text{m/s} \\\\ 5\\,\\text{s} & \\bar{v}_s \\leq\n25\t  442\\,\\text{m/s} \\end{cases}\n26\t  $$\n27\tThe spectral response acceleration parameter at short periods ($S_{MS}$) and the spectral response acceleration parameter at a period of 1 s ($S_{M1}$) are calculated as: ^[Confidence: LOW, Rationale: The text correctly identifies $S_{MS}$ and $S_{M1}$ as MCER-level parameters related by back-calculation to the design-level parameters $S_{DS}$ and $S_{D1}$. The relationship $S_{MS} = 1.5\\,S_{DS}$ is correct as the algebraic inverse of $S_{DS} = (2/3)\\,S_{MS}$ [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. However, the display math that follows contains a critical error: it states $S_{M1} = 1.5\\,S_{M1}$, which is a self-referential tautology that reduces to the trivial identity $0 = 0$ and is algebraically meaningless. The correct formula is $S_{M1} = 1.5\\,S_{D1}$, which is the inverse of $S_{D1} = (2/3)\\,S_{M1}$ [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. This is a transcription error that must be corrected. Confidence is LOW on account of this error in the associated display math.]\n28\t\n29\t$$\n30\tS_{MS}= 1.5 S_{DS}, \\qquad\n31\tS_{M1}=1.5 S_{M1}\n32\t$$\n33\t\n34\t^[Confidence: LOW, Rationale: The equation $S_{M1} = 1.5\\,S_{M1}$ is a self-referential tautology and does not define $S_{M1}$. The correct ASCE 7-22 relationship is $S_{M1} = 1.5\\,S_{D1}$, consistent with the fundamental (2/3)-factor relationship between MCER and design-level parameters [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. This constitutes a transcription error in the source document that may cause incorrect calculations if followed as written.]\n35\t\n36\t\n37\t\n38\t\n39\t\n40\t\n41\tThree international frameworks govern the seismic design of tailings storage facilities (TSFs): the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management [@gistm2020], the Canadian Dam Association guidelines [@cda2021], and the Australian National Committee on Large Dams [@ancold2019]. Each framework defines performance objectives and design earthquake criteria on the basis of two factors: the consequence classification of the facility and its lifecycle phase. Consequence classification reflects the potential severity of a dam failure, assessed through population at risk (PAR), anticipated loss of life, and the magnitude of environmental and socio-economic impacts. All three standards define consequence classes ranging from Low to Extreme, with progressively more stringent seismic criteria as the consequence class increases. The classification criteria and corresponding design earthquake annual exceedance probabilities (AEPs) for each standard are tabulated in Appendix [-@sec-criteria_appendix] (Tables [-@tbl-gistm], [-@tbl-cda], and [-@tbl-ancold]). ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The existence and scope of all three referenced frameworks is confirmed. GISTM was published in August 2020 by UNEP, PRI, and ICMM and defines consequence classifications ranging from Low to Extreme with progressively stringent design criteria [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. The CDA technical bulletin on tailings dam breach analysis (2021) is a published CDA guidance document [WEB:https://cda.ca/publications/technical-documents-home/tailings-dam-breach-analysis-technical-bulletin-2021]. The ANCOLD 2019 revision to the Guidelines on Tailings Dams is a published guideline [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/]. The characterisation of the consequence classification system and its role in determining seismic design criteria is consistent with all three sources. The claim is well-supported.]\n42\t\n43\tFor a given consequence class, the applicable design earthquake also varies by lifecycle phase. Each phase of a TSF's life — construction, operation, closure, and post-closure — presents distinct engineering conditions and risk profiles. The design ground motions presented in this chapter are organized into three stages corresponding to the principal design earthquake levels: operational (OBE), closure (SEE), and post-closure (PCE). The assignment of AEPs to each stage differs between standards and is most differentiated by consequence class during operations, where AEPs range from 1/100 to 1/10,000 depending on standard and consequence class (Tables [-@tbl-gistm], [-@tbl-cda], [-@tbl-ancold]). During post-closure, this differentiation collapses: all three frameworks require convergence to the most stringent criterion — the Maximum Credible Earthquake (MCE) or the 1/10,000-year event — irrespective of the original consequence class. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The general structure of lifecycle-phased design earthquake criteria in all three frameworks is consistent with published descriptions of GISTM [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf] and ANCOLD [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The statement that all three frameworks converge to MCE or 1/10,000-year AEP at post-closure is confirmed for ANCOLD [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The specific AEP range of 1/100 to 1/10,000 during operations across all three standards could not be verified in full from publicly available excerpts; the GISTM and CDA specific AEP tables are referenced in appendices rather than reproduced in this paragraph. Confidence is MEDIUM because the post-closure convergence claim is supported but the specific operational AEP range requires direct reference to the appendix tables for complete verification.]\n44\t\n45\tThe approaches differ in structure. Under GISTM [-@gistm2020], a single earthquake design ground motion (EDGM) is specified for each consequence class and phase; the MCE must also be evaluated and governs if it produces higher demand than the probabilistic criterion. Importantly, GISTM applies the same AEPs during operations and active closure, with a step increase to a uniform 1/10,000 AEP for all consequence classes only at passive closure (Table [-@tbl-gistm]). CDA [-@cda2021] employs the Safety Evaluation Earthquake (SEE) as the design basis, with the MCE applied as an upper bound for high-consequence facilities; AEPs are progressively tightened from operations through closure, and risk-based approaches may be permitted for lower consequence classes during transitional closure phases, provided that post-closure requirements are ultimately satisfied (Table [-@tbl-cda]). ANCOLD [-@ancold2019] applies a dual-level methodology, assigning a uniform Operating Basis Earthquake (OBE) of approximately 1/500 AEP across all consequence categories during operations, with a Safety Evaluation Earthquake (SEE) that varies from 1/1,000 to 1/10,000 depending on consequence class; the most severe criteria (MCE or 1/10,000-year event, 85th percentile) are mandated for Extreme-class facilities and required for all dams prior to abandonment (Table [-@tbl-ancold]). ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The ANCOLD dual-level framework with an approximately 1/500 AEP OBE and a consequence-class-dependent SEE is confirmed by the ANCOLD 2019 guidelines addendum [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The ANCOLD requirement for Extreme-class and pre-abandonment facilities to be assessed at MCE or 1/10,000 AEP (85th percentile) is confirmed [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The specific structural descriptions of GISTM and CDA (single EDGM vs. SEE-based approaches, passive closure step increase to 1/10,000 AEP for GISTM, risk-based transition provisions for CDA) are broadly consistent with publicly available summaries [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf][WEB:https://cda.ca/publications/cda-guidance-documents]. However, specific AEP tables and thresholds for GISTM and CDA are not fully reproduced in the publicly accessible sources reviewed; verification relies on the referenced appendix tables. Confidence is MEDIUM.]\n46\t\n47\t### Construction\n48\t\n49\tTailing deposits are frequently constructed in stages, with embankments raised incrementally. Seismic stability during construction is a critical consideration, as the dam may be at partial height or incomplete strength. Even with a low probability of major earthquake occurrence during the relatively brief construction phase, the potential consequences of a moderate event on an incomplete dam can be severe. Accordingly, design requirements stipulate that temporary factors of safety under seismic loading must be satisfied at all construction stages. Initial and intermediate dam stages are analyzed for at least OBE-level events; for facilities with high consequence classifications or in high-seismicity regions, SEE criteria may also be applied. Temporary material properties and geometries are used in analyses, with design iterations as the dam evolves. Standards (e.g., CDA mining dams bulletin, 2014) recommend explicit consideration of seismic hazards at every project phase, supported by independent review at critical milestones. Construction methods and schedules are adjusted as needed to maintain seismic safety, including limiting impounded water volumes or avoiding vulnerable states during periods of heightened seismic risk. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The general principles described — phased construction analysis, OBE and SEE application during construction, use of temporary material properties, and independent review at critical milestones — are consistent with current dam engineering practice and with the ANCOLD 2019 guidelines for tailings dams [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The reference to a \"CDA mining dams bulletin, 2014\" cannot be verified against the CDA's current publication list from available sources; the CDA has published a series of technical bulletins on tailings dam topics, but the specific 2014 bulletin cited is not prominently confirmed in the public record for 2021 edition references [WEB:https://cda.ca/publications/cda-guidance-documents]. This citation warrants verification. The substantive engineering claims are reasonable and consistent with industry practice, but the unverified citation reduces confidence to MEDIUM.]\n50\t\n51\t### Operation (Active Life)\n52\t\n53\tDuring operations, when the facility contains large volumes of tailings and water, the risk profile is at its highest and the design earthquake criteria are most differentiated by consequence class. Under GISTM, operational AEPs range from 1/200 for Low-consequence facilities to 1/10,000 for Extreme (Table [-@tbl-gistm]); CDA spans 1/100 to 1/10,000 (Table [-@tbl-cda]); and ANCOLD assigns a uniform OBE at 1/500 AEP with an SEE that varies from 1/1,000 to MCE or 1/10,000 by consequence category (Table [-@tbl-ancold]). The dam must safely withstand the OBE without operational interruption and must not release tailings under the SEE. Water management is integral — adequate freeboard must be maintained to accommodate seismic settlement, wave run-up, or ground deformations. For high or extreme consequence dams, design includes provision for concurrent storm and seismic loading (e.g., maintaining a 0.1% AEP storm pond without overtopping during seismic events, in line with ANCOLD and international practice). Operational seismic readiness is maintained through regular surveillance (crack/deformation monitoring) and emergency response planning. Engineering features — such as buttresses, drainage to control pore pressures, and control of loose, saturated zones — can enhance seismic performance. If an OBE is exceeded, post-event inspection and prompt repairs are required to restore the safety margin. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The ANCOLD uniform OBE at approximately 1/500 AEP during operations and the consequence-dependent SEE range are confirmed [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The specific GISTM AEP range from 1/200 (Low) to 1/10,000 (Extreme) during operations and the CDA range of 1/100 to 1/10,000 are stated in reference to appendix tables and are plausible based on publicly available descriptions of these frameworks, but direct normative confirmation requires access to the full GISTM Annex 2 and CDA guidelines. The claim regarding concurrent storm and seismic loading at 0.1% AEP storm pond is consistent with ANCOLD practice but could not be independently confirmed from the publicly accessible ANCOLD 2019 addendum text. The general engineering principles (OBE without operational interruption, no tailings release under SEE, freeboard maintenance) are standard in dam engineering and consistent with known framework requirements. Confidence is MEDIUM due to the partially unverifiable specific AEP values and storm-seismic combination criterion.]\n54\t\n55\t### Closure and Post-Closure\n56\t\n57\tAfter the cessation of operations, TSFs are expected to remain physically stable for the indefinite future, in the absence of active management. Design for closure and post-closure is based on the principle that the probability of a rare seismic event approaches certainty over an infinite time horizon. The three frameworks handle the transition differently. Under GISTM, the same consequence-dependent AEPs that govern operations continue through active closure; only at passive closure does a uniform 1/10,000 AEP apply to all consequence classes (Table [-@tbl-gistm]). CDA progressively tightens criteria: facilities classified as High or above must already meet 1/10,000 AEP at the closure stage, while lower classes transition from their operational levels toward more conservative values; all facilities must converge to 1/10,000 AEP or MCE before abandonment (Table [-@tbl-cda]). ANCOLD mandates reassessment of all facilities under Extreme-class criteria (MCE or 1/10,000 AEP, 85th percentile) prior to abandonment (Table [-@tbl-ancold]). ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The ANCOLD requirement for all facilities to be assessed at MCE or 1/10,000 AEP (85th percentile) prior to abandonment is confirmed [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The GISTM structure — same AEPs during active closure, step to 1/10,000 at passive closure — and the CDA progressive tightening toward 1/10,000 or MCE before abandonment are consistent with publicly available descriptions [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. Full normative verification of the GISTM and CDA closure-phase AEP thresholds requires access to the complete framework documents. Confidence is MEDIUM.]\n58\t\n59\tClosure design is typically more stringent than operational design for lower consequence facilities. Rehabilitation measures (flattened slopes, reinforcements, erosion protection) may be implemented to achieve maintenance-free performance. Permanent spillways are designed for probable maximum floods, eliminating reliance on operational water management. Post-closure stability analyses must explicitly account for the degradation of temporary features. Components not expected to persist (e.g., geomembranes) are excluded from the long-term stability evaluation; the residual embankment must independently resist seismic loading. Long-term factors of safety are typically higher, and materials (cemented tailings, stabilizing berms) may be specified to enhance seismic resilience. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The general engineering principles described for post-closure design — exclusion of temporary components from long-term stability analysis, use of permanent spillways sized for probable maximum floods, and the requirement for higher long-term factors of safety — are consistent with established dam engineering practice and with the spirit of the ANCOLD 2019 and GISTM frameworks [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf][WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. The claims are plausible and broadly supported, though no specific quantitative references are provided in this paragraph. Confidence is MEDIUM because the paragraph presents general engineering principles without direct normative citations, making claim-level verification against specific standard requirements incomplete.]\n60\t\n61\t---\n62\treference-location: section\n63\tlink-citations: true                   # make in-text cites clickable\n64\t---\n65\t\n66\t\n67\tProbabilistic seismic hazard analysis (PSHA) aggregates the contributions of all plausible earthquake scenarios, obscuring the specific combinations of magnitude ($M$), source-to-site distance ($R$), and ground-motion residual ($\\varepsilon$) that control site hazard. **Hazard disaggregation** quantifies the relative contribution of each scenario class—binned by magnitude $m_k$, distance $r_j$, and residual $\\varepsilon_\\ell$—to the annual frequency of exceedance at a specified ground-motion threshold $i^*$ [@Bazzurro1999]. ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of PSHA as aggregating all scenario contributions and the description of hazard disaggregation as a procedure to identify the relative contributions of magnitude, distance, and residual bins to the annual exceedance frequency are accurate. The foundational reference [@Bazzurro1999] corresponds to Bazzurro and Cornell (1999), \"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard,\" Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, 89(2):501-520, which introduced systematic disaggregation procedures [DOI:10.1785/BSSA0890020501][WEB:https://pubs.geoscienceworld.org/ssa/bssa/article-abstract/89/2/501/342641/Disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard]. The claim is well-supported.]\n68\t\n69\tDisaggregation is performed at a target hazard level, defined by an annual exceedance probability or return period. This produces conditional probability distributions over scenario bins and identifies controlling scenario parameters responsible for site hazard [@Kramer1997]. The ground-motion exceedance probability and total annual exceedance rate are defined in the hazard analysis (see [@eq-hazard-integral], [@eq-epsilon-star]). The joint exceedance rate for scenario $(m_k, r_j, \\varepsilon_\\ell)$ from source $s$ is given in [@eq-disagg-rate], where $\\phi$ is the standard normal probability density function and $\\mathbf 1_{{\\cdot}}$ is the indicator function. Integration over $\\varepsilon$ yields the two-dimensional ($M$--$R$) formulation. Partitioning parameter space into magnitude bins $m_k$, distance bins $r_j$, and residual bins $\\varepsilon_\\ell$, the contribution from bin $(k,j,\\ell)$ is in [@eq-disagg-bin]. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The description of disaggregation as producing conditional probability distributions at a target hazard level and identifying controlling scenario parameters is accurate and consistent with Bazzurro and Cornell (1999) [DOI:10.1785/BSSA0890020501]. The reference [@Kramer1997] most likely corresponds to the widely cited textbook \"Geotechnical Earthquake Engineering\" by Steven L. Kramer; however, that textbook was published in 1996 (Prentice Hall), not 1997, suggesting a possible citation year error [WEB:https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/documents/cornell-1968]. If the citation year is incorrect, it reduces bibliographic accuracy. The mathematical framework that follows, using the standard normal PDF $\\phi$ and indicator function $\\mathbf{1}_{(\\cdot)}$ to express the joint exceedance rate, is standard in the disaggregation literature [DOI:10.1785/BSSA0890020501]. Confidence is MEDIUM due to the potentially incorrect year in the Kramer citation.]\n70\t\n71\t$$\n72\t\\Delta\\lambda_{I}(i^{*},m_k,r_j,\\varepsilon)^{(s)} =\n73\t\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\varepsilon\\ge\\varepsilon^{*}(m_k,r_j)\\}}\n74\t\\,\n75\t\\phi(\\varepsilon)\\,\n76\tf_{M,s}(m_k)\\,\n77\tf_{R|M,s}(r_j|m_k)\\,\n78\t\\nu_{s},\n79\t$${#eq-disagg-rate}\n80\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bf7093-818d-4370-9615-a0aeec356215","uuid":"fadbea4e-43d0-463a-9b4b-008951937b8a","timestamp":"2026-03-30T01:28:18.171Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/review/psha/sessions/run-20260329_214042/psha_hive2.review.md","content":"## MCER and ELastic Design Spectra (ASCE) {#sec-mcer}\n\nSeismic demand for all Risk Categories I–IV was derived in accordance with **ASCE/SEI 7-22**—as adopted by **IBC 2021**—using the site-specific uniform-hazard spectrum (UHS) established in the preceding chapter. Chapter 11 of ASCE 7 permits replacement of mapped spectral accelerations by values obtained from a probabilistic hazard analysis coupled with site-response modelling, provided the procedures of §11.4.7 are satisfied [@ASCE722 §11.4.7]. ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: ASCE/SEI 7-22 is the current edition of the standard and IBC 2021 does adopt ASCE 7-22 for seismic design requirements. Section 11.4.7 of ASCE 7-22 explicitly permits site-specific procedures as an alternative to mapped spectral accelerations provided the requirements of Chapter 21 are satisfied [WEB:https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s11]. The description of the UHS-based approach is accurate and the procedure is well-established. The claim is well-supported.]\n\nChapter §11.4.7 indicate that to perform a site response analysis and a ground motion hazard analysis to determine ground motions for any structure procedures of sections §21.1 and §21.2 must be applied. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The procedural requirement to use §21.1 (probabilistic ground motion hazard analysis) and §21.2 (deterministic ground motion analysis) when conducting site-specific analyses under §11.4.7 is consistent with the structure of ASCE 7-22 Chapter 21 [WEB:https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s21]. The paragraph contains a minor grammatical error: \"indicate\" should be \"indicates\" (subject-verb agreement with the singular antecedent \"Chapter §11.4.7\"). This error does not affect the technical accuracy of the statement. Confidence is MEDIUM rather than HIGH because the paragraph paraphrases normative language without direct quotation, and the grammatical error slightly reduces precision.]\n\nWhen the procedures of either Section §21.1 or §21.2 are used, the design response spectrum shall be determined in accordance with Section §21.3, the design acceleration parameters shall be determined in accordance with Section §21.4. ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: This paragraph accurately describes the procedural chain required by ASCE 7-22: site-specific ground motions are developed via §21.1 or §21.2, the design response spectrum is derived per §21.3, and design acceleration parameters $S_{DS}$ and $S_{D1}$ are determined per §21.4 [WEB:https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s21]. This sequence is a direct and accurate reflection of the normative structure of ASCE 7-22 Chapter 21.]\n\nThe probabilistic spectral response accelerations shall be taken as the spectral response accelerations in the direction of maximum horizontal response represented by a 5% damped acceleration response spectrum that is expected to achieve a 1% probability of collapse within a 50-year period. If the spectral response accelerations predicted by the ground motion models do not represent the maximum response in the horizontal plane, then the response spectral accelerations computed from the hazard analysis shall be scaled by factors to increase the motions to the maximum response. If the ground motion models predict the geometric mean or similar metric of the two horizontal components, then the scale factors shall be 1.2 for periods less than, or equal to, 0.2 s, 1.25 for a period of 1.0 s, and 1.3 for periods greater than or equal to 10 s. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The definition of MCER as a 5% damped spectrum targeting a 1% probability of collapse in 50 years is consistent with the risk-targeted ground motion framework introduced in ASCE 7-10 and retained in ASCE 7-22 [WEB:https://www.asce.org/publications-and-news/codes-and-standards/asce-sei-7-22]. The scale factors for converting geometric-mean (RotD50) predictions to the maximum horizontal component (RotD100) are specified in ASCE 7-22 §21.1 and are consistent with published research on the RotD100/RotD50 ratio [WEB:https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s21]. The stated values of 1.2 at $T \\leq 0.2$ s, 1.25 at $T = 1.0$ s, and 1.3 at $T \\geq 10$ s are plausible period-anchored values consistent with the ASCE 7-22 framework; however, direct normative text confirmation of the precise endpoint value of 1.3 at 10 s could not be independently verified from publicly accessible sources without access to the full standard text. The paragraph does not specify that log-log interpolation is used between anchored period values, which is a relevant omission for practitioners applying the scaling. Confidence is MEDIUM on account of this gap.]\n\nConsistent with §21.3, the **design-level spectral accelerations** are obtained by scaling the MCER ordinates by the factor $2/3$ [@ASCE722 Eq. 21.3-1]: ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The two-thirds scaling relationship $S_a = (2/3)\\,S_{aM}$ from MCER to design-level spectral acceleration is the standard relationship codified since ASCE 7-10 and retained in ASCE 7-22 as Equation 21.3-1 [WEB:https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s21][WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. The claim is unambiguous and fully supported.]\n\n$$\nS_{a}=\\frac{2}{3}S_{aM}\n$$\n\nWhen a site-specific procedure is used to determine the design ground motion the design acceleration parameters are calculated as [@ASCE722 §21.4]: ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The formulas for $S_{DS}$ and $S_{D1}$ using 0.9-scaled spectral envelope values over defined period ranges reflect provisions introduced in ASCE 7-16 §21.4 and carried forward into ASCE 7-22 [WEB:https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s21]. The $T^*$ threshold distinguishing a 2 s upper limit (for $\\bar{v}_s > 442$ m/s) from a 5 s upper limit (for $\\bar{v}_s \\leq 442$ m/s) is consistent with published descriptions of the site-specific $S_{D1}$ procedure [WEB:https://shop.iccsafe.org/media/wysiwyg/material/9011S181-Sample.pdf]. However, the display math that follows embeds the non-English word \"con\" (Spanish for \"with\") as a $\\text{con}$ label within the equation environment. This constitutes a language inconsistency in an English-language document and likely reflects adaptation from a Spanish-language source. The label should read \"with\" or \"where\" to conform to the language of the document. Confidence is MEDIUM because the 0.9 factors and $T^*$ definition cannot be fully confirmed from open-access normative sources alone.]\n$$\n  S_{DS} = 0.9 \\max_{T\\,\\in\\,[0.2,\\,5\\,\\text{s}]} S_a(T)\n  $$ $$\n  S_{D1} = \\max\\!\\left(0.9\\max_{T\\,\\in\\,[1,\\,T^*]\\,\\text{s}} \\left[T \\cdot S_a(T)\\right],\\quad\n  S_a(1\\,\\text{s})\\right)\n  \\quad \\text{con} \\quad\n  T^* = \\begin{cases} 2\\,\\text{s} & \\bar{v}_s > 442\\,\\text{m/s} \\\\ 5\\,\\text{s} & \\bar{v}_s \\leq\n  442\\,\\text{m/s} \\end{cases}\n  $$\nThe spectral response acceleration parameter at short periods ($S_{MS}$) and the spectral response acceleration parameter at a period of 1 s ($S_{M1}$) are calculated as: ^[Confidence: LOW, Rationale: The text correctly identifies $S_{MS}$ and $S_{M1}$ as MCER-level parameters related by back-calculation to the design-level parameters $S_{DS}$ and $S_{D1}$. The relationship $S_{MS} = 1.5\\,S_{DS}$ is correct as the algebraic inverse of $S_{DS} = (2/3)\\,S_{MS}$ [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. However, the display math that follows contains a critical error: it states $S_{M1} = 1.5\\,S_{M1}$, which is a self-referential tautology that reduces to the trivial identity $0 = 0$ and is algebraically meaningless. The correct formula is $S_{M1} = 1.5\\,S_{D1}$, which is the inverse of $S_{D1} = (2/3)\\,S_{M1}$ [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. This is a transcription error that must be corrected. Confidence is LOW on account of this error in the associated display math.]\n\n$$\nS_{MS}= 1.5 S_{DS}, \\qquad\nS_{M1}=1.5 S_{M1}\n$$\n\n^[Confidence: LOW, Rationale: The equation $S_{M1} = 1.5\\,S_{M1}$ is a self-referential tautology and does not define $S_{M1}$. The correct ASCE 7-22 relationship is $S_{M1} = 1.5\\,S_{D1}$, consistent with the fundamental (2/3)-factor relationship between MCER and design-level parameters [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. This constitutes a transcription error in the source document that may cause incorrect calculations if followed as written.]\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThree international frameworks govern the seismic design of tailings storage facilities (TSFs): the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management [@gistm2020], the Canadian Dam Association guidelines [@cda2021], and the Australian National Committee on Large Dams [@ancold2019]. Each framework defines performance objectives and design earthquake criteria on the basis of two factors: the consequence classification of the facility and its lifecycle phase. Consequence classification reflects the potential severity of a dam failure, assessed through population at risk (PAR), anticipated loss of life, and the magnitude of environmental and socio-economic impacts. All three standards define consequence classes ranging from Low to Extreme, with progressively more stringent seismic criteria as the consequence class increases. The classification criteria and corresponding design earthquake annual exceedance probabilities (AEPs) for each standard are tabulated in Appendix [-@sec-criteria_appendix] (Tables [-@tbl-gistm], [-@tbl-cda], and [-@tbl-ancold]). ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The existence and scope of all three referenced frameworks is confirmed. GISTM was published in August 2020 by UNEP, PRI, and ICMM and defines consequence classifications ranging from Low to Extreme with progressively stringent design criteria [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. The CDA technical bulletin on tailings dam breach analysis (2021) is a published CDA guidance document [WEB:https://cda.ca/publications/technical-documents-home/tailings-dam-breach-analysis-technical-bulletin-2021]. The ANCOLD 2019 revision to the Guidelines on Tailings Dams is a published guideline [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/]. The characterisation of the consequence classification system and its role in determining seismic design criteria is consistent with all three sources. The claim is well-supported.]\n\nFor a given consequence class, the applicable design earthquake also varies by lifecycle phase. Each phase of a TSF's life — construction, operation, closure, and post-closure — presents distinct engineering conditions and risk profiles. The design ground motions presented in this chapter are organized into three stages corresponding to the principal design earthquake levels: operational (OBE), closure (SEE), and post-closure (PCE). The assignment of AEPs to each stage differs between standards and is most differentiated by consequence class during operations, where AEPs range from 1/100 to 1/10,000 depending on standard and consequence class (Tables [-@tbl-gistm], [-@tbl-cda], [-@tbl-ancold]). During post-closure, this differentiation collapses: all three frameworks require convergence to the most stringent criterion — the Maximum Credible Earthquake (MCE) or the 1/10,000-year event — irrespective of the original consequence class. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The general structure of lifecycle-phased design earthquake criteria in all three frameworks is consistent with published descriptions of GISTM [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf] and ANCOLD [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The statement that all three frameworks converge to MCE or 1/10,000-year AEP at post-closure is confirmed for ANCOLD [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The specific AEP range of 1/100 to 1/10,000 during operations across all three standards could not be verified in full from publicly available excerpts; the GISTM and CDA specific AEP tables are referenced in appendices rather than reproduced in this paragraph. Confidence is MEDIUM because the post-closure convergence claim is supported but the specific operational AEP range requires direct reference to the appendix tables for complete verification.]\n\nThe approaches differ in structure. Under GISTM [-@gistm2020], a single earthquake design ground motion (EDGM) is specified for each consequence class and phase; the MCE must also be evaluated and governs if it produces higher demand than the probabilistic criterion. Importantly, GISTM applies the same AEPs during operations and active closure, with a step increase to a uniform 1/10,000 AEP for all consequence classes only at passive closure (Table [-@tbl-gistm]). CDA [-@cda2021] employs the Safety Evaluation Earthquake (SEE) as the design basis, with the MCE applied as an upper bound for high-consequence facilities; AEPs are progressively tightened from operations through closure, and risk-based approaches may be permitted for lower consequence classes during transitional closure phases, provided that post-closure requirements are ultimately satisfied (Table [-@tbl-cda]). ANCOLD [-@ancold2019] applies a dual-level methodology, assigning a uniform Operating Basis Earthquake (OBE) of approximately 1/500 AEP across all consequence categories during operations, with a Safety Evaluation Earthquake (SEE) that varies from 1/1,000 to 1/10,000 depending on consequence class; the most severe criteria (MCE or 1/10,000-year event, 85th percentile) are mandated for Extreme-class facilities and required for all dams prior to abandonment (Table [-@tbl-ancold]). ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The ANCOLD dual-level framework with an approximately 1/500 AEP OBE and a consequence-class-dependent SEE is confirmed by the ANCOLD 2019 guidelines addendum [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The ANCOLD requirement for Extreme-class and pre-abandonment facilities to be assessed at MCE or 1/10,000 AEP (85th percentile) is confirmed [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The specific structural descriptions of GISTM and CDA (single EDGM vs. SEE-based approaches, passive closure step increase to 1/10,000 AEP for GISTM, risk-based transition provisions for CDA) are broadly consistent with publicly available summaries [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf][WEB:https://cda.ca/publications/cda-guidance-documents]. However, specific AEP tables and thresholds for GISTM and CDA are not fully reproduced in the publicly accessible sources reviewed; verification relies on the referenced appendix tables. Confidence is MEDIUM.]\n\n### Construction\n\nTailing deposits are frequently constructed in stages, with embankments raised incrementally. Seismic stability during construction is a critical consideration, as the dam may be at partial height or incomplete strength. Even with a low probability of major earthquake occurrence during the relatively brief construction phase, the potential consequences of a moderate event on an incomplete dam can be severe. Accordingly, design requirements stipulate that temporary factors of safety under seismic loading must be satisfied at all construction stages. Initial and intermediate dam stages are analyzed for at least OBE-level events; for facilities with high consequence classifications or in high-seismicity regions, SEE criteria may also be applied. Temporary material properties and geometries are used in analyses, with design iterations as the dam evolves. Standards (e.g., CDA mining dams bulletin, 2014) recommend explicit consideration of seismic hazards at every project phase, supported by independent review at critical milestones. Construction methods and schedules are adjusted as needed to maintain seismic safety, including limiting impounded water volumes or avoiding vulnerable states during periods of heightened seismic risk. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The general principles described — phased construction analysis, OBE and SEE application during construction, use of temporary material properties, and independent review at critical milestones — are consistent with current dam engineering practice and with the ANCOLD 2019 guidelines for tailings dams [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The reference to a \"CDA mining dams bulletin, 2014\" cannot be verified against the CDA's current publication list from available sources; the CDA has published a series of technical bulletins on tailings dam topics, but the specific 2014 bulletin cited is not prominently confirmed in the public record for 2021 edition references [WEB:https://cda.ca/publications/cda-guidance-documents]. This citation warrants verification. The substantive engineering claims are reasonable and consistent with industry practice, but the unverified citation reduces confidence to MEDIUM.]\n\n### Operation (Active Life)\n\nDuring operations, when the facility contains large volumes of tailings and water, the risk profile is at its highest and the design earthquake criteria are most differentiated by consequence class. Under GISTM, operational AEPs range from 1/200 for Low-consequence facilities to 1/10,000 for Extreme (Table [-@tbl-gistm]); CDA spans 1/100 to 1/10,000 (Table [-@tbl-cda]); and ANCOLD assigns a uniform OBE at 1/500 AEP with an SEE that varies from 1/1,000 to MCE or 1/10,000 by consequence category (Table [-@tbl-ancold]). The dam must safely withstand the OBE without operational interruption and must not release tailings under the SEE. Water management is integral — adequate freeboard must be maintained to accommodate seismic settlement, wave run-up, or ground deformations. For high or extreme consequence dams, design includes provision for concurrent storm and seismic loading (e.g., maintaining a 0.1% AEP storm pond without overtopping during seismic events, in line with ANCOLD and international practice). Operational seismic readiness is maintained through regular surveillance (crack/deformation monitoring) and emergency response planning. Engineering features — such as buttresses, drainage to control pore pressures, and control of loose, saturated zones — can enhance seismic performance. If an OBE is exceeded, post-event inspection and prompt repairs are required to restore the safety margin. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The ANCOLD uniform OBE at approximately 1/500 AEP during operations and the consequence-dependent SEE range are confirmed [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The specific GISTM AEP range from 1/200 (Low) to 1/10,000 (Extreme) during operations and the CDA range of 1/100 to 1/10,000 are stated in reference to appendix tables and are plausible based on publicly available descriptions of these frameworks, but direct normative confirmation requires access to the full GISTM Annex 2 and CDA guidelines. The claim regarding concurrent storm and seismic loading at 0.1% AEP storm pond is consistent with ANCOLD practice but could not be independently confirmed from the publicly accessible ANCOLD 2019 addendum text. The general engineering principles (OBE without operational interruption, no tailings release under SEE, freeboard maintenance) are standard in dam engineering and consistent with known framework requirements. Confidence is MEDIUM due to the partially unverifiable specific AEP values and storm-seismic combination criterion.]\n\n### Closure and Post-Closure\n\nAfter the cessation of operations, TSFs are expected to remain physically stable for the indefinite future, in the absence of active management. Design for closure and post-closure is based on the principle that the probability of a rare seismic event approaches certainty over an infinite time horizon. The three frameworks handle the transition differently. Under GISTM, the same consequence-dependent AEPs that govern operations continue through active closure; only at passive closure does a uniform 1/10,000 AEP apply to all consequence classes (Table [-@tbl-gistm]). CDA progressively tightens criteria: facilities classified as High or above must already meet 1/10,000 AEP at the closure stage, while lower classes transition from their operational levels toward more conservative values; all facilities must converge to 1/10,000 AEP or MCE before abandonment (Table [-@tbl-cda]). ANCOLD mandates reassessment of all facilities under Extreme-class criteria (MCE or 1/10,000 AEP, 85th percentile) prior to abandonment (Table [-@tbl-ancold]). ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The ANCOLD requirement for all facilities to be assessed at MCE or 1/10,000 AEP (85th percentile) prior to abandonment is confirmed [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf]. The GISTM structure — same AEPs during active closure, step to 1/10,000 at passive closure — and the CDA progressive tightening toward 1/10,000 or MCE before abandonment are consistent with publicly available descriptions [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. Full normative verification of the GISTM and CDA closure-phase AEP thresholds requires access to the complete framework documents. Confidence is MEDIUM.]\n\nClosure design is typically more stringent than operational design for lower consequence facilities. Rehabilitation measures (flattened slopes, reinforcements, erosion protection) may be implemented to achieve maintenance-free performance. Permanent spillways are designed for probable maximum floods, eliminating reliance on operational water management. Post-closure stability analyses must explicitly account for the degradation of temporary features. Components not expected to persist (e.g., geomembranes) are excluded from the long-term stability evaluation; the residual embankment must independently resist seismic loading. Long-term factors of safety are typically higher, and materials (cemented tailings, stabilizing berms) may be specified to enhance seismic resilience. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The general engineering principles described for post-closure design — exclusion of temporary components from long-term stability analysis, use of permanent spillways sized for probable maximum floods, and the requirement for higher long-term factors of safety — are consistent with established dam engineering practice and with the spirit of the ANCOLD 2019 and GISTM frameworks [WEB:https://www.ancold.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Tailings-Guideline-Addendum-July-2019_v2.pdf][WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. The claims are plausible and broadly supported, though no specific quantitative references are provided in this paragraph. Confidence is MEDIUM because the paragraph presents general engineering principles without direct normative citations, making claim-level verification against specific standard requirements incomplete.]\n\n---\nreference-location: section\nlink-citations: true                   # make in-text cites clickable\n---\n\n\nProbabilistic seismic hazard analysis (PSHA) aggregates the contributions of all plausible earthquake scenarios, obscuring the specific combinations of magnitude ($M$), source-to-site distance ($R$), and ground-motion residual ($\\varepsilon$) that control site hazard. **Hazard disaggregation** quantifies the relative contribution of each scenario class—binned by magnitude $m_k$, distance $r_j$, and residual $\\varepsilon_\\ell$—to the annual frequency of exceedance at a specified ground-motion threshold $i^*$ [@Bazzurro1999]. ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of PSHA as aggregating all scenario contributions and the description of hazard disaggregation as a procedure to identify the relative contributions of magnitude, distance, and residual bins to the annual exceedance frequency are accurate. The foundational reference [@Bazzurro1999] corresponds to Bazzurro and Cornell (1999), \"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard,\" Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, 89(2):501-520, which introduced systematic disaggregation procedures [DOI:10.1785/BSSA0890020501][WEB:https://pubs.geoscienceworld.org/ssa/bssa/article-abstract/89/2/501/342641/Disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard]. The claim is well-supported.]\n\nDisaggregation is performed at a target hazard level, defined by an annual exceedance probability or return period. This produces conditional probability distributions over scenario bins and identifies controlling scenario parameters responsible for site hazard [@Kramer1997]. The ground-motion exceedance probability and total annual exceedance rate are defined in the hazard analysis (see [@eq-hazard-integral], [@eq-epsilon-star]). The joint exceedance rate for scenario $(m_k, r_j, \\varepsilon_\\ell)$ from source $s$ is given in [@eq-disagg-rate], where $\\phi$ is the standard normal probability density function and $\\mathbf 1_{{\\cdot}}$ is the indicator function. Integration over $\\varepsilon$ yields the two-dimensional ($M$--$R$) formulation. Partitioning parameter space into magnitude bins $m_k$, distance bins $r_j$, and residual bins $\\varepsilon_\\ell$, the contribution from bin $(k,j,\\ell)$ is in [@eq-disagg-bin]. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The description of disaggregation as producing conditional probability distributions at a target hazard level and identifying controlling scenario parameters is accurate and consistent with Bazzurro and Cornell (1999) [DOI:10.1785/BSSA0890020501]. The reference [@Kramer1997] most likely corresponds to the widely cited textbook \"Geotechnical Earthquake Engineering\" by Steven L. Kramer; however, that textbook was published in 1996 (Prentice Hall), not 1997, suggesting a possible citation year error [WEB:https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/documents/cornell-1968]. If the citation year is incorrect, it reduces bibliographic accuracy. The mathematical framework that follows, using the standard normal PDF $\\phi$ and indicator function $\\mathbf{1}_{(\\cdot)}$ to express the joint exceedance rate, is standard in the disaggregation literature [DOI:10.1785/BSSA0890020501]. Confidence is MEDIUM due to the potentially incorrect year in the Kramer citation.]\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\lambda_{I}(i^{*},m_k,r_j,\\varepsilon)^{(s)} =\n\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\varepsilon\\ge\\varepsilon^{*}(m_k,r_j)\\}}\n\\,\n\\phi(\\varepsilon)\\,\nf_{M,s}(m_k)\\,\nf_{R|M,s}(r_j|m_k)\\,\n\\nu_{s},\n$${#eq-disagg-rate}\n","numLines":80,"startLine":1,"totalLines":224}}}
