{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EjKxDvVYgregzV5KBQ7Bhv","type":"tool_result","content":"158\t---\n159\t\n160\t\n161\tNear-surface geology modifies seismic waves as they propagate from bedrock to the ground surface. Soft soils amplify ground motion at intermediate-to-long periods while potentially deamplifying short-period motion through nonlinear strain effects; stiff rock sites transmit near-bedrock intensity levels with minimal modification [@Baker2021]. In probabilistic seismic hazard analysis, site effects can be incorporated through two mechanisms: (1) the site term embedded within ground-motion prediction equations (GMPEs), in which the median prediction and aleatory variability are functions of $V_{S30}$, or (2) an external amplification model applied to a reference-rock hazard curve [@Stewart2020]. The first mechanism is ergodic by construction—the empirical site term averages over many recording sites globally and may not capture conditions at a specific site or region. The second mechanism allows regionally calibrated amplification that accounts for nonlinear soil behavior as a function of shaking intensity. This assessment employs both approaches and selects the controlling (maximum) spectral ordinate for each period and hazard level to ensure that the design spectrum is not governed by whichever model happens to be less conservative at a given period.\n162\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The description of site amplification mechanisms -- GMPE-embedded $V_{S30}$-dependent site terms (ergodic) versus external amplification models applied to reference-rock hazard curves -- is consistent with established PSHA practice [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/webpeer-2014-16-jonathan_p._stewart_kioumars_afshari_and_youssef_m.a._hashash.pdf]. The Baker (2021) and Stewart (2020) references are appropriate citations for site effects in PSHA. The characterisation of GMPE site terms as ergodic (averaging over many sites globally) and therefore potentially not capturing site-specific or regional conditions is a well-documented limitation [WEB:https://www.daveboore.com/pubs_online/stewart_etal_ergodic_amps_cena.eqspectra_published.pdf]. The envelope approach (selecting the maximum spectral ordinate from both methods) is a conservative design choice consistent with standard practice.]\n163\t\n164\tSite effects were introduced in the probabilistic seismic hazard analysis through two complementary models. The first used an ergodic ground-motion framework in which a logic tree of $V_{S30}$-dependent GMPEs was evaluated for shear-wave velocities ranging from 200 m/s to 1100 m/s. For every branch, OpenQuake computed spectral accelerations $S_a(T_n, V_{S30})$ at the required service levels. Epistemic uncertainty was represented by the branch weights prescribed in the logic tree, and aleatory variability followed the total standard deviation of each GMPE. Because the site term is embedded empirically within the GMPEs, the resulting uniform-hazard spectra remain ergodic.\n165\t^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The use of OpenQuake Engine for logic-tree PSHA with $V_{S30}$-dependent GMPEs is well established and consistent with standard practice [WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf]. The stated $V_{S30}$ range (200 to 1100 m/s) is a plausible range for engineering applications. The description of epistemic uncertainty via logic-tree branch weights and aleatory variability via GMPE sigma is the canonical two-uncertainty framework in PSHA. The conclusion that GMPE-based spectra remain ergodic because the site term is empirically embedded is technically correct [WEB:https://www.daveboore.com/pubs_online/stewart_etal_ergodic_amps_cena.eqspectra_published.pdf]. The specific GMPEs used in the logic tree are not identified in this paragraph, preventing full assessment of their suitability for the site region.]\n166\t\n167\tThe second model is an amplification model based on a reference-rock hazard curve at $V_{\\mathrm{ref}} = 760\\,\\text{m/s}$ and transforms it to the target condition $V_{S30}$ by applying period-dependent, nonlinear amplification factors $F(T_n, V_{S30})$, according to [@eq-site-af]. The amplification model follows the formulation of Stewart and co-authors, which expresses $F$ as a log-normal random variable whose mean $\\eta_{F\\mid\\mathrm{PGA}}\\!\\left(T_n, V_{S30}\\right)$ and dispersion $\\sigma_{\\ln F}$ depend on oscillator period, shear-wave velocity, and input PGA [@Stewart2020; @Hashash2020]. For $V_{S30} < V_{\\mathrm{ref}}$, the model captures nonlinear deamplification at short periods and amplification at intermediate-to-long periods; for stiffer sites, the amplification asymptotically approaches unity. Formally, the amplification factor is modeled as a lognormal random variable [@eq-site-lnf]:\n168\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The use of $V_{\\mathrm{ref}} = 760\\,\\text{m/s}$ as the reference rock condition is standard and consistent with the NGA-West2 framework and the Stewart et al. amplification model [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/webpeer-2014-16-jonathan_p._stewart_kioumars_afshari_and_youssef_m.a._hashash.pdf][WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193]. The description of the amplification factor as a lognormal random variable with period-, $V_{S30}$-, and PGA-dependent mean and dispersion follows the formulation in Stewart (2020) and Hashash (2020), confirmed by published documentation of that model [WEB:https://escholarship.org/content/qt3037q1t6/qt3037q1t6.pdf]. The physical behaviour (deamplification at short periods for soft sites, amplification at intermediate-to-long periods, asymptotic approach to unity for stiff sites) is consistent with empirical site amplification data.]\n169\t\n170\t$$\n171\t\\ln F \\sim \\mathcal{N}\\!\\left(\\mu_{\\ln F},\\; \\sigma_{\\ln F}^2\\right)\n172\t$${#eq-site-lnf}\n173\t\n174\twhere $\\mu_{\\ln F}$ is the conditional mean of the log-amplification factor and $\\sigma_{\\ln F}$ is the total aleatory standard deviation, both for a given ($V_{S30}$, $T_n$, $pga^*$). The mean $\\mu_{\\ln F}$ decomposes additively into three period-dependent terms [@Stewart2020]: a reference-condition adjustment $F_{760}$ that bridges the difference between the 760 m/s and 3000 m/s reference horizons, a linear site term $F_V$ that scales with $V_{S30}$ relative to the reference velocity, and a nonlinear factor $F_{nl}$ that captures PGA-dependent soil deamplification at high strain levels. Similarly, the total dispersion decomposes in quadrature [@eq-site-sigma-decomp]:\n175\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The additive decomposition of $\\mu_{\\ln F}$ into three terms ($F_{760}$, $F_V$, $F_{nl}$) -- bridging the 760 m/s to 3000 m/s reference, linear $V_{S30}$ scaling, and nonlinear PGA-dependent deamplification -- is the functional form documented in Stewart (2020) and consistent with the published PEER report describing the model [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/webpeer-2014-16-jonathan_p._stewart_kioumars_afshari_and_youssef_m.a._hashash.pdf][WEB:https://escholarship.org/content/qt3037q1t6/qt3037q1t6.pdf]. The quadrature decomposition of dispersion (three components summed in quadrature) is a standard approach for combining independent variance components. No errors are identified.]\n176\t\n177\t$$\n178\t\\sigma_{\\ln F}^2 = \\sigma_{L}^2 + \\sigma_{I}^2 + \\sigma_{NL}^2\n179\t$${#eq-site-sigma-decomp}\n180\t\n181\twhere $\\sigma_L$ is the linear site-term dispersion (dependent on $V_{S30}$ and period), $\\sigma_I$ is the inter-reference dispersion (nonzero only when $V_{\\mathrm{ref}} = 3000\\,\\text{m/s}$), and $\\sigma_{NL}$ is the nonlinear dispersion (dependent on $V_{S30}$ and $pga^*$). All components are period-dependent and tabulated in the published model [@Stewart2020]. The detailed functional forms and calibration of each component are documented in [@sec-site_model].\n182\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The identification of three variance components ($\\sigma_L^2$, $\\sigma_I^2$, $\\sigma_{NL}^2$) and their physical interpretations -- linear site-term dispersion, inter-reference dispersion (nonzero only for the 3000 m/s reference), and nonlinear dispersion -- are consistent with the published Stewart (2020) model documentation [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/webpeer-2014-16-jonathan_p._stewart_kioumars_afshari_and_youssef_m.a._hashash.pdf][WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193]. The statement that all components are period-dependent and tabulated in the model is accurate for the Stewart (2020) formulation.]\n183\t\n184\t$$\n185\tS_a\\!\\left(T_n, V_{S30}\\right) = F\\!\\left(T_n, V_{S30}\\right)\\,S_a\\!\\left(T_n, V_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\right).\n186\t$${#eq-site-af}\n187\t\n188\tFor each return period $T_R$, oscillator period $T_n$, fractile $p$, and site class $V_{S30}$, the design spectral ordinate is defined as the maximum across the GMPE-based and amplification-based estimates [@eq-site-envelope]:\n189\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The envelope approach for defining the design spectral ordinate as the maximum of the GMPE-based and amplification-based estimates across all return periods, oscillator periods, and $V_{S30}$ values is a technically sound and conservative methodology. This approach is consistent with standard engineering practice for site-specific PSHA [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/webpeer-2014-16-jonathan_p._stewart_kioumars_afshari_and_youssef_m.a._hashash.pdf]. The reference to fractile $p$ in the definition of the design spectral ordinate indicates awareness of the probabilistic nature of the amplification factor, which is appropriate.]\n190\t\n191\t$$\n192\tS_a^{\\mathrm{max}}(T_n, V_{S30}) = \\max\\!\\left\\{S_a^{\\mathrm{GMPE}}(T_n, V_{S30}),\\; S_a^{F}(T_n, V_{S30})\\right\\}\n193\t$${#eq-site-envelope}\n194\t\n195\twhere $S_a^{\\mathrm{GMPE}}$ is the spectral ordinate obtained from the GMPE logic tree evaluated directly at the target $V_{S30}$, and $S_a^{F}$ is the spectral ordinate obtained by applying the amplification factor $F$ to the reference-rock hazard curve per [@eq-site-af]. This envelope ensures that the controlling (most hazardous) estimate governs the design spectrum at every period. GMPEs may underpredict at certain periods or $V_{S30}$ ranges where their empirical site terms are poorly constrained by the underlying strong-motion database, while the amplification model may underpredict where the reference-rock hazard curve does not fully represent near-field or source-specific effects. The envelope eliminates the risk of unconservative design by selecting the larger ordinate at each spectral period.\n196\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The rationale for the envelope approach -- that GMPEs may underpredict where empirical site terms are poorly constrained, while amplification models may underpredict where reference-rock hazard does not capture near-field effects -- is technically sound and well-motivated [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/webpeer-2014-16-jonathan_p._stewart_kioumars_afshari_and_youssef_m.a._hashash.pdf][WEB:https://escholarship.org/content/qt3037q1t6/qt3037q1t6.pdf]. The conclusion that taking the maximum eliminates the risk of unconservative design at any given spectral period is correct by construction. No technical errors are identified.]\n197\t## Site Classification {#sec-vs30_appendix}\n198\t\n199\tThe **Vs30** parameter is widely measured in the field using methods such as borehole logging, seismic cone penetration tests, and surface wave techniques. Introduced into engineering practice in the early 1990s, it provides a simple, robust metric to characterize site conditions for earthquake ground motions. **Vs30** is defined as the time-averaged shear-wave velocity in the upper 30 meters of a site profile. In practical terms, it represents the overall stiffness of the near-surface soils and rock. Mathematically, Vs30 is computed as the harmonic average of shear-wave velocity ($V_{S}$) over the top 30 m:\n200\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The definition of $V_{S30}$ as the time-averaged (harmonic-mean) shear-wave velocity over the upper 30 m, its introduction in the early 1990s, and its measurement via borehole logging, seismic CPT, and surface-wave techniques are all well-established facts confirmed by extensive literature [WEB:http://masw.com/files/NEHRP.pdf][WEB:https://www.usgs.gov/publications/mapping-nehrp-vs30-site-classes]. The characterisation of $V_{S30}$ as a measure of near-surface stiffness and its use as the primary site parameter in modern GMPEs is broadly supported.]\n201\t\n202\t$$\n203\tV_{S30} = \\frac{30\\,\\text{m}}{\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\frac{H_i}{V_{S,i}}}\n204\t$$\n205\t\n206\twhere $H_i$ and $V_{S,i}$ are the thickness and shear-wave velocity of the $i$-th layer in the upper 30 m. This formula gives the equivalent uniform velocity that yields the same travel time through 30 m of material as the actual layered profile.\n207\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The harmonic-mean formula $V_{S30} = 30\\,\\text{m} / \\sum_i (H_i / V_{S,i})$ is the standard definition of $V_{S30}$ and is consistent with the NEHRP provisions, ASCE 7-22, and all major building codes that adopt this parameter [WEB:http://masw.com/files/NEHRP.pdf][WEB:https://www.daveboore.com/pubs_online/vs30_from_shallow_models.pdf]. The physical interpretation as the equivalent uniform velocity yielding the same travel time is mathematically correct.]\n208\t\n209\tVs30 has become the primary quantitative predictor of site effects in modern ground-motion models. Virtually all recent ground motion prediction equations (GMPEs) incorporate Vs30 as a key input parameter to adjust predicted shaking for site conditions. Empirical GMPEs (e.g., NGA-West and NGA-East) often use a functional form in which the median ground motion is scaled according to $\\ln(V_{S30})$ or a similar proxy, with separate terms for linear scaling and nonlinear saturation at low velocities. Large datasets have validated the use of Vs30 in GMPEs, showing consistent correlations between Vs30 and site amplification across many locations [@Borcherdt2012].\n210\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The role of $V_{S30}$ as the primary site predictor in modern GMPEs, the $\\ln(V_{S30})$ functional form used in NGA-West and NGA-East models, and the separation of linear scaling from nonlinear saturation terms are well documented [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/webpeer-2014-16-jonathan_p._stewart_kioumars_afshari_and_youssef_m.a._hashash.pdf][WEB:https://escholarship.org/content/qt3037q1t6/qt3037q1t6.pdf]. The Borcherdt (2012) citation for validation of $V_{S30}$ correlations with site amplification is plausible -- R.D. Borcherdt has published extensively on site amplification and $V_{S30}$-based site classification -- though the specific 2012 publication was not retrieved from publicly available sources to confirm the exact citation.]\n211\t\n212\tIn parallel, site-specific analyses (such as 1D site response simulations) rely on Vs30 as an initial classification of the site profile, although they incorporate the full shear-wave velocity profile with depth. While Vs30 does not capture all facets of site response—such as basin depth or resonance effects—it serves as a practical first-order proxy.\n213\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The statement that site-specific 1D analyses use $V_{S30}$ for initial classification but rely on the full velocity profile, and the acknowledged limitations of $V_{S30}$ (inability to capture basin depth, resonance effects) while retaining utility as a first-order proxy, are well supported in the site-response literature [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/webpeer-2014-16-jonathan_p._stewart_kioumars_afshari_and_youssef_m.a._hashash.pdf][WEB:https://www.daveboore.com/pubs_online/vs30_from_shallow_models.pdf]. No errors are identified.]\n214\t\n215\t### NEHRP Site Classes\n216\t\n217\tThe NEHRP provisions [-@BSSC2015] classify sites into categories (Site Class A through E, and special Class F) based on Vs30 ranges. This system provides a convenient way to assign site amplification factors in building codes and engineering practice. The standard classes and their Vs30 criteria are approximately:\n218\t^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The NEHRP A-through-F site classification system is well established and confirmed by USGS and FEMA documentation [WEB:http://masw.com/files/NEHRP.pdf][WEB:https://www.fema.gov/sites/default/files/documents/fema_p-2192-nehrp-provisions-seismic-design-maps-2024-irc-ibc.pdf]. However, the citation references BSSC 2015 (the 2015 NEHRP Recommended Seismic Provisions, FEMA P-1050-1), while the most recent edition is the 2020 NEHRP Recommended Seismic Provisions (FEMA P-2082), which introduced an expanded site classification system with additional sub-classes. Using the 2015 edition is not incorrect (the A-F framework predates both editions), but a reference to the 2020 provisions would be more current [WEB:https://www.fema.gov/sites/default/files/documents/fema_p-2192-nehrp-provisions-seismic-design-maps-2024-irc-ibc.pdf].]\n219\t\n220\t* **Site Class A:** Hard rock, $V_{S30}>1500$ m/s (very high velocity material).\n221\t* **Site Class B:** Rock, $V_{S30}\\approx$ 760–1500 m/s (typical competent rock; 760 m/s is the B/C boundary).\n222\t* **Site Class C:** Very dense soil or soft rock, $V_{S30}\\approx$ 360–760 m/s.\n223\t* **Site Class D:** Stiff soil, $V_{S30}\\approx$ 180–360 m/s.\n224\t* **Site Class E:** Soft soil, $V_{S30}<$ 180 m/s (includes clay-rich soils prone to high amplification).\n225\t* **Site Class F:** Soils requiring site-specific evaluation (e.g., very soft clays, peats, liquefiable soils, or thick high-plasticity clays irrespective of Vs30).\n226\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The $V_{S30}$ thresholds for NEHRP site classes A through F -- Class A $>$1500 m/s, B 760-1500 m/s, C 360-760 m/s, D 180-360 m/s, E $<$180 m/s, and F defined by qualitative conditions rather than a $V_{S30}$ value -- are consistent with the long-established NEHRP classification system and confirmed by multiple published sources [WEB:http://masw.com/files/NEHRP.pdf][WEB:https://www.usgs.gov/publications/mapping-nehrp-vs30-site-classes]. The description of Class F by qualitative subsoil conditions (softness, thickness, liquefiability) rather than a $V_{S30}$ cutoff is accurate. The 760 m/s B/C boundary value is the standard reference-rock condition used across building codes and GMPE development.]\n227\t\n228\tClasses A–E are defined by the Vs30 thresholds above. Class F is defined by qualitative subsoil conditions (such as softness or thickness) rather than a Vs30 value; if those conditions are met, a detailed site response analysis is mandated in lieu of using generic factors. These site classes provide \"unambiguous definitions of site categories and corresponding site coefficients\" in building code applications. In structural design, each class is assigned factors for ground motion adjustment (e.g. $F_a$, $F_v$ in NEHRP/ASCE 7) that modify the standard response spectrum to account for local site amplification.\n229\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The description of Class F as requiring site-specific analysis, the role of Classes A-E in providing tabulated amplification factors ($F_a$, $F_v$), and the mandate for detailed response analysis when Class F conditions are met are all consistent with NEHRP and ASCE 7-22 provisions [WEB:http://masw.com/files/NEHRP.pdf][WEB:https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s11]. The quoted phrase \"unambiguous definitions of site categories and corresponding site coefficients\" appears to be drawn from building code commentary; its attribution is not independently verified, but the substance is accurate.]\n230\t\n231\t### AS1726 Guidelines\n232\t\n233\tThe Australian Standard AS 1726-2017 [-@AS1726-2017] provides guidelines for conducting geotechnical investigations and includes a site classification system that categorises sites into classes based on their soil properties, such as shear wave velocity parameter $\\bar v_S$, depth to bedrock, and soil type. The Australian Standard AS 1170.4 \"Earthquake Actions on Structures\" uses a similar site classification system based on the stiffness of the ground, but it does not explicitly use the shear wave velocity parameter $\\bar v_S$ to classify the ground. The categories range from A (rock) to E (soft soil), with a special category (S) for sites requiring site-specific investigations.\n234\t^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: AS 1726-2017 is a legitimate Australian Standard for geotechnical site investigations, and AS 1170.4 is the Australian Standard for earthquake actions on structures; both are referenced in the context of site classification for seismic applications. The described A-through-E-plus-S classification structure, and the distinction that AS 1170.4 does not explicitly use $\\bar v_S$ while AS 1726-2017 does, are plausible but could not be fully verified from publicly available sources, as both standards are behind a paywall. The claim that AS 1170.4 relies on stiffness without explicit $\\bar v_S$ is consistent with the historical development of Australian seismic provisions, which predate widespread $V_{S30}$ adoption [WEB:https://www.earthquakes.bgs.ac.uk/hazard/haz_guide/psha.html].]\n235\t\n236\t{{< include /psha/_tbl/AS1726.qmd >}}\n237\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bf7093-818d-4370-9615-a0aeec356215","uuid":"50444ee7-264c-4791-8c96-d1990e42d2a2","timestamp":"2026-03-30T01:28:34.369Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/review/psha/sessions/run-20260329_195849/psha_hive.review.md","content":"---\n\n\nNear-surface geology modifies seismic waves as they propagate from bedrock to the ground surface. Soft soils amplify ground motion at intermediate-to-long periods while potentially deamplifying short-period motion through nonlinear strain effects; stiff rock sites transmit near-bedrock intensity levels with minimal modification [@Baker2021]. In probabilistic seismic hazard analysis, site effects can be incorporated through two mechanisms: (1) the site term embedded within ground-motion prediction equations (GMPEs), in which the median prediction and aleatory variability are functions of $V_{S30}$, or (2) an external amplification model applied to a reference-rock hazard curve [@Stewart2020]. The first mechanism is ergodic by construction—the empirical site term averages over many recording sites globally and may not capture conditions at a specific site or region. The second mechanism allows regionally calibrated amplification that accounts for nonlinear soil behavior as a function of shaking intensity. This assessment employs both approaches and selects the controlling (maximum) spectral ordinate for each period and hazard level to ensure that the design spectrum is not governed by whichever model happens to be less conservative at a given period.\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The description of site amplification mechanisms -- GMPE-embedded $V_{S30}$-dependent site terms (ergodic) versus external amplification models applied to reference-rock hazard curves -- is consistent with established PSHA practice [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/webpeer-2014-16-jonathan_p._stewart_kioumars_afshari_and_youssef_m.a._hashash.pdf]. The Baker (2021) and Stewart (2020) references are appropriate citations for site effects in PSHA. The characterisation of GMPE site terms as ergodic (averaging over many sites globally) and therefore potentially not capturing site-specific or regional conditions is a well-documented limitation [WEB:https://www.daveboore.com/pubs_online/stewart_etal_ergodic_amps_cena.eqspectra_published.pdf]. The envelope approach (selecting the maximum spectral ordinate from both methods) is a conservative design choice consistent with standard practice.]\n\nSite effects were introduced in the probabilistic seismic hazard analysis through two complementary models. The first used an ergodic ground-motion framework in which a logic tree of $V_{S30}$-dependent GMPEs was evaluated for shear-wave velocities ranging from 200 m/s to 1100 m/s. For every branch, OpenQuake computed spectral accelerations $S_a(T_n, V_{S30})$ at the required service levels. Epistemic uncertainty was represented by the branch weights prescribed in the logic tree, and aleatory variability followed the total standard deviation of each GMPE. Because the site term is embedded empirically within the GMPEs, the resulting uniform-hazard spectra remain ergodic.\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The use of OpenQuake Engine for logic-tree PSHA with $V_{S30}$-dependent GMPEs is well established and consistent with standard practice [WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf]. The stated $V_{S30}$ range (200 to 1100 m/s) is a plausible range for engineering applications. The description of epistemic uncertainty via logic-tree branch weights and aleatory variability via GMPE sigma is the canonical two-uncertainty framework in PSHA. The conclusion that GMPE-based spectra remain ergodic because the site term is empirically embedded is technically correct [WEB:https://www.daveboore.com/pubs_online/stewart_etal_ergodic_amps_cena.eqspectra_published.pdf]. The specific GMPEs used in the logic tree are not identified in this paragraph, preventing full assessment of their suitability for the site region.]\n\nThe second model is an amplification model based on a reference-rock hazard curve at $V_{\\mathrm{ref}} = 760\\,\\text{m/s}$ and transforms it to the target condition $V_{S30}$ by applying period-dependent, nonlinear amplification factors $F(T_n, V_{S30})$, according to [@eq-site-af]. The amplification model follows the formulation of Stewart and co-authors, which expresses $F$ as a log-normal random variable whose mean $\\eta_{F\\mid\\mathrm{PGA}}\\!\\left(T_n, V_{S30}\\right)$ and dispersion $\\sigma_{\\ln F}$ depend on oscillator period, shear-wave velocity, and input PGA [@Stewart2020; @Hashash2020]. For $V_{S30} < V_{\\mathrm{ref}}$, the model captures nonlinear deamplification at short periods and amplification at intermediate-to-long periods; for stiffer sites, the amplification asymptotically approaches unity. Formally, the amplification factor is modeled as a lognormal random variable [@eq-site-lnf]:\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The use of $V_{\\mathrm{ref}} = 760\\,\\text{m/s}$ as the reference rock condition is standard and consistent with the NGA-West2 framework and the Stewart et al. amplification model [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/webpeer-2014-16-jonathan_p._stewart_kioumars_afshari_and_youssef_m.a._hashash.pdf][WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193]. The description of the amplification factor as a lognormal random variable with period-, $V_{S30}$-, and PGA-dependent mean and dispersion follows the formulation in Stewart (2020) and Hashash (2020), confirmed by published documentation of that model [WEB:https://escholarship.org/content/qt3037q1t6/qt3037q1t6.pdf]. The physical behaviour (deamplification at short periods for soft sites, amplification at intermediate-to-long periods, asymptotic approach to unity for stiff sites) is consistent with empirical site amplification data.]\n\n$$\n\\ln F \\sim \\mathcal{N}\\!\\left(\\mu_{\\ln F},\\; \\sigma_{\\ln F}^2\\right)\n$${#eq-site-lnf}\n\nwhere $\\mu_{\\ln F}$ is the conditional mean of the log-amplification factor and $\\sigma_{\\ln F}$ is the total aleatory standard deviation, both for a given ($V_{S30}$, $T_n$, $pga^*$). The mean $\\mu_{\\ln F}$ decomposes additively into three period-dependent terms [@Stewart2020]: a reference-condition adjustment $F_{760}$ that bridges the difference between the 760 m/s and 3000 m/s reference horizons, a linear site term $F_V$ that scales with $V_{S30}$ relative to the reference velocity, and a nonlinear factor $F_{nl}$ that captures PGA-dependent soil deamplification at high strain levels. Similarly, the total dispersion decomposes in quadrature [@eq-site-sigma-decomp]:\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The additive decomposition of $\\mu_{\\ln F}$ into three terms ($F_{760}$, $F_V$, $F_{nl}$) -- bridging the 760 m/s to 3000 m/s reference, linear $V_{S30}$ scaling, and nonlinear PGA-dependent deamplification -- is the functional form documented in Stewart (2020) and consistent with the published PEER report describing the model [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/webpeer-2014-16-jonathan_p._stewart_kioumars_afshari_and_youssef_m.a._hashash.pdf][WEB:https://escholarship.org/content/qt3037q1t6/qt3037q1t6.pdf]. The quadrature decomposition of dispersion (three components summed in quadrature) is a standard approach for combining independent variance components. No errors are identified.]\n\n$$\n\\sigma_{\\ln F}^2 = \\sigma_{L}^2 + \\sigma_{I}^2 + \\sigma_{NL}^2\n$${#eq-site-sigma-decomp}\n\nwhere $\\sigma_L$ is the linear site-term dispersion (dependent on $V_{S30}$ and period), $\\sigma_I$ is the inter-reference dispersion (nonzero only when $V_{\\mathrm{ref}} = 3000\\,\\text{m/s}$), and $\\sigma_{NL}$ is the nonlinear dispersion (dependent on $V_{S30}$ and $pga^*$). All components are period-dependent and tabulated in the published model [@Stewart2020]. The detailed functional forms and calibration of each component are documented in [@sec-site_model].\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The identification of three variance components ($\\sigma_L^2$, $\\sigma_I^2$, $\\sigma_{NL}^2$) and their physical interpretations -- linear site-term dispersion, inter-reference dispersion (nonzero only for the 3000 m/s reference), and nonlinear dispersion -- are consistent with the published Stewart (2020) model documentation [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/webpeer-2014-16-jonathan_p._stewart_kioumars_afshari_and_youssef_m.a._hashash.pdf][WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193]. The statement that all components are period-dependent and tabulated in the model is accurate for the Stewart (2020) formulation.]\n\n$$\nS_a\\!\\left(T_n, V_{S30}\\right) = F\\!\\left(T_n, V_{S30}\\right)\\,S_a\\!\\left(T_n, V_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\right).\n$${#eq-site-af}\n\nFor each return period $T_R$, oscillator period $T_n$, fractile $p$, and site class $V_{S30}$, the design spectral ordinate is defined as the maximum across the GMPE-based and amplification-based estimates [@eq-site-envelope]:\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The envelope approach for defining the design spectral ordinate as the maximum of the GMPE-based and amplification-based estimates across all return periods, oscillator periods, and $V_{S30}$ values is a technically sound and conservative methodology. This approach is consistent with standard engineering practice for site-specific PSHA [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/webpeer-2014-16-jonathan_p._stewart_kioumars_afshari_and_youssef_m.a._hashash.pdf]. The reference to fractile $p$ in the definition of the design spectral ordinate indicates awareness of the probabilistic nature of the amplification factor, which is appropriate.]\n\n$$\nS_a^{\\mathrm{max}}(T_n, V_{S30}) = \\max\\!\\left\\{S_a^{\\mathrm{GMPE}}(T_n, V_{S30}),\\; S_a^{F}(T_n, V_{S30})\\right\\}\n$${#eq-site-envelope}\n\nwhere $S_a^{\\mathrm{GMPE}}$ is the spectral ordinate obtained from the GMPE logic tree evaluated directly at the target $V_{S30}$, and $S_a^{F}$ is the spectral ordinate obtained by applying the amplification factor $F$ to the reference-rock hazard curve per [@eq-site-af]. This envelope ensures that the controlling (most hazardous) estimate governs the design spectrum at every period. GMPEs may underpredict at certain periods or $V_{S30}$ ranges where their empirical site terms are poorly constrained by the underlying strong-motion database, while the amplification model may underpredict where the reference-rock hazard curve does not fully represent near-field or source-specific effects. The envelope eliminates the risk of unconservative design by selecting the larger ordinate at each spectral period.\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The rationale for the envelope approach -- that GMPEs may underpredict where empirical site terms are poorly constrained, while amplification models may underpredict where reference-rock hazard does not capture near-field effects -- is technically sound and well-motivated [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/webpeer-2014-16-jonathan_p._stewart_kioumars_afshari_and_youssef_m.a._hashash.pdf][WEB:https://escholarship.org/content/qt3037q1t6/qt3037q1t6.pdf]. The conclusion that taking the maximum eliminates the risk of unconservative design at any given spectral period is correct by construction. No technical errors are identified.]\n## Site Classification {#sec-vs30_appendix}\n\nThe **Vs30** parameter is widely measured in the field using methods such as borehole logging, seismic cone penetration tests, and surface wave techniques. Introduced into engineering practice in the early 1990s, it provides a simple, robust metric to characterize site conditions for earthquake ground motions. **Vs30** is defined as the time-averaged shear-wave velocity in the upper 30 meters of a site profile. In practical terms, it represents the overall stiffness of the near-surface soils and rock. Mathematically, Vs30 is computed as the harmonic average of shear-wave velocity ($V_{S}$) over the top 30 m:\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The definition of $V_{S30}$ as the time-averaged (harmonic-mean) shear-wave velocity over the upper 30 m, its introduction in the early 1990s, and its measurement via borehole logging, seismic CPT, and surface-wave techniques are all well-established facts confirmed by extensive literature [WEB:http://masw.com/files/NEHRP.pdf][WEB:https://www.usgs.gov/publications/mapping-nehrp-vs30-site-classes]. The characterisation of $V_{S30}$ as a measure of near-surface stiffness and its use as the primary site parameter in modern GMPEs is broadly supported.]\n\n$$\nV_{S30} = \\frac{30\\,\\text{m}}{\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\frac{H_i}{V_{S,i}}}\n$$\n\nwhere $H_i$ and $V_{S,i}$ are the thickness and shear-wave velocity of the $i$-th layer in the upper 30 m. This formula gives the equivalent uniform velocity that yields the same travel time through 30 m of material as the actual layered profile.\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The harmonic-mean formula $V_{S30} = 30\\,\\text{m} / \\sum_i (H_i / V_{S,i})$ is the standard definition of $V_{S30}$ and is consistent with the NEHRP provisions, ASCE 7-22, and all major building codes that adopt this parameter [WEB:http://masw.com/files/NEHRP.pdf][WEB:https://www.daveboore.com/pubs_online/vs30_from_shallow_models.pdf]. The physical interpretation as the equivalent uniform velocity yielding the same travel time is mathematically correct.]\n\nVs30 has become the primary quantitative predictor of site effects in modern ground-motion models. Virtually all recent ground motion prediction equations (GMPEs) incorporate Vs30 as a key input parameter to adjust predicted shaking for site conditions. Empirical GMPEs (e.g., NGA-West and NGA-East) often use a functional form in which the median ground motion is scaled according to $\\ln(V_{S30})$ or a similar proxy, with separate terms for linear scaling and nonlinear saturation at low velocities. Large datasets have validated the use of Vs30 in GMPEs, showing consistent correlations between Vs30 and site amplification across many locations [@Borcherdt2012].\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The role of $V_{S30}$ as the primary site predictor in modern GMPEs, the $\\ln(V_{S30})$ functional form used in NGA-West and NGA-East models, and the separation of linear scaling from nonlinear saturation terms are well documented [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/webpeer-2014-16-jonathan_p._stewart_kioumars_afshari_and_youssef_m.a._hashash.pdf][WEB:https://escholarship.org/content/qt3037q1t6/qt3037q1t6.pdf]. The Borcherdt (2012) citation for validation of $V_{S30}$ correlations with site amplification is plausible -- R.D. Borcherdt has published extensively on site amplification and $V_{S30}$-based site classification -- though the specific 2012 publication was not retrieved from publicly available sources to confirm the exact citation.]\n\nIn parallel, site-specific analyses (such as 1D site response simulations) rely on Vs30 as an initial classification of the site profile, although they incorporate the full shear-wave velocity profile with depth. While Vs30 does not capture all facets of site response—such as basin depth or resonance effects—it serves as a practical first-order proxy.\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The statement that site-specific 1D analyses use $V_{S30}$ for initial classification but rely on the full velocity profile, and the acknowledged limitations of $V_{S30}$ (inability to capture basin depth, resonance effects) while retaining utility as a first-order proxy, are well supported in the site-response literature [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/webpeer-2014-16-jonathan_p._stewart_kioumars_afshari_and_youssef_m.a._hashash.pdf][WEB:https://www.daveboore.com/pubs_online/vs30_from_shallow_models.pdf]. No errors are identified.]\n\n### NEHRP Site Classes\n\nThe NEHRP provisions [-@BSSC2015] classify sites into categories (Site Class A through E, and special Class F) based on Vs30 ranges. This system provides a convenient way to assign site amplification factors in building codes and engineering practice. The standard classes and their Vs30 criteria are approximately:\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The NEHRP A-through-F site classification system is well established and confirmed by USGS and FEMA documentation [WEB:http://masw.com/files/NEHRP.pdf][WEB:https://www.fema.gov/sites/default/files/documents/fema_p-2192-nehrp-provisions-seismic-design-maps-2024-irc-ibc.pdf]. However, the citation references BSSC 2015 (the 2015 NEHRP Recommended Seismic Provisions, FEMA P-1050-1), while the most recent edition is the 2020 NEHRP Recommended Seismic Provisions (FEMA P-2082), which introduced an expanded site classification system with additional sub-classes. Using the 2015 edition is not incorrect (the A-F framework predates both editions), but a reference to the 2020 provisions would be more current [WEB:https://www.fema.gov/sites/default/files/documents/fema_p-2192-nehrp-provisions-seismic-design-maps-2024-irc-ibc.pdf].]\n\n* **Site Class A:** Hard rock, $V_{S30}>1500$ m/s (very high velocity material).\n* **Site Class B:** Rock, $V_{S30}\\approx$ 760–1500 m/s (typical competent rock; 760 m/s is the B/C boundary).\n* **Site Class C:** Very dense soil or soft rock, $V_{S30}\\approx$ 360–760 m/s.\n* **Site Class D:** Stiff soil, $V_{S30}\\approx$ 180–360 m/s.\n* **Site Class E:** Soft soil, $V_{S30}<$ 180 m/s (includes clay-rich soils prone to high amplification).\n* **Site Class F:** Soils requiring site-specific evaluation (e.g., very soft clays, peats, liquefiable soils, or thick high-plasticity clays irrespective of Vs30).\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The $V_{S30}$ thresholds for NEHRP site classes A through F -- Class A $>$1500 m/s, B 760-1500 m/s, C 360-760 m/s, D 180-360 m/s, E $<$180 m/s, and F defined by qualitative conditions rather than a $V_{S30}$ value -- are consistent with the long-established NEHRP classification system and confirmed by multiple published sources [WEB:http://masw.com/files/NEHRP.pdf][WEB:https://www.usgs.gov/publications/mapping-nehrp-vs30-site-classes]. The description of Class F by qualitative subsoil conditions (softness, thickness, liquefiability) rather than a $V_{S30}$ cutoff is accurate. The 760 m/s B/C boundary value is the standard reference-rock condition used across building codes and GMPE development.]\n\nClasses A–E are defined by the Vs30 thresholds above. Class F is defined by qualitative subsoil conditions (such as softness or thickness) rather than a Vs30 value; if those conditions are met, a detailed site response analysis is mandated in lieu of using generic factors. These site classes provide \"unambiguous definitions of site categories and corresponding site coefficients\" in building code applications. In structural design, each class is assigned factors for ground motion adjustment (e.g. $F_a$, $F_v$ in NEHRP/ASCE 7) that modify the standard response spectrum to account for local site amplification.\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The description of Class F as requiring site-specific analysis, the role of Classes A-E in providing tabulated amplification factors ($F_a$, $F_v$), and the mandate for detailed response analysis when Class F conditions are met are all consistent with NEHRP and ASCE 7-22 provisions [WEB:http://masw.com/files/NEHRP.pdf][WEB:https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s11]. The quoted phrase \"unambiguous definitions of site categories and corresponding site coefficients\" appears to be drawn from building code commentary; its attribution is not independently verified, but the substance is accurate.]\n\n### AS1726 Guidelines\n\nThe Australian Standard AS 1726-2017 [-@AS1726-2017] provides guidelines for conducting geotechnical investigations and includes a site classification system that categorises sites into classes based on their soil properties, such as shear wave velocity parameter $\\bar v_S$, depth to bedrock, and soil type. The Australian Standard AS 1170.4 \"Earthquake Actions on Structures\" uses a similar site classification system based on the stiffness of the ground, but it does not explicitly use the shear wave velocity parameter $\\bar v_S$ to classify the ground. The categories range from A (rock) to E (soft soil), with a special category (S) for sites requiring site-specific investigations.\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: AS 1726-2017 is a legitimate Australian Standard for geotechnical site investigations, and AS 1170.4 is the Australian Standard for earthquake actions on structures; both are referenced in the context of site classification for seismic applications. The described A-through-E-plus-S classification structure, and the distinction that AS 1170.4 does not explicitly use $\\bar v_S$ while AS 1726-2017 does, are plausible but could not be fully verified from publicly available sources, as both standards are behind a paywall. The claim that AS 1170.4 relies on stiffness without explicit $\\bar v_S$ is consistent with the historical development of Australian seismic provisions, which predate widespread $V_{S30}$ adoption [WEB:https://www.earthquakes.bgs.ac.uk/hazard/haz_guide/psha.html].]\n\n{{< include /psha/_tbl/AS1726.qmd >}}\n","numLines":80,"startLine":158,"totalLines":251}}}
