{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019wnbF4YsPWC9DkRDa4uqXZ","type":"tool_result","content":"158\t\n159\twhere $\\mu_{\\ln F}$ is the conditional mean of the log-amplification factor and $\\sigma_{\\ln F}$ is the total aleatory standard deviation, both for a given ($V_{S30}$, $T_n$, $pga^*$). The mean $\\mu_{\\ln F}$ decomposes additively into three period-dependent terms [@Stewart2020]: a reference-condition adjustment $F_{760}$ that bridges the difference between the 760 m/s and 3000 m/s reference horizons, a linear site term $F_V$ that scales with $V_{S30}$ relative to the reference velocity, and a nonlinear factor $F_{nl}$ that captures PGA-dependent soil deamplification at high strain levels. Similarly, the total dispersion decomposes in quadrature [@eq-site-sigma-decomp]: ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The three-component additive decomposition of $\\mu_{\\ln F}$ into $F_{760}$, $F_V$, and $F_{nl}$ is consistent with the Hashash et al. (2020) model for central and eastern North America, which explicitly defines these three components [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193]. The $F_{760}$ component bridges the 760 m/s and 3000 m/s reference horizons; the $F_V$ component provides linear $V_{S30}$ scaling; and $F_{nl}$ captures nonlinear PGA-dependent deamplification, all consistent with the published model [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193]. The description of total dispersion decomposing in quadrature is consistent with standard uncertainty propagation for independent dispersion components. The claim is well-supported.]\n160\t\n161\t$$\n162\t\\sigma_{\\ln F}^2 = \\sigma_{L}^2 + \\sigma_{I}^2 + \\sigma_{NL}^2\n163\t$${#eq-site-sigma-decomp}\n164\t\n165\twhere $\\sigma_L$ is the linear site-term dispersion (dependent on $V_{S30}$ and period), $\\sigma_I$ is the inter-reference dispersion (nonzero only when $V_{\\mathrm{ref}} = 3000\\,\\text{m/s}$), and $\\sigma_{NL}$ is the nonlinear dispersion (dependent on $V_{S30}$ and $pga^*$). All components are period-dependent and tabulated in the published model [@Stewart2020]. The detailed functional forms and calibration of each component are documented in [@sec-site_model]. ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The identification of $\\sigma_L$ (linear $V_{S30}$-dependent dispersion), $\\sigma_I$ (inter-reference dispersion, nonzero only when $V_{\\mathrm{ref}} = 3000$ m/s), and $\\sigma_{NL}$ (nonlinear, $V_{S30}$- and PGA-dependent dispersion) is consistent with the structure of the Hashash et al. (2020) model [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193]. The statement that $\\sigma_I$ is nonzero only when $V_{\\mathrm{ref}} = 3000$ m/s (the NGA-East hard-rock reference condition) correctly reflects the model's construction, where the inter-reference component accounts for uncertainty in the adjustment from the 3000 m/s to the 760 m/s reference [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193]. The claim is well-supported.]\n166\t\n167\t$$\n168\tS_a\\!\\left(T_n, V_{S30}\\right) = F\\!\\left(T_n, V_{S30}\\right)\\,S_a\\!\\left(T_n, V_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\right).\n169\t$${#eq-site-af}\n170\t\n171\tFor each return period $T_R$, oscillator period $T_n$, fractile $p$, and site class $V_{S30}$, the design spectral ordinate is defined as the maximum across the GMPE-based and amplification-based estimates [@eq-site-envelope]: ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The envelope approach — selecting the maximum spectral ordinate from the two model approaches at each period and hazard level — is a recognized and conservative method for design spectrum construction that protects against underestimation by either model individually. The notation and formulation are internally consistent with the preceding sections. The claim is well-supported.]\n172\t\n173\t$$\n174\tS_a^{\\mathrm{max}}(T_n, V_{S30}) = \\max\\!\\left\\{S_a^{\\mathrm{GMPE}}(T_n, V_{S30}),\\; S_a^{F}(T_n, V_{S30})\\right\\}\n175\t$${#eq-site-envelope}\n176\t\n177\twhere $S_a^{\\mathrm{GMPE}}$ is the spectral ordinate obtained from the GMPE logic tree evaluated directly at the target $V_{S30}$, and $S_a^{F}$ is the spectral ordinate obtained by applying the amplification factor $F$ to the reference-rock hazard curve per [@eq-site-af]. This envelope ensures that the controlling (most hazardous) estimate governs the design spectrum at every period. GMPEs may underpredict at certain periods or $V_{S30}$ ranges where their empirical site terms are poorly constrained by the underlying strong-motion database, while the amplification model may underpredict where the reference-rock hazard curve does not fully represent near-field or source-specific effects. The envelope eliminates the risk of unconservative design by selecting the larger ordinate at each spectral period. ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The rationale for the envelope — that GMPEs may underpredict at poorly constrained $V_{S30}$ ranges while amplification models may underpredict for near-field or source-specific effects — is technically sound and reflects known limitations of both approaches [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193][WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf]. The selection of the maximum ordinate at each period as the governing design value is a standard conservative design choice. The claim is well-supported and technically accurate.]\n178\t## Site Classification {#sec-vs30_appendix}\n179\t\n180\tThe **Vs30** parameter is widely measured in the field using methods such as borehole logging, seismic cone penetration tests, and surface wave techniques. Introduced into engineering practice in the early 1990s, it provides a simple, robust metric to characterize site conditions for earthquake ground motions. **Vs30** is defined as the time-averaged shear-wave velocity in the upper 30 meters of a site profile. In practical terms, it represents the overall stiffness of the near-surface soils and rock. Mathematically, Vs30 is computed as the harmonic average of shear-wave velocity ($V_{S}$) over the top 30 m: ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The definition of $V_{S30}$ as the time-averaged (harmonic mean) shear-wave velocity in the upper 30 m, its introduction in the early 1990s, and its widespread use in GMPE site characterisation are all confirmed by multiple authoritative sources including the USGS and NEHRP provisions [WEB:https://www.usgs.gov/publications/mapping-nehrp-vs30-site-classes]. The field measurement methods listed (borehole logging, seismic cone penetration test, surface wave techniques) are standard methods for determining shear-wave velocity profiles. The claim is well-supported.]\n181\t\n182\t$$\n183\tV_{S30} = \\frac{30\\,\\text{m}}{\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\frac{H_i}{V_{S,i}}}\n184\t$$\n185\t\n186\twhere $H_i$ and $V_{S,i}$ are the thickness and shear-wave velocity of the $i$-th layer in the upper 30 m. This formula gives the equivalent uniform velocity that yields the same travel time through 30 m of material as the actual layered profile. ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The formula $V_{S30} = 30\\,\\text{m} / \\sum_{i=1}^{N}(H_i / V_{S,i})$ is the standard harmonic-mean (travel-time-weighted) definition of $V_{S30}$ as specified in ASCE 7 and NEHRP provisions [WEB:https://www.usgs.gov/publications/mapping-nehrp-vs30-site-classes][WEB:https://www.daveboore.com/pubs_online/vs30_from_shallow_models.pdf]. The interpretation as the equivalent uniform velocity yielding the same travel time through 30 m is mathematically correct. The claim is fully supported.]\n187\t\n188\tVs30 has become the primary quantitative predictor of site effects in modern ground-motion models. Virtually all recent ground motion prediction equations (GMPEs) incorporate Vs30 as a key input parameter to adjust predicted shaking for site conditions. Empirical GMPEs (e.g., NGA-West and NGA-East) often use a functional form in which the median ground motion is scaled according to $\\ln(V_{S30})$ or a similar proxy, with separate terms for linear scaling and nonlinear saturation at low velocities. Large datasets have validated the use of Vs30 in GMPEs, showing consistent correlations between Vs30 and site amplification across many locations [@Borcherdt2012]. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The use of $V_{S30}$ as the primary site parameter in NGA-West2 and NGA-East GMPEs, with $\\ln(V_{S30})$-based functional forms and separate nonlinear terms, is well-established and confirmed by published GMPE documentation [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193][WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf]. The reference [@Borcherdt2012] likely refers to Borcherdt (2012) on site coefficients and site classification, a recognized authority on the topic [WEB:https://pubs.usgs.gov/publication/70022904]. However, the specific year (2012) and full citation details for this Borcherdt reference could not be independently confirmed from the available sources; published Borcherdt work on site classification is from several periods including 1994 and later. Confidence is MEDIUM due to the partially unverified citation year for the Borcherdt reference.]\n189\t\n190\tIn parallel, site-specific analyses (such as 1D site response simulations) rely on Vs30 as an initial classification of the site profile, although they incorporate the full shear-wave velocity profile with depth. While Vs30 does not capture all facets of site response—such as basin depth or resonance effects—it serves as a practical first-order proxy. ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The statement that site-specific 1D site response simulations use the full shear-wave velocity profile rather than relying solely on $V_{S30}$, and that $V_{S30}$ does not capture basin depth or resonance effects, is accurate and consistent with the established literature on site response analysis [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193][WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf]. The description of $V_{S30}$ as a practical first-order proxy is standard and well-supported. The claim is accurate.]\n191\t\n192\t### NEHRP Site Classes\n193\t\n194\tThe NEHRP provisions [-@BSSC2015] classify sites into categories (Site Class A through E, and special Class F) based on Vs30 ranges. This system provides a convenient way to assign site amplification factors in building codes and engineering practice. The standard classes and their Vs30 criteria are approximately: ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The NEHRP site classification system (Site Classes A through F) based on $V_{S30}$ thresholds is defined in the NEHRP provisions and adopted by ASCE 7 and IBC. The reference [@BSSC2015] corresponds to the 2015 NEHRP Recommended Seismic Provisions for New Buildings and Other Structures (FEMA P-1050), which documents this classification system [WEB:https://www.nehrp.gov/pdf/H-18-8.pdf]. The description is accurate and the claim is well-supported.]\n195\t\n196\t* **Site Class A:** Hard rock, $V_{S30}>1500$ m/s (very high velocity material).\n197\t* **Site Class B:** Rock, $V_{S30}\\approx$ 760–1500 m/s (typical competent rock; 760 m/s is the B/C boundary).\n198\t* **Site Class C:** Very dense soil or soft rock, $V_{S30}\\approx$ 360–760 m/s.\n199\t* **Site Class D:** Stiff soil, $V_{S30}\\approx$ 180–360 m/s.\n200\t* **Site Class E:** Soft soil, $V_{S30}<$ 180 m/s (includes clay-rich soils prone to high amplification).\n201\t* **Site Class F:** Soils requiring site-specific evaluation (e.g., very soft clays, peats, liquefiable soils, or thick high-plasticity clays irrespective of Vs30).\n202\t\n203\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The $V_{S30}$ thresholds for NEHRP Site Classes A through F — A: $>1500$ m/s, B: 760-1500 m/s, C: 360-760 m/s, D: 180-360 m/s, E: $<180$ m/s, F: special conditions — are consistent with NEHRP provisions and are confirmed by multiple USGS and ASCE 7 sources [WEB:https://www.usgs.gov/publications/mapping-nehrp-vs30-site-classes][WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. The identification of 760 m/s as the B/C boundary is accurate. The description of Site Class F as defined by qualitative subsoil conditions rather than a $V_{S30}$ threshold is also correct. The claim is well-supported.]\n204\t\n205\tClasses A–E are defined by the Vs30 thresholds above. Class F is defined by qualitative subsoil conditions (such as softness or thickness) rather than a Vs30 value; if those conditions are met, a detailed site response analysis is mandated in lieu of using generic factors. These site classes provide \"unambiguous definitions of site categories and corresponding site coefficients\" in building code applications. In structural design, each class is assigned factors for ground motion adjustment (e.g. $F_a$, $F_v$ in NEHRP/ASCE 7) that modify the standard response spectrum to account for local site amplification. ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The description of Site Class F as requiring site-specific analysis and the use of $F_a$ and $F_v$ factors in NEHRP/ASCE 7 for Classes A-E to adjust the standard response spectrum are standard provisions confirmed by NEHRP and ASCE 7 documentation [WEB:https://www.nehrp.gov/pdf/H-18-8.pdf][WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. The quoted phrase \"unambiguous definitions of site categories and corresponding site coefficients\" is consistent with the purpose of the NEHRP site classification system. The claim is accurate and well-supported.]\n206\t\n207\t### AS1726 Guidelines\n208\t\n209\tThe Australian Standard AS 1726-2017 [-@AS1726-2017] provides guidelines for conducting geotechnical investigations and includes a site classification system that categorises sites into classes based on their soil properties, such as shear wave velocity parameter $\\bar v_S$, depth to bedrock, and soil type. The Australian Standard AS 1170.4 \"Earthquake Actions on Structures\" uses a similar site classification system based on the stiffness of the ground, but it does not explicitly use the shear wave velocity parameter $\\bar v_S$ to classify the ground. The categories range from A (rock) to E (soft soil), with a special category (S) for sites requiring site-specific investigations. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: AS 1726-2017 is a current Australian standard for geotechnical site investigations and includes guidance on site classification. AS 1170.4 is the Australian standard for earthquake actions. The description of the A-to-E plus special category (S) classification hierarchy and the role of shear wave velocity in AS 1726-2017 but not explicitly in AS 1170.4 is plausible and consistent with the known structure of Australian standards. However, independent external confirmation of the specific AS 1170.4 classification details described here (particularly that it does not explicitly use $\\bar v_S$) could not be established from the publicly available sources reviewed. Confidence is MEDIUM due to the limited publicly accessible external verification of the specific AS 1170.4 provisions.]\n210\t\n211\t{{< include /psha/_tbl/AS1726.qmd >}}\n212\t\n213\t### ASCE/SEI 7-22 Guidelines\n214\t\n215\tThe ASCE/SEI 7-22 Standard [-@ASCE722] provides guidelines for conducting geotechnical investigations and includes a site classification system that categorises sites into classes based on their soil properties, such as shear wave velocity, depth to bedrock, and soil type. According to ASCE 7-22 [@ASCE722], the site soil shall be classified based on the average shear wave velocity parameter, $\\bar v_S$, which is derived from the measured shear wave velocity profile from the ground surface to a depth of 30 m. ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: ASCE 7-22 Chapter 20 specifies site classification based on $\\bar v_S$ (average shear-wave velocity in the upper 30 m, equivalent to $V_{S30}$), along with average standard penetration test blow count and undrained shear strength. The description of the classification being based on soil properties including shear wave velocity, depth to bedrock, and soil type is accurate [WEB:https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s11][WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. The claim is well-supported.]\n216\t\n217\t{{< include /psha/_tbl/ASCE722.qmd >}}\n218\t\n219\t### NBC-2020 Guidelines\n220\t\n221\tThe National Building Code of Canada [-@NBC2020] provides guidelines for site characterization and seismic site response. Similar to ASCE 7-22, it uses an average shear wave velocity in the upper 30 m (commonly denoted $v_{S30}$), standard penetration test (SPT) blow count, and/or undrained shear strength ($s_{u}$) to classify sites into categories A through F. Where site-specific investigations are warranted (e.g., liquefiable soils, highly sensitive clays, peat), the code requires more detailed analysis. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The National Building Code of Canada (NBC) 2020 includes seismic site classification provisions that use $v_{S30}$, SPT blow count, and undrained shear strength $s_u$ for site categories A through F, consistent with the description provided. The classification structure is aligned with the NEHRP/ASCE 7 framework [WEB:https://www.usgs.gov/publications/mapping-nehrp-vs30-site-classes]. The requirement for more detailed analysis for special site conditions (liquefiable soils, sensitive clays, peat) is standard across major building codes. Confidence is MEDIUM because independent external confirmation of the specific NBC 2020 site classification provisions from publicly accessible sources was limited; the description is consistent with the known NBC 2015/2020 framework but the specific 2020 edition details require access to the normative document.]\n222\t\n223\t{{< include /psha/_tbl/NBC2020.qmd >}}\n224\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bf7093-818d-4370-9615-a0aeec356215","uuid":"022a7a65-bbeb-41a5-aa31-0f42bae8d217","timestamp":"2026-03-30T01:28:34.881Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/review/psha/sessions/run-20260329_214042/psha_hive2.review.md","content":"\nwhere $\\mu_{\\ln F}$ is the conditional mean of the log-amplification factor and $\\sigma_{\\ln F}$ is the total aleatory standard deviation, both for a given ($V_{S30}$, $T_n$, $pga^*$). The mean $\\mu_{\\ln F}$ decomposes additively into three period-dependent terms [@Stewart2020]: a reference-condition adjustment $F_{760}$ that bridges the difference between the 760 m/s and 3000 m/s reference horizons, a linear site term $F_V$ that scales with $V_{S30}$ relative to the reference velocity, and a nonlinear factor $F_{nl}$ that captures PGA-dependent soil deamplification at high strain levels. Similarly, the total dispersion decomposes in quadrature [@eq-site-sigma-decomp]: ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The three-component additive decomposition of $\\mu_{\\ln F}$ into $F_{760}$, $F_V$, and $F_{nl}$ is consistent with the Hashash et al. (2020) model for central and eastern North America, which explicitly defines these three components [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193]. The $F_{760}$ component bridges the 760 m/s and 3000 m/s reference horizons; the $F_V$ component provides linear $V_{S30}$ scaling; and $F_{nl}$ captures nonlinear PGA-dependent deamplification, all consistent with the published model [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193]. The description of total dispersion decomposing in quadrature is consistent with standard uncertainty propagation for independent dispersion components. The claim is well-supported.]\n\n$$\n\\sigma_{\\ln F}^2 = \\sigma_{L}^2 + \\sigma_{I}^2 + \\sigma_{NL}^2\n$${#eq-site-sigma-decomp}\n\nwhere $\\sigma_L$ is the linear site-term dispersion (dependent on $V_{S30}$ and period), $\\sigma_I$ is the inter-reference dispersion (nonzero only when $V_{\\mathrm{ref}} = 3000\\,\\text{m/s}$), and $\\sigma_{NL}$ is the nonlinear dispersion (dependent on $V_{S30}$ and $pga^*$). All components are period-dependent and tabulated in the published model [@Stewart2020]. The detailed functional forms and calibration of each component are documented in [@sec-site_model]. ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The identification of $\\sigma_L$ (linear $V_{S30}$-dependent dispersion), $\\sigma_I$ (inter-reference dispersion, nonzero only when $V_{\\mathrm{ref}} = 3000$ m/s), and $\\sigma_{NL}$ (nonlinear, $V_{S30}$- and PGA-dependent dispersion) is consistent with the structure of the Hashash et al. (2020) model [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193]. The statement that $\\sigma_I$ is nonzero only when $V_{\\mathrm{ref}} = 3000$ m/s (the NGA-East hard-rock reference condition) correctly reflects the model's construction, where the inter-reference component accounts for uncertainty in the adjustment from the 3000 m/s to the 760 m/s reference [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193]. The claim is well-supported.]\n\n$$\nS_a\\!\\left(T_n, V_{S30}\\right) = F\\!\\left(T_n, V_{S30}\\right)\\,S_a\\!\\left(T_n, V_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\right).\n$${#eq-site-af}\n\nFor each return period $T_R$, oscillator period $T_n$, fractile $p$, and site class $V_{S30}$, the design spectral ordinate is defined as the maximum across the GMPE-based and amplification-based estimates [@eq-site-envelope]: ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The envelope approach — selecting the maximum spectral ordinate from the two model approaches at each period and hazard level — is a recognized and conservative method for design spectrum construction that protects against underestimation by either model individually. The notation and formulation are internally consistent with the preceding sections. The claim is well-supported.]\n\n$$\nS_a^{\\mathrm{max}}(T_n, V_{S30}) = \\max\\!\\left\\{S_a^{\\mathrm{GMPE}}(T_n, V_{S30}),\\; S_a^{F}(T_n, V_{S30})\\right\\}\n$${#eq-site-envelope}\n\nwhere $S_a^{\\mathrm{GMPE}}$ is the spectral ordinate obtained from the GMPE logic tree evaluated directly at the target $V_{S30}$, and $S_a^{F}$ is the spectral ordinate obtained by applying the amplification factor $F$ to the reference-rock hazard curve per [@eq-site-af]. This envelope ensures that the controlling (most hazardous) estimate governs the design spectrum at every period. GMPEs may underpredict at certain periods or $V_{S30}$ ranges where their empirical site terms are poorly constrained by the underlying strong-motion database, while the amplification model may underpredict where the reference-rock hazard curve does not fully represent near-field or source-specific effects. The envelope eliminates the risk of unconservative design by selecting the larger ordinate at each spectral period. ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The rationale for the envelope — that GMPEs may underpredict at poorly constrained $V_{S30}$ ranges while amplification models may underpredict for near-field or source-specific effects — is technically sound and reflects known limitations of both approaches [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193][WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf]. The selection of the maximum ordinate at each period as the governing design value is a standard conservative design choice. The claim is well-supported and technically accurate.]\n## Site Classification {#sec-vs30_appendix}\n\nThe **Vs30** parameter is widely measured in the field using methods such as borehole logging, seismic cone penetration tests, and surface wave techniques. Introduced into engineering practice in the early 1990s, it provides a simple, robust metric to characterize site conditions for earthquake ground motions. **Vs30** is defined as the time-averaged shear-wave velocity in the upper 30 meters of a site profile. In practical terms, it represents the overall stiffness of the near-surface soils and rock. Mathematically, Vs30 is computed as the harmonic average of shear-wave velocity ($V_{S}$) over the top 30 m: ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The definition of $V_{S30}$ as the time-averaged (harmonic mean) shear-wave velocity in the upper 30 m, its introduction in the early 1990s, and its widespread use in GMPE site characterisation are all confirmed by multiple authoritative sources including the USGS and NEHRP provisions [WEB:https://www.usgs.gov/publications/mapping-nehrp-vs30-site-classes]. The field measurement methods listed (borehole logging, seismic cone penetration test, surface wave techniques) are standard methods for determining shear-wave velocity profiles. The claim is well-supported.]\n\n$$\nV_{S30} = \\frac{30\\,\\text{m}}{\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\frac{H_i}{V_{S,i}}}\n$$\n\nwhere $H_i$ and $V_{S,i}$ are the thickness and shear-wave velocity of the $i$-th layer in the upper 30 m. This formula gives the equivalent uniform velocity that yields the same travel time through 30 m of material as the actual layered profile. ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The formula $V_{S30} = 30\\,\\text{m} / \\sum_{i=1}^{N}(H_i / V_{S,i})$ is the standard harmonic-mean (travel-time-weighted) definition of $V_{S30}$ as specified in ASCE 7 and NEHRP provisions [WEB:https://www.usgs.gov/publications/mapping-nehrp-vs30-site-classes][WEB:https://www.daveboore.com/pubs_online/vs30_from_shallow_models.pdf]. The interpretation as the equivalent uniform velocity yielding the same travel time through 30 m is mathematically correct. The claim is fully supported.]\n\nVs30 has become the primary quantitative predictor of site effects in modern ground-motion models. Virtually all recent ground motion prediction equations (GMPEs) incorporate Vs30 as a key input parameter to adjust predicted shaking for site conditions. Empirical GMPEs (e.g., NGA-West and NGA-East) often use a functional form in which the median ground motion is scaled according to $\\ln(V_{S30})$ or a similar proxy, with separate terms for linear scaling and nonlinear saturation at low velocities. Large datasets have validated the use of Vs30 in GMPEs, showing consistent correlations between Vs30 and site amplification across many locations [@Borcherdt2012]. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The use of $V_{S30}$ as the primary site parameter in NGA-West2 and NGA-East GMPEs, with $\\ln(V_{S30})$-based functional forms and separate nonlinear terms, is well-established and confirmed by published GMPE documentation [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193][WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf]. The reference [@Borcherdt2012] likely refers to Borcherdt (2012) on site coefficients and site classification, a recognized authority on the topic [WEB:https://pubs.usgs.gov/publication/70022904]. However, the specific year (2012) and full citation details for this Borcherdt reference could not be independently confirmed from the available sources; published Borcherdt work on site classification is from several periods including 1994 and later. Confidence is MEDIUM due to the partially unverified citation year for the Borcherdt reference.]\n\nIn parallel, site-specific analyses (such as 1D site response simulations) rely on Vs30 as an initial classification of the site profile, although they incorporate the full shear-wave velocity profile with depth. While Vs30 does not capture all facets of site response—such as basin depth or resonance effects—it serves as a practical first-order proxy. ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The statement that site-specific 1D site response simulations use the full shear-wave velocity profile rather than relying solely on $V_{S30}$, and that $V_{S30}$ does not capture basin depth or resonance effects, is accurate and consistent with the established literature on site response analysis [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193][WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf]. The description of $V_{S30}$ as a practical first-order proxy is standard and well-supported. The claim is accurate.]\n\n### NEHRP Site Classes\n\nThe NEHRP provisions [-@BSSC2015] classify sites into categories (Site Class A through E, and special Class F) based on Vs30 ranges. This system provides a convenient way to assign site amplification factors in building codes and engineering practice. The standard classes and their Vs30 criteria are approximately: ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The NEHRP site classification system (Site Classes A through F) based on $V_{S30}$ thresholds is defined in the NEHRP provisions and adopted by ASCE 7 and IBC. The reference [@BSSC2015] corresponds to the 2015 NEHRP Recommended Seismic Provisions for New Buildings and Other Structures (FEMA P-1050), which documents this classification system [WEB:https://www.nehrp.gov/pdf/H-18-8.pdf]. The description is accurate and the claim is well-supported.]\n\n* **Site Class A:** Hard rock, $V_{S30}>1500$ m/s (very high velocity material).\n* **Site Class B:** Rock, $V_{S30}\\approx$ 760–1500 m/s (typical competent rock; 760 m/s is the B/C boundary).\n* **Site Class C:** Very dense soil or soft rock, $V_{S30}\\approx$ 360–760 m/s.\n* **Site Class D:** Stiff soil, $V_{S30}\\approx$ 180–360 m/s.\n* **Site Class E:** Soft soil, $V_{S30}<$ 180 m/s (includes clay-rich soils prone to high amplification).\n* **Site Class F:** Soils requiring site-specific evaluation (e.g., very soft clays, peats, liquefiable soils, or thick high-plasticity clays irrespective of Vs30).\n\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The $V_{S30}$ thresholds for NEHRP Site Classes A through F — A: $>1500$ m/s, B: 760-1500 m/s, C: 360-760 m/s, D: 180-360 m/s, E: $<180$ m/s, F: special conditions — are consistent with NEHRP provisions and are confirmed by multiple USGS and ASCE 7 sources [WEB:https://www.usgs.gov/publications/mapping-nehrp-vs30-site-classes][WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. The identification of 760 m/s as the B/C boundary is accurate. The description of Site Class F as defined by qualitative subsoil conditions rather than a $V_{S30}$ threshold is also correct. The claim is well-supported.]\n\nClasses A–E are defined by the Vs30 thresholds above. Class F is defined by qualitative subsoil conditions (such as softness or thickness) rather than a Vs30 value; if those conditions are met, a detailed site response analysis is mandated in lieu of using generic factors. These site classes provide \"unambiguous definitions of site categories and corresponding site coefficients\" in building code applications. In structural design, each class is assigned factors for ground motion adjustment (e.g. $F_a$, $F_v$ in NEHRP/ASCE 7) that modify the standard response spectrum to account for local site amplification. ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The description of Site Class F as requiring site-specific analysis and the use of $F_a$ and $F_v$ factors in NEHRP/ASCE 7 for Classes A-E to adjust the standard response spectrum are standard provisions confirmed by NEHRP and ASCE 7 documentation [WEB:https://www.nehrp.gov/pdf/H-18-8.pdf][WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. The quoted phrase \"unambiguous definitions of site categories and corresponding site coefficients\" is consistent with the purpose of the NEHRP site classification system. The claim is accurate and well-supported.]\n\n### AS1726 Guidelines\n\nThe Australian Standard AS 1726-2017 [-@AS1726-2017] provides guidelines for conducting geotechnical investigations and includes a site classification system that categorises sites into classes based on their soil properties, such as shear wave velocity parameter $\\bar v_S$, depth to bedrock, and soil type. The Australian Standard AS 1170.4 \"Earthquake Actions on Structures\" uses a similar site classification system based on the stiffness of the ground, but it does not explicitly use the shear wave velocity parameter $\\bar v_S$ to classify the ground. The categories range from A (rock) to E (soft soil), with a special category (S) for sites requiring site-specific investigations. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: AS 1726-2017 is a current Australian standard for geotechnical site investigations and includes guidance on site classification. AS 1170.4 is the Australian standard for earthquake actions. The description of the A-to-E plus special category (S) classification hierarchy and the role of shear wave velocity in AS 1726-2017 but not explicitly in AS 1170.4 is plausible and consistent with the known structure of Australian standards. However, independent external confirmation of the specific AS 1170.4 classification details described here (particularly that it does not explicitly use $\\bar v_S$) could not be established from the publicly available sources reviewed. Confidence is MEDIUM due to the limited publicly accessible external verification of the specific AS 1170.4 provisions.]\n\n{{< include /psha/_tbl/AS1726.qmd >}}\n\n### ASCE/SEI 7-22 Guidelines\n\nThe ASCE/SEI 7-22 Standard [-@ASCE722] provides guidelines for conducting geotechnical investigations and includes a site classification system that categorises sites into classes based on their soil properties, such as shear wave velocity, depth to bedrock, and soil type. According to ASCE 7-22 [@ASCE722], the site soil shall be classified based on the average shear wave velocity parameter, $\\bar v_S$, which is derived from the measured shear wave velocity profile from the ground surface to a depth of 30 m. ^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: ASCE 7-22 Chapter 20 specifies site classification based on $\\bar v_S$ (average shear-wave velocity in the upper 30 m, equivalent to $V_{S30}$), along with average standard penetration test blow count and undrained shear strength. The description of the classification being based on soil properties including shear wave velocity, depth to bedrock, and soil type is accurate [WEB:https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s11][WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. The claim is well-supported.]\n\n{{< include /psha/_tbl/ASCE722.qmd >}}\n\n### NBC-2020 Guidelines\n\nThe National Building Code of Canada [-@NBC2020] provides guidelines for site characterization and seismic site response. Similar to ASCE 7-22, it uses an average shear wave velocity in the upper 30 m (commonly denoted $v_{S30}$), standard penetration test (SPT) blow count, and/or undrained shear strength ($s_{u}$) to classify sites into categories A through F. Where site-specific investigations are warranted (e.g., liquefiable soils, highly sensitive clays, peat), the code requires more detailed analysis. ^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The National Building Code of Canada (NBC) 2020 includes seismic site classification provisions that use $v_{S30}$, SPT blow count, and undrained shear strength $s_u$ for site categories A through F, consistent with the description provided. The classification structure is aligned with the NEHRP/ASCE 7 framework [WEB:https://www.usgs.gov/publications/mapping-nehrp-vs30-site-classes]. The requirement for more detailed analysis for special site conditions (liquefiable soils, sensitive clays, peat) is standard across major building codes. Confidence is MEDIUM because independent external confirmation of the specific NBC 2020 site classification provisions from publicly accessible sources was limited; the description is consistent with the known NBC 2015/2020 framework but the specific 2020 edition details require access to the normative document.]\n\n{{< include /psha/_tbl/NBC2020.qmd >}}\n","numLines":67,"startLine":158,"totalLines":224}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01NiafC3X9WaeDFoW9jGgo8o","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I need to read more of both source files to see all the content. Let me continue with more chunks. I'll focus on the parts past line 236 for source1 and past line 224 for source2.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11763,"cache_read_input_tokens":66178,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11763,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bf7093-818d-4370-9615-a0aeec356215","uuid":"ce6a0e86-0f71-4962-9ba3-622cdc74639f"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01NiafC3X9WaeDFoW9jGgo8o","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Gofu3Aq4g5DHGRsvf4dnBi","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/review/psha/sessions/run-20260329_195849/psha_hive.review.md","limit":80,"offset":236},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11763,"cache_read_input_tokens":66178,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11763,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bf7093-818d-4370-9615-a0aeec356215","uuid":"254e1491-cd9b-4100-9b95-8375b8768893"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Gofu3Aq4g5DHGRsvf4dnBi","type":"tool_result","content":"236\t{{< include /psha/_tbl/AS1726.qmd >}}\n237\t\n238\t### ASCE/SEI 7-22 Guidelines\n239\t\n240\tThe ASCE/SEI 7-22 Standard [-@ASCE722] provides guidelines for conducting geotechnical investigations and includes a site classification system that categorises sites into classes based on their soil properties, such as shear wave velocity, depth to bedrock, and soil type. According to ASCE 7-22 [@ASCE722], the site soil shall be classified based on the average shear wave velocity parameter, $\\bar v_S$, which is derived from the measured shear wave velocity profile from the ground surface to a depth of 30 m.\n241\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The description of ASCE 7-22 site classification based on the average shear-wave velocity $\\bar v_S$ over the upper 30 m is consistent with the ASCE 7-22 standard and confirmed by multiple sources [WEB:https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s11][WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. The additional classification inputs (depth to bedrock, soil type) are also part of the ASCE 7-22 site classification procedure. The notation $\\bar v_S$ (overbar) is used in ASCE 7-22 to denote the parameter previously labeled $V_{S30}$ in earlier editions, which is a minor but accurate terminological distinction.]\n242\t\n243\t{{< include /psha/_tbl/ASCE722.qmd >}}\n244\t\n245\t### NBC-2020 Guidelines\n246\t\n247\tThe National Building Code of Canada [-@NBC2020] provides guidelines for site characterization and seismic site response. Similar to ASCE 7-22, it uses an average shear wave velocity in the upper 30 m (commonly denoted $v_{S30}$), standard penetration test (SPT) blow count, and/or undrained shear strength ($s_{u}$) to classify sites into categories A through F. Where site-specific investigations are warranted (e.g., liquefiable soils, highly sensitive clays, peat), the code requires more detailed analysis.\n248\t^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The National Building Code of Canada 2020 (NBC 2020) includes seismic site classification using $v_{S30}$, SPT blow count, and undrained shear strength, with categories A through F and requirements for site-specific analysis under special conditions; this structure is consistent with the 2015 edition of the NBC (which introduced the $v_{S30}$-based classification aligned with NEHRP practice) and is plausible for the 2020 edition. The NBC is not freely available in its full text, preventing complete independent verification of the exact classification thresholds and procedure for the 2020 edition [WEB:https://www.earthquakes.bgs.ac.uk/hazard/haz_guide/psha.html]. The description is nonetheless technically plausible and consistent with the evolution of Canadian seismic provisions.]\n249\t\n250\t{{< include /psha/_tbl/NBC2020.qmd >}}\n251\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bf7093-818d-4370-9615-a0aeec356215","uuid":"2ecccd9b-7f4d-4d35-9360-a5ebbe362644","timestamp":"2026-03-30T01:28:39.943Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/review/psha/sessions/run-20260329_195849/psha_hive.review.md","content":"{{< include /psha/_tbl/AS1726.qmd >}}\n\n### ASCE/SEI 7-22 Guidelines\n\nThe ASCE/SEI 7-22 Standard [-@ASCE722] provides guidelines for conducting geotechnical investigations and includes a site classification system that categorises sites into classes based on their soil properties, such as shear wave velocity, depth to bedrock, and soil type. According to ASCE 7-22 [@ASCE722], the site soil shall be classified based on the average shear wave velocity parameter, $\\bar v_S$, which is derived from the measured shear wave velocity profile from the ground surface to a depth of 30 m.\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The description of ASCE 7-22 site classification based on the average shear-wave velocity $\\bar v_S$ over the upper 30 m is consistent with the ASCE 7-22 standard and confirmed by multiple sources [WEB:https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s11][WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. The additional classification inputs (depth to bedrock, soil type) are also part of the ASCE 7-22 site classification procedure. The notation $\\bar v_S$ (overbar) is used in ASCE 7-22 to denote the parameter previously labeled $V_{S30}$ in earlier editions, which is a minor but accurate terminological distinction.]\n\n{{< include /psha/_tbl/ASCE722.qmd >}}\n\n### NBC-2020 Guidelines\n\nThe National Building Code of Canada [-@NBC2020] provides guidelines for site characterization and seismic site response. Similar to ASCE 7-22, it uses an average shear wave velocity in the upper 30 m (commonly denoted $v_{S30}$), standard penetration test (SPT) blow count, and/or undrained shear strength ($s_{u}$) to classify sites into categories A through F. Where site-specific investigations are warranted (e.g., liquefiable soils, highly sensitive clays, peat), the code requires more detailed analysis.\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The National Building Code of Canada 2020 (NBC 2020) includes seismic site classification using $v_{S30}$, SPT blow count, and undrained shear strength, with categories A through F and requirements for site-specific analysis under special conditions; this structure is consistent with the 2015 edition of the NBC (which introduced the $v_{S30}$-based classification aligned with NEHRP practice) and is plausible for the 2020 edition. The NBC is not freely available in its full text, preventing complete independent verification of the exact classification thresholds and procedure for the 2020 edition [WEB:https://www.earthquakes.bgs.ac.uk/hazard/haz_guide/psha.html]. The description is nonetheless technically plausible and consistent with the evolution of Canadian seismic provisions.]\n\n{{< include /psha/_tbl/NBC2020.qmd >}}\n","numLines":16,"startLine":236,"totalLines":251}}}
