{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KnCEUxsnkYrrTbSjPgeAJo","type":"tool_result","content":"# GISTM AEP Requirements and Comparisons\n\nBased on the article provided, **specific annual exceedance probability (AEP) values for each consequence class are not mentioned**. The document does not list numerical AEP requirements such as 1/200, 1/500, 1/1000, 1/2500, or 1/10000.\n\n## What the Article Actually Discusses\n\nThe article emphasizes that designers should \"Develop preliminary designs for the tailings facility with external loading design criteria consistent with both the consequence of failure classification selected based on current conditions and higher Consequence Classifications (including 'Extreme').\"\n\nThe key distinction highlighted is that the GISTM recommends adopting **\"Extreme\" consequence category as the default setting** for new facilities and upgrades, which requires \"design for potentially significantly higher design loads (i.e. earthquakes with a 10,000 year return period).\"\n\n## Absent Information\n\nThe article contains **no discussion of**:\n- Specific AEP values for any consequence class\n- Passive closure requirements\n- Comparative analysis with CDA or ANCOLD AEP specifications\n\nThe focus remains on governance, transparency, and the principle of designing to extreme consequences rather than providing technical engineering parameters."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bf7093-818d-4370-9615-a0aeec356215","uuid":"4c0d5ce4-1423-423b-a4f9-997c69418018","timestamp":"2026-03-30T01:31:39.723Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":215639,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# GISTM AEP Requirements and Comparisons\n\nBased on the article provided, **specific annual exceedance probability (AEP) values for each consequence class are not mentioned**. The document does not list numerical AEP requirements such as 1/200, 1/500, 1/1000, 1/2500, or 1/10000.\n\n## What the Article Actually Discusses\n\nThe article emphasizes that designers should \"Develop preliminary designs for the tailings facility with external loading design criteria consistent with both the consequence of failure classification selected based on current conditions and higher Consequence Classifications (including 'Extreme').\"\n\nThe key distinction highlighted is that the GISTM recommends adopting **\"Extreme\" consequence category as the default setting** for new facilities and upgrades, which requires \"design for potentially significantly higher design loads (i.e. earthquakes with a 10,000 year return period).\"\n\n## Absent Information\n\nThe article contains **no discussion of**:\n- Specific AEP values for any consequence class\n- Passive closure requirements\n- Comparative analysis with CDA or ANCOLD AEP specifications\n\nThe focus remains on governance, transparency, and the principle of designing to extreme consequences rather than providing technical engineering parameters.","durationMs":25454,"url":"https://atcwilliams.com/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management-major-improvement-or-death-by-a-thousand-codes/"}}
