{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013EVt9ZjLaxVwzQgYZJL2Fy","type":"tool_result","content":"# AEP Requirements and Technical Specifications in GISTM\n\nBased on my review of the provided web page content, **there is no information about Annual Exceedance Probability (AEP) requirements, specific AEP values by consequence class, passive closure requirements, or any tables comparing AEP standards.**\n\nThe article provides a high-level overview of GISTM's purpose, governance structure, and implementation timeline, but does not contain technical specifications regarding:\n\n- Specific AEP thresholds (1/200, 1/500, 1/1000, etc.)\n- Consequence class-specific design requirements\n- Lifecycle phase distinctions for hydrological standards\n- Passive closure technical requirements\n- Comparative analysis with other tailings management standards\n\nThe page focuses on contextual elements such as the standard's origins following the Brumadinho disaster, its 15 principles and 77 requirements framework, and compliance deadlines for ICMM members, but omits the technical detail you're seeking.\n\nTo find detailed AEP specifications and consequence-class tables, you would need to consult the full GISTM documentation or technical guidance materials directly."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bf7093-818d-4370-9615-a0aeec356215","uuid":"f24b1087-e47d-4c33-befe-84a168214af5","timestamp":"2026-03-30T01:32:45.299Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":34236,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# AEP Requirements and Technical Specifications in GISTM\n\nBased on my review of the provided web page content, **there is no information about Annual Exceedance Probability (AEP) requirements, specific AEP values by consequence class, passive closure requirements, or any tables comparing AEP standards.**\n\nThe article provides a high-level overview of GISTM's purpose, governance structure, and implementation timeline, but does not contain technical specifications regarding:\n\n- Specific AEP thresholds (1/200, 1/500, 1/1000, etc.)\n- Consequence class-specific design requirements\n- Lifecycle phase distinctions for hydrological standards\n- Passive closure technical requirements\n- Comparative analysis with other tailings management standards\n\nThe page focuses on contextual elements such as the standard's origins following the Brumadinho disaster, its 15 principles and 77 requirements framework, and compliance deadlines for ICMM members, but omits the technical detail you're seeking.\n\nTo find detailed AEP specifications and consequence-class tables, you would need to consult the full GISTM documentation or technical guidance materials directly.","durationMs":11553,"url":"https://blog.gistm.net/blog/a1-understanding-gistm-complete-overview/"}}
