{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BZ3hYaFs3g12JK6VGyParC","type":"tool_result","content":"# Response\n\nBased on the article provided, **neither of these topics is addressed**.\n\nThe article focuses specifically on guidance for structural engineers using ASCE 7-22 seismic design provisions in the Central and Eastern United States. It compares two response spectrum options (Multi-Period and Two-Period Design Response Spectra) and recommends practical approaches to avoid overly conservative designs.\n\nThe article does not discuss:\n- Scale factors for converting RotD50 to RotD100 spectral values\n- Log-log interpolation methods between period anchor values\n- S_MS, S_M1 MCER (Maximum Considered Earthquake Response) parameters\n- The mathematical relationships between MCER-level and design-level parameters\n\nTo find information on these technical specifications, you would need to consult the full ASCE 7-22 standard document itself rather than this guidance article."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bf7093-818d-4370-9615-a0aeec356215","uuid":"c1e3f63e-0f8a-46ff-a93d-17f13a67a933","timestamp":"2026-03-30T01:34:40.864Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":145997,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Response\n\nBased on the article provided, **neither of these topics is addressed**.\n\nThe article focuses specifically on guidance for structural engineers using ASCE 7-22 seismic design provisions in the Central and Eastern United States. It compares two response spectrum options (Multi-Period and Two-Period Design Response Spectra) and recommends practical approaches to avoid overly conservative designs.\n\nThe article does not discuss:\n- Scale factors for converting RotD50 to RotD100 spectral values\n- Log-log interpolation methods between period anchor values\n- S_MS, S_M1 MCER (Maximum Considered Earthquake Response) parameters\n- The mathematical relationships between MCER-level and design-level parameters\n\nTo find information on these technical specifications, you would need to consult the full ASCE 7-22 standard document itself rather than this guidance article.","durationMs":13098,"url":"https://www.asce.org/communities/institutes-and-technical-groups/structural-engineering-institute/news/asce-7-22-ground-motions---a-rational-approach-for-structural-engineers"}}
