CE%207-16%20Supplements-%20For%20reference.pdf\"}]\n\nLet me search more specifically for Section 21.1 of ASCE 7-22 with the specific scale factor values you mentioned.\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 21 - Site-Specific Ground Motion Procedures for Seismic Design | ASCE Amplify\",\"url\":\"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s21\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE 7-22 Ground Motions - A Rational Approach for Structural Engineers | ASCE\",\"url\":\"https://www.asce.org/communities/institutes-and-technical-groups/structural-engineering-institute/news/asce-7-22-ground-motions---a-rational-approach-for-structural-engineers\"},{\"title\":\"Design Ground Motions | U.S. Geological Survey\",\"url\":\"https://www.usgs.gov/programs/earthquake-hazards/design-ground-motions\"},{\"title\":\"S-1 Standard 7-16 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/pdf/10.1061/9780784414248.sup1\"},{\"title\":\"GROUND MOTION SELECTION GUIDE\",\"url\":\"https://slc.eeri.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/Ground-Motion-Selection-Guide-2024.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"S-1 Standard 7-16 Minimum Design Loads and Associated Criteria\",\"url\":\"https://sbcc.wa.gov/sites/default/files/2022-04/3)%20ASCE%207-16%20Supplements-%20For%20reference.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"ASCE/SEI 7-22 | Chapter 22 - Seismic Ground Motion and Long-Period Transition Maps | ASCE Amplify\",\"url\":\"https://amplify.asce.org/content/standard/9780784415788/part/provisions/standard-chapter/s22\"},{\"title\":\"Page 1 of 18\",\"url\":\"https://seau.starchapter.com/images/downloads/SEAUNews_Documents/asce7_16workshop_whitepaper_20200131_final.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"How many records should be used in ASCE/SEI-7 ground motion scaling procedure | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/275600580_How_many_records_should_be_used_in_ASCESEI-7_ground_motion_scaling_procedure\"},{\"title\":\"NIST GCR 11-917-15 Selecting and Scaling Earthquake Ground\",\"url\":\"https://www.nehrp.gov/pdf/nistgcr11-917-15.pdf\"}]\n\nLet me try a more specific search focused on the numerical values you mentioned.\n\nNo links found.\n\nBased on my web search results, I was able to find general information about ASCE 7-22 and ground motion procedures related to RotD50 and RotD100 spectral definitions, but I was unable to locate the specific technical details from ASCE 7-22 Section 21.1 that you're inquiring about, particularly the exact scale factors (1.2, 1.25, 1.3), the maximum direction definition related to log-log interpolation, and how these specific numerical values are applied.\n\nHere's what I can confirm from the search results:\n\nGround motion models in the US are established using ground motion models based on the RotD50 response spectra definition, however, the code spectrum is based on the maximum direction definition defined by RotD100. These methods typically use the median spectral ordinate over all non-redundant orientations, referred to as RotD50, and the maximum spectral ordinate over all non-redundant orientations, referred to as RotD100.\n\nThe search results indicate that ASCE 7-22 Chapter 21 addresses site-specific ground motion procedures, but the specific scale factors and interpolation methodology you're asking about are not clearly detailed in the available web sources.\n\nTo get the specific technical details about the scale factors (1.2, 1.25, 1.3) and log-log interpolation procedures in Section 21.1, you would need to consult the actual ASCE 7-22 standard document directly, as these detailed specifications are typically found in the full standard text rather than in publicly available excerpts or summaries online.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bf7093-818d-4370-9615-a0aeec356215","uuid":"a99b2ab9-36db-4a84-9cb3-aaf00c75cef7","timestamp":"2026-03-30T01:36:18.175Z","tool_us