ormation about GISTM design criteria requirements.",{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01AxRN47bfFJhzzuLRfzrCjo","content":[{"title":"Practical steps to Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM) compliance for operational tailings storage facilities in South Africa","url":"https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S2225-62532022000600006"},{"title":"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management (GISTM)","url":"https://www.goldfields.com/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management-gistm.php"},{"title":"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management: Major Improvement or “Death by a Thousand Codes”? - ATC Williams","url":"https://atcwilliams.com/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management-major-improvement-or-death-by-a-thousand-codes/"},{"title":"Tailings facility dam safety review (DSR) - Tailings storage dam management & regulations - Barr","url":"https://www.barr.com/Insights/Insights-Article/ArtMID/1344/ArticleID/616/How-safe-is-your-tailings-dam"},{"title":"Tailings Storage Facility GISTM Disclosure Report","url":"https://www.teck.com/media/Aug-2025-GISTM-TSF-Disclosure.pdf"},{"title":"Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management","url":"https://thegtmi.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/GTMI-FAQ.pdf"},{"title":"GISTM Principle 15 – August 2025 Public Disclosure El Indio - Tambo Mine","url":"http://q4live.s25.clientfiles.s3-website-us-east-1.amazonaws.com/322814910/files/doc_downloads/gistm/Pastos-Largos-TSF_GISTM_Principle15_2025_Final.pdf"},{"title":"ICMM - Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management","url":"https://www.icmm.com/en-gb/our-principles/tailings/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management"},{"title":"Our approach to GISTM | Anglo American","url":"https://www.angloamerican.com/esg-policies-and-data/tailings-summary/our-approach-to-gistm"},{"title":"Reviewing Principles 2 & 3 of the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management - The Insight Blog from Insight Terra","url":"https://www.insightterra.com/blog-posts/gistm-principles-2-and-3"}]},"Based on my search results, I have found relevant information about GISTM 2020, though the specific design criteria values (1/200 and 1/10000 AEP) you mentioned were not explicitly detailed in the search results I found. However, I can provide you with what the search found:\n\n## GISTM 2020 Consequence Classification and Design Criteria\n\nThe GISTM dam classification has five classes of potential consequences (Low, Significant, High, Very High, and Extreme).\n\nThe current value used for flood design in South Africa is a 150-year, 24-hour storm event (2% annual exceedance probability) for all dam classifications, though GISTM requirements are significantly stricter than the South African requirements and are based on the consequence classification of the individual dams.\n\nRegarding design approach, the main difference between the GISTM and other commonly used guidelines is the recommended adoption of the extreme design criteria (i.e. consequence category) as the default setting for the design of new and upgrades to existing tailings storage facilities (TSFs).\n\nRegarding passive closure specifically, long term draindown will be passively managed in evaporation cells in closure configurations for tailings facilities.\n\nHowever, I was unable to find the specific AEP values of 1/200 and 1/10000 directly linked to the Low, Medium, High, and Extreme consequence classes in these search results. To find the exact design criteria table mapping these AEP values to specific consequence classifications, you may need to access the full GISTM 2020 document directly, particularly Annex 2 which is referenced for consequence classification tables."],"durationSeconds":20.706147885000043}}
