{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01StbgJYRg8DkrbZnhbCbh1A","type":"tool_result","content":"# GISTM AEP Criteria and Related Topics\n\n## Annual Exceedance Probability Values\n\nThe paper **does not provide specific AEP values** (such as 1/200, 1/500, etc.) for different consequence classes. It only states that \"The GISTM requirements are shown in [Table V]\" regarding flood and seismic design criteria, but the actual table content with specific numerical values is not included in the provided text.\n\n## Current South African Practice\n\nThe document notes that present methodology uses: \"a 150-year, 24-hour storm event (2% annual exceedance probability) for all dam classifications.\"\n\n## Comparison with International Standards\n\nThe paper does **not compare GISTM with CDA (Canadian Dam Association) or ANCOLD (Australasian National Committee on Large Dams)** standards. It only references Campbell et al. (2019) regarding comparative analysis of tailings legislation across mining jurisdictions.\n\n## Passive Closure Requirements\n\nThe document **contains no discussion of passive closure requirements**. Its focus is on operational facility compliance with GISTM rather than decommissioning or closure-phase management.\n\n## Key Limitation\n\nThe referenced [Table V] would contain the specific design criteria details, but this table's data is not reproduced in the provided web content excerpt."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bf7093-818d-4370-9615-a0aeec356215","uuid":"c07de3cd-6def-4a97-8c79-53480d143947","timestamp":"2026-03-30T01:36:33.650Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":68779,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# GISTM AEP Criteria and Related Topics\n\n## Annual Exceedance Probability Values\n\nThe paper **does not provide specific AEP values** (such as 1/200, 1/500, etc.) for different consequence classes. It only states that \"The GISTM requirements are shown in [Table V]\" regarding flood and seismic design criteria, but the actual table content with specific numerical values is not included in the provided text.\n\n## Current South African Practice\n\nThe document notes that present methodology uses: \"a 150-year, 24-hour storm event (2% annual exceedance probability) for all dam classifications.\"\n\n## Comparison with International Standards\n\nThe paper does **not compare GISTM with CDA (Canadian Dam Association) or ANCOLD (Australasian National Committee on Large Dams)** standards. It only references Campbell et al. (2019) regarding comparative analysis of tailings legislation across mining jurisdictions.\n\n## Passive Closure Requirements\n\nThe document **contains no discussion of passive closure requirements**. Its focus is on operational facility compliance with GISTM rather than decommissioning or closure-phase management.\n\n## Key Limitation\n\nThe referenced [Table V] would contain the specific design criteria details, but this table's data is not reproduced in the provided web content excerpt.","durationMs":6688,"url":"https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S2225-62532022000600006"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Mn1xXkTMELCTYgsCNyZH72","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"NEHRP 2020 FEMA P-2082 site classification changes new sub-classes BC CD expanded site categories\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"NEHRP Recommended Seismic Provisions for New ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.wbdg.org/FFC/DHS/fema_p_2082_1_vol_2_part_3_resource_papers_2020.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"FEMA P-2082-1: NEHRP Recommended Seismic ...\",\"url\":\"https://nibs.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/fema_2020-nehrp-provisions_part-1-and-part-2.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Fema\",\"url\":\"https://www.fema.gov/sites/default/files/2020-10/fema_2020-nehrp-provisions_part-1-and-part-2.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"S e i s m i c D e s i gn Categor y Ma ps for 2024 I RC and IBC\",\"url\":\"https://www.fema.gov/sites/default/files/documents/fema_p-2192-nehrp-provisions-seismic-design-maps-2024-irc-ibc.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"2020 Edition - NEHRP Recommended Seismic Provisions For New Buildings And Other Structures Volume I: Part 1 Provisions, Part 2 Commentary | WBDG - Whole Building Design Guide\",\"url\":\"https://www.wbdg.org/dhs/criteria/fema-p-2082-vol-1\"},{\"title\":\"FEMA P-2082-2 NEHRP Recommended Seismic ...\",\"url\":\"https://drupal.nibs.org/files/pdfs/FEMA_P-2082-2_2020-nehrp-pro.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"2020 Edition NEHRP Recommended Seismic Provisions ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.wbdg.org/FFC/DHS/fema_p_2082_1_vol_1_pt_1_prov_pt_2_comm_2020.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction to 2024 Edition Seismic Design Category Maps July 2024\",\"url\":\"https://www.fema.gov/sites/default/files/documents/fema-seismicdesigncategorymaps-july2024.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"NEHRP (National Earthquake Hazards Reduction Program) Recommended Seismic Provisions: for New Buildings and Other Structures (FEMA P-2082-1) 2020 ... I: Part 1 Provisions and Part 2 Commentary: FEMA: 9798553320195: Amazon.com: Books\",\"url\":\"https://www.amazon.com/National-Earthquake-Reduction-Recommended-Provisions/dp/B08M8DBG1L\"},{\"title\":\"FEMA P-2082-2 NEHRP Recommended Seismic ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.nibs.org/files/pdfs/FEMA_P-2082-2_2020-nehrp-pro.pdf\"}]\n\nI found information indicating that the 2020 NEHRP Recommended Provisions define Site Classes C, CD, and D. The search results reference FEMA P-2082 (the 2020 NEHRP Recommended Seismic Provisions), which includes site classification updates. \n\nHowever, the search results provided limited detailed content about the specific changes to site classifications and the introduction of new sub-classes BC and CD. The results indicate these are real classifications in the 2020 NEHRP document, but don't contain extensive details about how these categories were expanded or modified.\n\nTo get more comprehensive information about the specific changes and expanded site categories BC, CD, and other classification details in FEMA P-2082, you may want to directly access the full FEMA P-2082-1 document (the Provisions and Commentary volumes) or consult technical summaries and guidance documents that specifically address the site classification changes between previous editions and the 2020 update.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bf7093-818d-4370-9615-a0aeec356215","uuid":"92a62881-f7ce-4090-b203-40120d92517c","timestamp":"2026-03-30T01:36:35.317Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"NEHRP 2020 FEMA P-2082 site classification changes new sub-classes BC CD expanded site categories","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01BWmahZVmKJYDHGYhUGjJFS","content":[{"title":"NEHRP Recommended Seismic Provisions for New ...","url":"https://www.wbdg.org/FFC/DHS/fema_p_2082_1_vol_2_part_3_resource_papers_2020.pdf"},{"title":"FEMA P-2082-1: NEHRP Recommended Seismic ...","url":"https://nibs.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/fema_2020-nehrp-provisions_part-1-and-part-2.pdf"},{"title":"Fema","url":"https://www.fema.gov/sites/default/files/2020-10/fema_2020-nehrp-provisions_part-1-and-part-2.pdf"},{"title":"S e i s m i c D e s i gn Categor y Ma ps for 2024 I RC and IBC","url":"https://www.fema.gov/sites/default/files/documents/fema_p-2192-nehrp-provisions-seismic-design-maps-2024-irc-ibc.pdf"},{"title":"2020 Edition - NEHRP Recommended Seismic Provisions For New Buildings And Other Structures Volume I: Part 1 Provisions, Part 2 Commentary | WBDG - Whole Building Design Guide","url":"https://www.wbdg.org/dhs/criteria/fema-p-2082-vol-1"},{"title":"FEMA P-2082-2 NEHRP Recommended Seismic ...","url":"https://drupal.nibs.org/files/pdfs/FEMA_P-2082-2_2020-nehrp-pro.pdf"},{"title":"2020 Edition NEHRP Recommended Seismic Provisions ...","url":"https://www.wbdg.org/FFC/DHS/fema_p_2082_1_vol_1_pt_1_prov_pt_2_comm_2020.pdf"},{"title":"Introduction to 2024 Edition Seismic Design Category Maps July 2024","url":"https://www.fema.gov/sites/default/files/documents/fema-seismicdesigncategorymaps-july2024.pdf"},{"title":"NEHRP (National Earthquake Hazards Reduction Program) Recommended Seismic Provisions: for New Buildings and Other Structures (FEMA P-2082-1) 2020 ... I: Part 1 Provisions and Part 2 Commentary: FEMA: 9798553320195: Amazon.com: Books","url":"https://www.amazon.com/National-Earthquake-Reduction-Recommended-Provisions/dp/B08M8DBG1L"},{"title":"FEMA P-2082-2 NEHRP Recommended Seismic ...","url":"https://www.nibs.org/files/pdfs/FEMA_P-2082-2_2020-nehrp-pro.pdf"}]},"I found information indicating that the 2020 NEHRP Recommended Provisions define Site Classes C, CD, and D. The search results reference FEMA P-2082 (the 2020 NEHRP Recommended Seismic Provisions), which includes site classification updates. \n\nHowever, the search results provided limited detailed content about the specific changes to site classifications and the introduction of new sub-classes BC and CD. The results indicate these are real classifications in the 2020 NEHRP document, but don't contain extensive details about how these categories were expanded or modified.\n\nTo get more comprehensive information about the specific changes and expanded site categories BC, CD, and other classification details in FEMA P-2082, you may want to directly access the full FEMA P-2082-1 document (the Provisions and Commentary volumes) or consult technical summaries and guidance documents that specifically address the site classification changes between previous editions and the 2020 update."],"durationSeconds":5.9346462060000045}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EXoFzfdzaLQs2VeWWMiXHg","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Bazzurro Cornell 1999 \"disaggregation of seismic hazard\" notation subscript ordering magnitude distance epsilon conditional probability\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard\",\"url\":\"http://sismologia.ist.utl.pt/~sismologia.daemon/files/Bazurro_and_Cornell_Disaggregation_of_Seismic_Hazard.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/245939078_Disaggregation_of_Seismic_Hazard\"},{\"title\":\"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard | Southern California Earthquake Center\",\"url\":\"https://www.scec.org/publication/407\"},{\"title\":\"Disaggregation of seismic hazard | Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America | GeoScienceWorld\",\"url\":\"https://pubs.geoscienceworld.org/ssa/bssa/article-abstract/89/2/501/342641/Disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard\"},{\"title\":\"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard | Statewide California Earthquake Center\",\"url\":\"https://central.scec.org/publication/407\"},{\"title\":\"Ground motion scenarios consistent with probabilistic seismic hazard disaggregation analysis. Application to Mainland Portugal | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-008-9088-z\"},{\"title\":\"Disaggregation of seismic hazard (1999) | Paolo Bazzurro | 518 Citations\",\"url\":\"https://typeset.io/papers/disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard-3e6zx96xtw\"},{\"title\":\"Researchgate\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Paolo_Bazzurro/publication/245939078_Disaggregation_of_Seismic_Hazard/links/54202dc10cf203f155c2afe3/Disaggregation-of-Seismic-Hazard.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Disaggregation of seismic hazard | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard-Bazzurro-Cornell/907cde3d1c6f6ca8b6e79ded693847be6abe6866\"},{\"title\":\"Disaggregation of seismic hazard according to Chinese ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.iitk.ac.in/nicee/wcee/article/14_07-0059.PDF\"}]\n\nI found the Bazzurro and Cornell (1999) paper on \"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard\". Based on the search results, here's what I can tell you about the notation and concepts in this foundational work:\n\nThe paper discusses how probabilistic seismic hazard analysis (PSHA) integrates over all potential earthquake occurrences and ground motions to estimate the mean frequency of exceedance of spectral acceleration. The paper presents methods to display the relative contributions to hazard from the range of values of magnitude (M), distance (R), and epsilon (ε), where epsilon represents the number of standard deviations from the median ground motion as predicted by an attenuation equation.\n\nEpsilon is defined as the number of (logarithmic) standard deviations by which the (logarithmic) ground motion deviates from the median value predicted by an attenuation equation.\n\nThe disaggregation of hazard and any derived quantity (e.g., conditional probability density functions of M, R, and ε, or their summary statistics, such as means and modes) are defined for a given combination of parameters.\n\nRegarding the subscript ordering you mentioned, the paper deals with contributions of magnitude and distance conditional on the exceedance of different levels of peak ground acceleration (PGA) and different spectral accelerations, and discusses methods that disaggregate the hazard conditional on exceeding the target value of the ground-motion parameter but accumulate the contributions in M, R, and ε bins.\n\nThe paper is published in the Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, volume 89, pages 501-520, April 1999.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bf7093-818d-4370-9615-a0aeec356215","uuid":"bb6db5b8-aedf-4455-b3d9-f919e0c08315","timestamp":"2026-03-30T01:36:37.254Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Bazzurro Cornell 1999 \"disaggregation of seismic hazard\" notation subscript ordering magnitude distance epsilon conditional probability","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01PuAmWUnqiJ1KCGapijqB9A","content":[{"title":"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard","url":"http://sismologia.ist.utl.pt/~sismologia.daemon/files/Bazurro_and_Cornell_Disaggregation_of_Seismic_Hazard.pdf"},{"title":"(PDF) Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/245939078_Disaggregation_of_Seismic_Hazard"},{"title":"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard | Southern California Earthquake Center","url":"https://www.scec.org/publication/407"},{"title":"Disaggregation of seismic hazard | Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America | GeoScienceWorld","url":"https://pubs.geoscienceworld.org/ssa/bssa/article-abstract/89/2/501/342641/Disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard"},{"title":"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard | Statewide California Earthquake Center","url":"https://central.scec.org/publication/407"},{"title":"Ground motion scenarios consistent with probabilistic seismic hazard disaggregation analysis. Application to Mainland Portugal | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-008-9088-z"},{"title":"Disaggregation of seismic hazard (1999) | Paolo Bazzurro | 518 Citations","url":"https://typeset.io/papers/disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard-3e6zx96xtw"},{"title":"Researchgate","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Paolo_Bazzurro/publication/245939078_Disaggregation_of_Seismic_Hazard/links/54202dc10cf203f155c2afe3/Disaggregation-of-Seismic-Hazard.pdf"},{"title":"Disaggregation of seismic hazard | Semantic Scholar","url":"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard-Bazzurro-Cornell/907cde3d1c6f6ca8b6e79ded693847be6abe6866"},{"title":"Disaggregation of seismic hazard according to Chinese ...","url":"https://www.iitk.ac.in/nicee/wcee/article/14_07-0059.PDF"}]},"I found the Bazzurro and Cornell (1999) paper on \"Disaggregation of Seismic Hazard\". Based on the search results, here's what I can tell you about the notation and concepts in this foundational work:\n\nThe paper discusses how probabilistic seismic hazard analysis (PSHA) integrates over all potential earthquake occurrences and ground motions to estimate the mean frequency of exceedance of spectral acceleration. The paper presents methods to display the relative contributions to hazard from the range of values of magnitude (M), distance (R), and epsilon (ε), where epsilon represents the number of standard deviations from the median ground motion as predicted by an attenuation equation.\n\nEpsilon is defined as the number of (logarithmic) standard deviations by which the (logarithmic) ground motion deviates from the median value predicted by an attenuation equation.\n\nThe disaggregation of hazard and any derived quantity (e.g., conditional probability density functions of M, R, and ε, or their summary statistics, such as means and modes) are defined for a given combination of parameters.\n\nRegarding the subscript ordering you mentioned, the paper deals with contributions of magnitude and distance conditional on the exceedance of different levels of peak ground acceleration (PGA) and different spectral accelerations, and discusses methods that disaggregate the hazard conditional on exceeding the target value of the ground-motion parameter but accumulate the contributions in M, R, and ε bins.\n\nThe paper is published in the Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, volume 89, pages 501-520, April 1999."],"durationSeconds":8.023517468000005}}
