{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FpMvxabmsZ2L9vRheyyWZw","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## MCER and ELastic Design Spectra (ASCE) {#sec-mcer}\n2\t\n3\tSeismic demand for all Risk Categories I-IV was derived in accordance with **ASCE/SEI 7-22**---as adopted by **IBC 2021**---using the site-specific uniform-hazard spectrum (UHS) established in the preceding chapter. Chapter 11 of ASCE 7 permits replacement of mapped spectral accelerations by values obtained from a probabilistic hazard analysis coupled with site-response modelling, provided the procedures of §11.4.7 are satisfied [@ASCE722 §11.4.7].\n4\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The reference to ASCE/SEI 7-22 as adopted by IBC 2021 and the claim that Chapter 11 permits replacement of mapped spectral accelerations with site-specific hazard analysis under §11.4.7 are well supported. Published practitioner documentation consistently confirms both the adoption relationship and the §11.4.7 pathway for site-specific procedures [WEB:https://www.iccsafe.org/building-safety-journal/bsj-technical/is-continued-use-of-the-two-period-design-spectrum-in-the-equivalent-lateral-force-procedure-of-seismic-design-permitted-by-asce-7-22/][WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. No contradictions identified.]\n5\t\n6\tChapter §11.4.7 indicates that to perform a site response analysis and a ground motion hazard analysis to determine ground motions for any structure, procedures of sections §21.1 and §21.2 must be applied.\n7\t^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The substance of this statement -- that §11.4.7 requires compliance with §21.1 and §21.2 for site-specific hazard analysis -- is consistent with the hierarchical structure of ASCE 7-22 as described in publicly available documentation [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. The grammatical error present in the source document (\"indicate\" instead of \"indicates\") has been corrected in this paragraph. Confidence is MEDIUM rather than HIGH because exact normative clause wording requires access to the licensed standard document.]\n8\t\n9\tWhen the procedures of either Section §21.1 or §21.2 are used, the design response spectrum shall be determined in accordance with Section §21.3, and the design acceleration parameters shall be determined in accordance with Section §21.4.\n10\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: This paragraph accurately describes the procedural chain required by ASCE 7-22: site-specific ground motions are developed via §21.1 or §21.2, the design response spectrum is derived per §21.3, and design acceleration parameters $S_{DS}$ and $S_{D1}$ are determined per §21.4 [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. This sequence is a direct and accurate reflection of the normative structure of ASCE 7-22 Chapter 21 as confirmed by the USGS design maps service and ICC documentation.]\n11\t\n12\tThe probabilistic spectral response accelerations shall be taken as the spectral response accelerations in the direction of maximum horizontal response represented by a 5% damped acceleration response spectrum that is expected to achieve a 1% probability of collapse within a 50-year period. If the spectral response accelerations predicted by the ground motion models do not represent the maximum response in the horizontal plane, then the response spectral accelerations computed from the hazard analysis shall be scaled by factors to increase the motions to the maximum response. If the ground motion models predict the geometric mean or similar metric of the two horizontal components, then the scale factors shall be 1.2 for periods less than, or equal to, 0.2 s, 1.25 for a period of 1.0 s, and 1.3 for periods greater than or equal to 10 s; linear interpolation between these period-anchored values is specified in §21.1, and is applied in log-period space consistent with standard spectral analysis convention [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html].\n13\t^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The risk-targeted MCER criterion of 1% probability of collapse in 50 years is a foundational element of the risk-targeted ground motion framework introduced in ASCE 7-10 and retained in ASCE 7-22 [WEB:https://www.asce.org/communities/institutes-and-technical-groups/structural-engineering-institute/news/asce-7-22-ground-motions---a-rational-approach-for-structural-engineers]. The 5% critical damping assumption is standard across building codes. The scale factors 1.2, 1.25, and 1.3 at period breakpoints 0.2 s, 1.0 s, and 10 s are confirmed in the USGS ASCE 7-22 web service metadata [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]; they are based on empirical RotD100/RotD50 ratios from Shahi and Baker (2014). The standard text specifies linear interpolation between anchored values; practitioners and the USGS API apply this in log-period space. The previous review sessions noted the omission of the interpolation specification; this enhanced paragraph adds that clarification. Confidence is MEDIUM because the normative §21.1 interpolation language requires the full licensed standard text for exact confirmation.]\n14\t\n15\tConsistent with §21.3, the **design-level spectral accelerations** are obtained by scaling the MCER ordinates by the factor $2/3$ [@ASCE722 Eq. 21.3-1]:\n16\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The factor-of-two-thirds relationship $S_a = \\frac{2}{3}S_{aM}$ is a foundational result in ASCE 7-22 §21.3, consistently confirmed by multiple authoritative practitioner sources [WEB:https://www.iccsafe.org/building-safety-journal/bsj-technical/is-continued-use-of-the-two-period-design-spectrum-in-the-equivalent-lateral-force-procedure-of-seismic-design-permitted-by-asce-7-22/][WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. No contradictions were identified.]\n17\t\n18\t$$\n19\tS_{a}=\\frac{2}{3}S_{aM}\n20\t$$\n21\t\n22\tWhen a site-specific procedure is used to determine the design ground motion, the design acceleration parameters are calculated as [@ASCE722 §21.4]:\n23\t^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The formulas for $S_{DS}$ (0.9 times the maximum $S_a$ over 0.2-5 s) and $S_{D1}$ (incorporating a velocity-dependent integration limit $T^*$ with a threshold of 442 m/s, approximately 1450 ft/s) are broadly consistent with the §21.4 provisions. The velocity threshold of 442 m/s (1450 ft/s) distinguishing a 2 s upper limit from a 5 s upper limit in the $S_{D1}$ integration is confirmed by ICC documentation [WEB:https://www.iccsafe.org/building-safety-journal/bsj-technical/is-continued-use-of-the-two-period-design-spectrum-in-the-equivalent-lateral-force-procedure-of-seismic-design-permitted-by-asce-7-22/]. The non-English word \"con\" present in the source document equation has been corrected to \"where\". The 0.9 factors cannot be fully confirmed from open-access normative sources alone, sustaining MEDIUM confidence.]\n24\t\n25\t$$\n26\tS_{DS} = 0.9 \\max_{T\\,\\in\\,[0.2,\\,5\\,\\text{s}]} S_a(T)\n27\t$$ $$\n28\tS_{D1} = \\max\\!\\left(0.9\\max_{T\\,\\in\\,[1,\\,T^*]\\,\\text{s}} \\left[T \\cdot S_a(T)\\right],\\quad\n29\tS_a(1\\,\\text{s})\\right)\n30\t\\quad \\text{where} \\quad\n31\tT^* = \\begin{cases} 2\\,\\text{s} & \\bar{v}_s > 442\\,\\text{m/s} \\\\ 5\\,\\text{s} & \\bar{v}_s \\leq\n32\t442\\,\\text{m/s} \\end{cases}\n33\t$$\n34\t\n35\tThe spectral response acceleration parameter at short periods ($S_{MS}$) and the spectral response acceleration parameter at a period of 1 s ($S_{M1}$) correspond to the MCER-level demands and are recovered from the design-level parameters by inverting the two-thirds scaling relationship:\n36\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The source document contained a critical transcription error: the second equation was written as $S_{M1} = 1.5\\,S_{M1}$, a trivially circular identity that conveys no information. The correct expression $S_{M1} = 1.5\\,S_{D1}$ follows directly from $S_{D1} = \\frac{2}{3}\\,S_{M1}$, and is confirmed by the USGS ASCE 7-22 web service, which explicitly lists \"SM1 = 1.5 x SD1\" in its response parameter definitions [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. Similarly, $S_{MS} = 1.5\\,S_{DS}$ is the algebraic inverse of $S_{DS} = \\frac{2}{3}\\,S_{MS}$, also confirmed by the same source. Both corrected formulas are algebraically consistent with the fundamental two-thirds relationship between MCER and design-level parameters.]\n37\t\n38\t$$\n39\tS_{MS}= 1.5\\,S_{DS}, \\qquad\n40\tS_{M1}=1.5\\,S_{D1}\n41\t$$\n42\t\n43\tThree international frameworks govern the seismic design of tailings storage facilities (TSFs): the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management [@gistm2020], the Canadian Dam Association guidelines [@cda2021], and the Australian National Committee on Large Dams [@ancold2019]. Each framework defines performance objectives and design earthquake criteria on the basis of two factors: the consequence classification of the facility and its lifecycle phase. Consequence classification reflects the potential severity of a dam failure, assessed through population at risk (PAR), anticipated loss of life, and the magnitude of environmental and socio-economic impacts. All three standards define consequence classes ranging from Low to Extreme, with progressively more stringent seismic criteria as the consequence class increases. The classification criteria and corresponding design earthquake annual exceedance probabilities (AEPs) for each standard are tabulated in Appendix [-@sec-criteria_appendix] (Tables [-@tbl-gistm], [-@tbl-cda], and [-@tbl-ancold]).\n44\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The existence and general scope of all three named frameworks are confirmed by publicly available documentation. GISTM was published in August 2020 by UNEP, PRI, and ICMM and establishes consequence-classification-based AEP criteria [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/global-industry-standard/]. The CDA tailings dam technical bulletin (2021) is a published CDA guidance document [WEB:https://cda.ca/publications/technical-documents-home/tailings-dam-breach-analysis-technical-bulletin-2021]. The ANCOLD 2019 revision to the Guidelines on Tailings Dams is a published guideline [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/]. The characterisation of the consequence classification system and its role in determining seismic design criteria is consistent with all three sources. The claim is well-supported.]\n45\t\n46\tFor a given consequence class, the applicable design earthquake also varies by lifecycle phase. Each phase of a TSF's life -- construction, operation, closure, and post-closure -- presents distinct engineering conditions and risk profiles. The design ground motions presented in this chapter are organized into three stages corresponding to the principal design earthquake levels: operational (OBE), closure (SEE), and post-closure (PCE). The assignment of AEPs to each stage differs between standards and is most differentiated by consequence class during operations, where AEPs range from 1/100 to 1/10,000 depending on standard and consequence class (Tables [-@tbl-gistm], [-@tbl-cda], [-@tbl-ancold]). During post-closure, this differentiation collapses: all three frameworks require convergence to the most stringent criterion -- the Maximum Credible Earthquake (MCE) or the 1/10,000-year event -- irrespective of the original consequence class.\n47\t^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The lifecycle-phase structure (construction, operation, closure, post-closure) and the use of OBE, SEE, and PCE as design levels are consistent with established practice across GISTM, CDA, and ANCOLD [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. The stated operational AEP range (1/100 to 1/10,000) and the convergence of all frameworks to MCE or 1/10,000 AEP at post-closure are broadly consistent with published summaries of these standards. Full verification of the specific AEP values tabulated in the appendix requires access to the primary standard documents, which are not freely available in complete normative form.]\n48\t\n49\tThe approaches differ in structure. Under GISTM [-@gistm2020], a single earthquake design ground motion (EDGM) is specified for each consequence class and phase; the MCE must also be evaluated and governs if it produces higher demand than the probabilistic criterion. Importantly, GISTM applies the same AEPs during operations and active closure, with a step increase to a uniform 1/10,000 AEP for all consequence classes only at passive closure (Table [-@tbl-gistm]). CDA [-@cda2021] employs the Safety Evaluation Earthquake (SEE) as the design basis, with the MCE applied as an upper bound for high-consequence facilities; AEPs are progressively tightened from operations through closure, and risk-based approaches may be permitted for lower consequence classes during transitional closure phases, provided that post-closure requirements are ultimately satisfied (Table [-@tbl-cda]). ANCOLD [-@ancold2019] applies a dual-level methodology, assigning a uniform Operating Basis Earthquake (OBE) of approximately 1/500 AEP across all consequence categories during operations, with a Safety Evaluation Earthquake (SEE) that varies from 1/1,000 to 1/10,000 depending on consequence class; the most severe criteria (MCE or 1/10,000-year event, 85th percentile) are mandated for Extreme-class facilities and required for all dams prior to abandonment (Table [-@tbl-ancold]).\n50\t^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The qualitative structural differences among the three frameworks (GISTM single-EDGM approach, CDA SEE-based design with progressive tightening, ANCOLD dual-level OBE/SEE methodology) are consistent with summaries available in the open literature [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. The ANCOLD dual-level framework with an approximately 1/500 AEP OBE and a consequence-class-dependent SEE, and the ANCOLD requirement for Extreme-class and pre-abandonment facilities to be assessed at MCE or 1/10,000 AEP (85th percentile), are consistent with the 2019 ANCOLD guidelines addendum [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/]. Specific AEP tables and thresholds for GISTM and CDA require verification against the full primary source documents.]\n51\t\n52\t### Construction\n53\t\n54\tTailing deposits are frequently constructed in stages, with embankments raised incrementally. Seismic stability during construction is a critical consideration, as the dam may be at partial height or incomplete strength. Even with a low probability of major earthquake occurrence during the relatively brief construction phase, the potential consequences of a moderate event on an incomplete dam can be severe. Accordingly, design requirements stipulate that temporary factors of safety under seismic loading must be satisfied at all construction stages. Initial and intermediate dam stages are analyzed for at least OBE-level events; for facilities with high consequence classifications or in high-seismicity regions, SEE criteria may also be applied. Temporary material properties and geometries are used in analyses, with design iterations as the dam evolves. The CDA Technical Bulletin: Application of Dam Safety Guidelines to Mining Dams [@cda2014] recommends explicit consideration of seismic hazards at every project phase, supported by independent review at critical milestones [WEB:https://cda.ca/publications/cda-guidance-documents]. Construction methods and schedules are adjusted as needed to maintain seismic safety, including limiting impounded water volumes or avoiding vulnerable states during periods of heightened seismic risk.\n55\t^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The general engineering principles stated in this paragraph -- incremental staged construction, application of OBE at a minimum with SEE for higher-consequence facilities, use of temporary material properties, and the recommendation for phase-by-phase seismic review -- are consistent with standard geotechnical engineering practice for TSF design [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. The source documents cited a \"CDA mining dams bulletin, 2014\"; follow-up research confirmed that the CDA Technical Bulletin: Application of Dam Safety Guidelines to Mining Dams (2014) is a real and distinct publication from the CDA 2021 Tailings Dam Breach Analysis bulletin, listed separately in the CDA guidance documents [WEB:https://cda.ca/publications/cda-guidance-documents]. The citation has been restored with appropriate specificity. The substantive engineering claims are well grounded in standard practice.]\n56\t\n57\t### Operation (Active Life)\n58\t\n59\tDuring operations, when the facility contains large volumes of tailings and water, the risk profile is at its highest and the design earthquake criteria are most differentiated by consequence class. Under GISTM, operational AEPs range from 1/200 for Low-consequence facilities to 1/10,000 for Extreme (Table [-@tbl-gistm]); CDA spans 1/100 to 1/10,000 (Table [-@tbl-cda]); and ANCOLD assigns a uniform OBE at 1/500 AEP with an SEE that varies from 1/1,000 to MCE or 1/10,000 by consequence category (Table [-@tbl-ancold]). The dam must safely withstand the OBE without operational interruption and must not release tailings under the SEE. Water management is integral -- adequate freeboard must be maintained to accommodate seismic settlement, wave run-up, or ground deformations. For high or extreme consequence dams, design includes provision for concurrent storm and seismic loading, in line with ANCOLD and international practice. Operational seismic readiness is maintained through regular surveillance (crack/deformation monitoring) and emergency response planning. Engineering features -- such as buttresses, drainage to control pore pressures, and control of loose, saturated zones -- can enhance seismic performance. If an OBE is exceeded, post-event inspection and prompt repairs are required to restore the safety margin.\n60\t^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The claim that operational risk is highest when a TSF contains large volumes of tailings and water, and the general engineering requirements for freeboard, concurrent loading, and post-event inspection, are consistent with published GISTM commentary and TSF design practice [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. The specific AEP values cited (GISTM 1/200 to 1/10,000; CDA 1/100 to 1/10,000; ANCOLD OBE 1/500, SEE 1/1,000 to MCE/1/10,000) are consistent with values reported in open-source summaries but require verification against the primary normative documents. The source documents included a specific claim that concurrent storm loading corresponds to a 0.1% AEP (1/1,000) storm pond criterion; this specific threshold was removed from the enhanced paragraph because it could not be independently confirmed from publicly available ANCOLD documentation. The general principle of concurrent storm-seismic design is retained.]\n61\t\n62\t### Closure and Post-Closure\n63\t\n64\tAfter the cessation of operations, TSFs are expected to remain physically stable for the indefinite future, in the absence of active management. Design for closure and post-closure is based on the principle that the probability of a rare seismic event approaches certainty over an infinite time horizon. The three frameworks handle the transition differently. Under GISTM, the same consequence-dependent AEPs that govern operations continue through active closure; only at passive closure does a uniform 1/10,000 AEP apply to all consequence classes (Table [-@tbl-gistm]). CDA progressively tightens criteria: facilities classified as High or above must already meet 1/10,000 AEP at the closure stage, while lower classes transition from their operational levels toward more conservative values; all facilities must converge to 1/10,000 AEP or MCE before abandonment (Table [-@tbl-cda]). ANCOLD mandates reassessment of all facilities under Extreme-class criteria (MCE or 1/10,000 AEP, 85th percentile) prior to abandonment (Table [-@tbl-ancold]).\n65\t^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The principle that post-closure design must account for certainty of rare events over an infinite horizon, and the convergence of all frameworks to the most stringent AEP criterion at abandonment, are consistent with GISTM and ANCOLD frameworks [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. The ANCOLD requirement for all facilities to be assessed at MCE or 1/10,000 AEP (85th percentile) prior to abandonment is consistent with the 2019 ANCOLD guidelines [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/]. The GISTM active-to-passive closure transition and the CDA progressive-tightening structure are consistent with publicly available framework descriptions. Specific clause-level thresholds require primary source verification.]\n66\t\n67\tClosure design is typically more stringent than operational design for lower consequence facilities. Rehabilitation measures (flattened slopes, reinforcements, erosion protection) may be implemented to achieve maintenance-free performance. Permanent spillways are designed for probable maximum floods, eliminating reliance on operational water management. Post-closure stability analyses must explicitly account for the degradation of temporary features. Components not expected to persist (e.g., geomembranes) are excluded from the long-term stability evaluation; the residual embankment must independently resist seismic loading. Long-term factors of safety are typically higher, and materials (cemented tailings, stabilizing berms) may be specified to enhance seismic resilience.\n68\t^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The engineering principles described -- exclusion of temporary components from long-term stability analyses, use of permanent spillways sized for probable maximum floods, higher long-term factors of safety, and rehabilitation to achieve maintenance-free performance -- reflect standard geotechnical engineering practice for TSF closure design, consistent with GISTM requirements and international dam engineering practice [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. The claim that closure design is more stringent than operational design for lower consequence facilities follows logically from the convergence of all frameworks to the most stringent criterion at abandonment. No quantitative claims requiring independent verification beyond those addressed in the preceding paragraphs are present.]\n69\t\n70\t---\n71\treference-location: section\n72\tlink-citations: true                   # make in-text cites clickable\n73\t---\n74\t\n75\t\n76\tProbabilistic seismic hazard analysis (PSHA) aggregates the contributions of all plausible earthquake scenarios, obscuring the specific combinations of magnitude ($M$), source-to-site distance ($R$), and ground-motion residual ($\\varepsilon$) that control site hazard. **Hazard disaggregation** quantifies the relative contribution of each scenario class---binned by magnitude $m_k$, distance $r_j$, and residual $\\varepsilon_\\ell$---to the annual frequency of exceedance at a specified ground-motion threshold $i^*$ [@Bazzurro1999].\n77\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The description of PSHA disaggregation as a method for quantifying the contributions of scenarios binned by magnitude, distance, and epsilon to the hazard at a specified ground-motion threshold is an accurate characterisation of the methodology introduced by Bazzurro and Cornell (1999), published in the Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, vol. 89, pp. 501-520 [DOI:10.1785/BSSA0890020501][WEB:https://pubs.geoscienceworld.org/ssa/bssa/article-abstract/89/2/501/342641/Disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard]. The notation ($M$, $R$, $\\varepsilon$) and the concept of binning by these three parameters are standard in the disaggregation literature.]\n78\t\n79\tDisaggregation is performed at a target hazard level, defined by an annual exceedance probability or return period. This produces conditional probability distributions over scenario bins and identifies controlling scenario parameters responsible for site hazard [@Kramer1996]. The ground-motion exceedance probability and total annual exceedance rate are defined in the hazard analysis (see [@eq-hazard-integral], [@eq-epsilon-star]). The joint exceedance rate for scenario $(m_k, r_j, \\varepsilon_\\ell)$ from source $s$ is given in [@eq-disagg-rate], where $\\phi$ is the standard normal probability density function and $\\mathbf 1_{{\\cdot}}$ is the indicator function. Integration over $\\varepsilon$ yields the two-dimensional ($M$-$R$) formulation. Partitioning parameter space into magnitude bins $m_k$, distance bins $r_j$, and residual bins $\\varepsilon_\\ell$, the contribution from bin $(k,j,\\ell)$ is in [@eq-disagg-bin].\n80\t^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The source documents cited this paragraph as [@Kramer1997]; however, the textbook \"Geotechnical Earthquake Engineering\" by Steven L. Kramer was published by Prentice-Hall in 1996, not 1997 [WEB:https://www.scirp.org/reference/referencespapers?referenceid=576202][WEB:https://archive.org/details/geotechnicaleart0000kram]. The citation key has been corrected to [@Kramer1996] in this enhanced session output. The Kramer (1996) textbook is an appropriate citation for the concept of conditional probability distributions identifying controlling scenario parameters, as it covers disaggregation methodology at a graduate level. The mathematical framework that follows, using the standard normal PDF $\\phi$ and indicator function $\\mathbf{1}_{(\\cdot)}$ to express the joint exceedance rate, is standard in the disaggregation literature [DOI:10.1785/BSSA0890020501]. Confidence is MEDIUM because the corrected citation year has not been verified against the physical book title page, and the description of the following equations refers to cross-references not reproduced here.]\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bf7093-818d-4370-9615-a0aeec356215","uuid":"37fbae44-acd5-43fb-9e7f-013240022976","timestamp":"2026-03-30T01:51:04.586Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/review/psha/sessions/run-20260329_222746/psha_enh1.enhance.md","content":"## MCER and ELastic Design Spectra (ASCE) {#sec-mcer}\n\nSeismic demand for all Risk Categories I-IV was derived in accordance with **ASCE/SEI 7-22**---as adopted by **IBC 2021**---using the site-specific uniform-hazard spectrum (UHS) established in the preceding chapter. Chapter 11 of ASCE 7 permits replacement of mapped spectral accelerations by values obtained from a probabilistic hazard analysis coupled with site-response modelling, provided the procedures of §11.4.7 are satisfied [@ASCE722 §11.4.7].\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The reference to ASCE/SEI 7-22 as adopted by IBC 2021 and the claim that Chapter 11 permits replacement of mapped spectral accelerations with site-specific hazard analysis under §11.4.7 are well supported. Published practitioner documentation consistently confirms both the adoption relationship and the §11.4.7 pathway for site-specific procedures [WEB:https://www.iccsafe.org/building-safety-journal/bsj-technical/is-continued-use-of-the-two-period-design-spectrum-in-the-equivalent-lateral-force-procedure-of-seismic-design-permitted-by-asce-7-22/][WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. No contradictions identified.]\n\nChapter §11.4.7 indicates that to perform a site response analysis and a ground motion hazard analysis to determine ground motions for any structure, procedures of sections §21.1 and §21.2 must be applied.\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The substance of this statement -- that §11.4.7 requires compliance with §21.1 and §21.2 for site-specific hazard analysis -- is consistent with the hierarchical structure of ASCE 7-22 as described in publicly available documentation [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. The grammatical error present in the source document (\"indicate\" instead of \"indicates\") has been corrected in this paragraph. Confidence is MEDIUM rather than HIGH because exact normative clause wording requires access to the licensed standard document.]\n\nWhen the procedures of either Section §21.1 or §21.2 are used, the design response spectrum shall be determined in accordance with Section §21.3, and the design acceleration parameters shall be determined in accordance with Section §21.4.\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: This paragraph accurately describes the procedural chain required by ASCE 7-22: site-specific ground motions are developed via §21.1 or §21.2, the design response spectrum is derived per §21.3, and design acceleration parameters $S_{DS}$ and $S_{D1}$ are determined per §21.4 [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. This sequence is a direct and accurate reflection of the normative structure of ASCE 7-22 Chapter 21 as confirmed by the USGS design maps service and ICC documentation.]\n\nThe probabilistic spectral response accelerations shall be taken as the spectral response accelerations in the direction of maximum horizontal response represented by a 5% damped acceleration response spectrum that is expected to achieve a 1% probability of collapse within a 50-year period. If the spectral response accelerations predicted by the ground motion models do not represent the maximum response in the horizontal plane, then the response spectral accelerations computed from the hazard analysis shall be scaled by factors to increase the motions to the maximum response. If the ground motion models predict the geometric mean or similar metric of the two horizontal components, then the scale factors shall be 1.2 for periods less than, or equal to, 0.2 s, 1.25 for a period of 1.0 s, and 1.3 for periods greater than or equal to 10 s; linear interpolation between these period-anchored values is specified in §21.1, and is applied in log-period space consistent with standard spectral analysis convention [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html].\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The risk-targeted MCER criterion of 1% probability of collapse in 50 years is a foundational element of the risk-targeted ground motion framework introduced in ASCE 7-10 and retained in ASCE 7-22 [WEB:https://www.asce.org/communities/institutes-and-technical-groups/structural-engineering-institute/news/asce-7-22-ground-motions---a-rational-approach-for-structural-engineers]. The 5% critical damping assumption is standard across building codes. The scale factors 1.2, 1.25, and 1.3 at period breakpoints 0.2 s, 1.0 s, and 10 s are confirmed in the USGS ASCE 7-22 web service metadata [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]; they are based on empirical RotD100/RotD50 ratios from Shahi and Baker (2014). The standard text specifies linear interpolation between anchored values; practitioners and the USGS API apply this in log-period space. The previous review sessions noted the omission of the interpolation specification; this enhanced paragraph adds that clarification. Confidence is MEDIUM because the normative §21.1 interpolation language requires the full licensed standard text for exact confirmation.]\n\nConsistent with §21.3, the **design-level spectral accelerations** are obtained by scaling the MCER ordinates by the factor $2/3$ [@ASCE722 Eq. 21.3-1]:\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The factor-of-two-thirds relationship $S_a = \\frac{2}{3}S_{aM}$ is a foundational result in ASCE 7-22 §21.3, consistently confirmed by multiple authoritative practitioner sources [WEB:https://www.iccsafe.org/building-safety-journal/bsj-technical/is-continued-use-of-the-two-period-design-spectrum-in-the-equivalent-lateral-force-procedure-of-seismic-design-permitted-by-asce-7-22/][WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. No contradictions were identified.]\n\n$$\nS_{a}=\\frac{2}{3}S_{aM}\n$$\n\nWhen a site-specific procedure is used to determine the design ground motion, the design acceleration parameters are calculated as [@ASCE722 §21.4]:\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The formulas for $S_{DS}$ (0.9 times the maximum $S_a$ over 0.2-5 s) and $S_{D1}$ (incorporating a velocity-dependent integration limit $T^*$ with a threshold of 442 m/s, approximately 1450 ft/s) are broadly consistent with the §21.4 provisions. The velocity threshold of 442 m/s (1450 ft/s) distinguishing a 2 s upper limit from a 5 s upper limit in the $S_{D1}$ integration is confirmed by ICC documentation [WEB:https://www.iccsafe.org/building-safety-journal/bsj-technical/is-continued-use-of-the-two-period-design-spectrum-in-the-equivalent-lateral-force-procedure-of-seismic-design-permitted-by-asce-7-22/]. The non-English word \"con\" present in the source document equation has been corrected to \"where\". The 0.9 factors cannot be fully confirmed from open-access normative sources alone, sustaining MEDIUM confidence.]\n\n$$\nS_{DS} = 0.9 \\max_{T\\,\\in\\,[0.2,\\,5\\,\\text{s}]} S_a(T)\n$$ $$\nS_{D1} = \\max\\!\\left(0.9\\max_{T\\,\\in\\,[1,\\,T^*]\\,\\text{s}} \\left[T \\cdot S_a(T)\\right],\\quad\nS_a(1\\,\\text{s})\\right)\n\\quad \\text{where} \\quad\nT^* = \\begin{cases} 2\\,\\text{s} & \\bar{v}_s > 442\\,\\text{m/s} \\\\ 5\\,\\text{s} & \\bar{v}_s \\leq\n442\\,\\text{m/s} \\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThe spectral response acceleration parameter at short periods ($S_{MS}$) and the spectral response acceleration parameter at a period of 1 s ($S_{M1}$) correspond to the MCER-level demands and are recovered from the design-level parameters by inverting the two-thirds scaling relationship:\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The source document contained a critical transcription error: the second equation was written as $S_{M1} = 1.5\\,S_{M1}$, a trivially circular identity that conveys no information. The correct expression $S_{M1} = 1.5\\,S_{D1}$ follows directly from $S_{D1} = \\frac{2}{3}\\,S_{M1}$, and is confirmed by the USGS ASCE 7-22 web service, which explicitly lists \"SM1 = 1.5 x SD1\" in its response parameter definitions [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. Similarly, $S_{MS} = 1.5\\,S_{DS}$ is the algebraic inverse of $S_{DS} = \\frac{2}{3}\\,S_{MS}$, also confirmed by the same source. Both corrected formulas are algebraically consistent with the fundamental two-thirds relationship between MCER and design-level parameters.]\n\n$$\nS_{MS}= 1.5\\,S_{DS}, \\qquad\nS_{M1}=1.5\\,S_{D1}\n$$\n\nThree international frameworks govern the seismic design of tailings storage facilities (TSFs): the Global Industry Standard on Tailings Management [@gistm2020], the Canadian Dam Association guidelines [@cda2021], and the Australian National Committee on Large Dams [@ancold2019]. Each framework defines performance objectives and design earthquake criteria on the basis of two factors: the consequence classification of the facility and its lifecycle phase. Consequence classification reflects the potential severity of a dam failure, assessed through population at risk (PAR), anticipated loss of life, and the magnitude of environmental and socio-economic impacts. All three standards define consequence classes ranging from Low to Extreme, with progressively more stringent seismic criteria as the consequence class increases. The classification criteria and corresponding design earthquake annual exceedance probabilities (AEPs) for each standard are tabulated in Appendix [-@sec-criteria_appendix] (Tables [-@tbl-gistm], [-@tbl-cda], and [-@tbl-ancold]).\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The existence and general scope of all three named frameworks are confirmed by publicly available documentation. GISTM was published in August 2020 by UNEP, PRI, and ICMM and establishes consequence-classification-based AEP criteria [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/global-industry-standard/]. The CDA tailings dam technical bulletin (2021) is a published CDA guidance document [WEB:https://cda.ca/publications/technical-documents-home/tailings-dam-breach-analysis-technical-bulletin-2021]. The ANCOLD 2019 revision to the Guidelines on Tailings Dams is a published guideline [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/]. The characterisation of the consequence classification system and its role in determining seismic design criteria is consistent with all three sources. The claim is well-supported.]\n\nFor a given consequence class, the applicable design earthquake also varies by lifecycle phase. Each phase of a TSF's life -- construction, operation, closure, and post-closure -- presents distinct engineering conditions and risk profiles. The design ground motions presented in this chapter are organized into three stages corresponding to the principal design earthquake levels: operational (OBE), closure (SEE), and post-closure (PCE). The assignment of AEPs to each stage differs between standards and is most differentiated by consequence class during operations, where AEPs range from 1/100 to 1/10,000 depending on standard and consequence class (Tables [-@tbl-gistm], [-@tbl-cda], [-@tbl-ancold]). During post-closure, this differentiation collapses: all three frameworks require convergence to the most stringent criterion -- the Maximum Credible Earthquake (MCE) or the 1/10,000-year event -- irrespective of the original consequence class.\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The lifecycle-phase structure (construction, operation, closure, post-closure) and the use of OBE, SEE, and PCE as design levels are consistent with established practice across GISTM, CDA, and ANCOLD [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. The stated operational AEP range (1/100 to 1/10,000) and the convergence of all frameworks to MCE or 1/10,000 AEP at post-closure are broadly consistent with published summaries of these standards. Full verification of the specific AEP values tabulated in the appendix requires access to the primary standard documents, which are not freely available in complete normative form.]\n\nThe approaches differ in structure. Under GISTM [-@gistm2020], a single earthquake design ground motion (EDGM) is specified for each consequence class and phase; the MCE must also be evaluated and governs if it produces higher demand than the probabilistic criterion. Importantly, GISTM applies the same AEPs during operations and active closure, with a step increase to a uniform 1/10,000 AEP for all consequence classes only at passive closure (Table [-@tbl-gistm]). CDA [-@cda2021] employs the Safety Evaluation Earthquake (SEE) as the design basis, with the MCE applied as an upper bound for high-consequence facilities; AEPs are progressively tightened from operations through closure, and risk-based approaches may be permitted for lower consequence classes during transitional closure phases, provided that post-closure requirements are ultimately satisfied (Table [-@tbl-cda]). ANCOLD [-@ancold2019] applies a dual-level methodology, assigning a uniform Operating Basis Earthquake (OBE) of approximately 1/500 AEP across all consequence categories during operations, with a Safety Evaluation Earthquake (SEE) that varies from 1/1,000 to 1/10,000 depending on consequence class; the most severe criteria (MCE or 1/10,000-year event, 85th percentile) are mandated for Extreme-class facilities and required for all dams prior to abandonment (Table [-@tbl-ancold]).\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The qualitative structural differences among the three frameworks (GISTM single-EDGM approach, CDA SEE-based design with progressive tightening, ANCOLD dual-level OBE/SEE methodology) are consistent with summaries available in the open literature [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. The ANCOLD dual-level framework with an approximately 1/500 AEP OBE and a consequence-class-dependent SEE, and the ANCOLD requirement for Extreme-class and pre-abandonment facilities to be assessed at MCE or 1/10,000 AEP (85th percentile), are consistent with the 2019 ANCOLD guidelines addendum [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/]. Specific AEP tables and thresholds for GISTM and CDA require verification against the full primary source documents.]\n\n### Construction\n\nTailing deposits are frequently constructed in stages, with embankments raised incrementally. Seismic stability during construction is a critical consideration, as the dam may be at partial height or incomplete strength. Even with a low probability of major earthquake occurrence during the relatively brief construction phase, the potential consequences of a moderate event on an incomplete dam can be severe. Accordingly, design requirements stipulate that temporary factors of safety under seismic loading must be satisfied at all construction stages. Initial and intermediate dam stages are analyzed for at least OBE-level events; for facilities with high consequence classifications or in high-seismicity regions, SEE criteria may also be applied. Temporary material properties and geometries are used in analyses, with design iterations as the dam evolves. The CDA Technical Bulletin: Application of Dam Safety Guidelines to Mining Dams [@cda2014] recommends explicit consideration of seismic hazards at every project phase, supported by independent review at critical milestones [WEB:https://cda.ca/publications/cda-guidance-documents]. Construction methods and schedules are adjusted as needed to maintain seismic safety, including limiting impounded water volumes or avoiding vulnerable states during periods of heightened seismic risk.\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The general engineering principles stated in this paragraph -- incremental staged construction, application of OBE at a minimum with SEE for higher-consequence facilities, use of temporary material properties, and the recommendation for phase-by-phase seismic review -- are consistent with standard geotechnical engineering practice for TSF design [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. The source documents cited a \"CDA mining dams bulletin, 2014\"; follow-up research confirmed that the CDA Technical Bulletin: Application of Dam Safety Guidelines to Mining Dams (2014) is a real and distinct publication from the CDA 2021 Tailings Dam Breach Analysis bulletin, listed separately in the CDA guidance documents [WEB:https://cda.ca/publications/cda-guidance-documents]. The citation has been restored with appropriate specificity. The substantive engineering claims are well grounded in standard practice.]\n\n### Operation (Active Life)\n\nDuring operations, when the facility contains large volumes of tailings and water, the risk profile is at its highest and the design earthquake criteria are most differentiated by consequence class. Under GISTM, operational AEPs range from 1/200 for Low-consequence facilities to 1/10,000 for Extreme (Table [-@tbl-gistm]); CDA spans 1/100 to 1/10,000 (Table [-@tbl-cda]); and ANCOLD assigns a uniform OBE at 1/500 AEP with an SEE that varies from 1/1,000 to MCE or 1/10,000 by consequence category (Table [-@tbl-ancold]). The dam must safely withstand the OBE without operational interruption and must not release tailings under the SEE. Water management is integral -- adequate freeboard must be maintained to accommodate seismic settlement, wave run-up, or ground deformations. For high or extreme consequence dams, design includes provision for concurrent storm and seismic loading, in line with ANCOLD and international practice. Operational seismic readiness is maintained through regular surveillance (crack/deformation monitoring) and emergency response planning. Engineering features -- such as buttresses, drainage to control pore pressures, and control of loose, saturated zones -- can enhance seismic performance. If an OBE is exceeded, post-event inspection and prompt repairs are required to restore the safety margin.\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The claim that operational risk is highest when a TSF contains large volumes of tailings and water, and the general engineering requirements for freeboard, concurrent loading, and post-event inspection, are consistent with published GISTM commentary and TSF design practice [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. The specific AEP values cited (GISTM 1/200 to 1/10,000; CDA 1/100 to 1/10,000; ANCOLD OBE 1/500, SEE 1/1,000 to MCE/1/10,000) are consistent with values reported in open-source summaries but require verification against the primary normative documents. The source documents included a specific claim that concurrent storm loading corresponds to a 0.1% AEP (1/1,000) storm pond criterion; this specific threshold was removed from the enhanced paragraph because it could not be independently confirmed from publicly available ANCOLD documentation. The general principle of concurrent storm-seismic design is retained.]\n\n### Closure and Post-Closure\n\nAfter the cessation of operations, TSFs are expected to remain physically stable for the indefinite future, in the absence of active management. Design for closure and post-closure is based on the principle that the probability of a rare seismic event approaches certainty over an infinite time horizon. The three frameworks handle the transition differently. Under GISTM, the same consequence-dependent AEPs that govern operations continue through active closure; only at passive closure does a uniform 1/10,000 AEP apply to all consequence classes (Table [-@tbl-gistm]). CDA progressively tightens criteria: facilities classified as High or above must already meet 1/10,000 AEP at the closure stage, while lower classes transition from their operational levels toward more conservative values; all facilities must converge to 1/10,000 AEP or MCE before abandonment (Table [-@tbl-cda]). ANCOLD mandates reassessment of all facilities under Extreme-class criteria (MCE or 1/10,000 AEP, 85th percentile) prior to abandonment (Table [-@tbl-ancold]).\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The principle that post-closure design must account for certainty of rare events over an infinite horizon, and the convergence of all frameworks to the most stringent AEP criterion at abandonment, are consistent with GISTM and ANCOLD frameworks [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. The ANCOLD requirement for all facilities to be assessed at MCE or 1/10,000 AEP (85th percentile) prior to abandonment is consistent with the 2019 ANCOLD guidelines [WEB:https://ancold.org.au/product/guidelines-on-tailings-dams-planning-design-construction-operation-and-closure-revision-1-july-2019/]. The GISTM active-to-passive closure transition and the CDA progressive-tightening structure are consistent with publicly available framework descriptions. Specific clause-level thresholds require primary source verification.]\n\nClosure design is typically more stringent than operational design for lower consequence facilities. Rehabilitation measures (flattened slopes, reinforcements, erosion protection) may be implemented to achieve maintenance-free performance. Permanent spillways are designed for probable maximum floods, eliminating reliance on operational water management. Post-closure stability analyses must explicitly account for the degradation of temporary features. Components not expected to persist (e.g., geomembranes) are excluded from the long-term stability evaluation; the residual embankment must independently resist seismic loading. Long-term factors of safety are typically higher, and materials (cemented tailings, stabilizing berms) may be specified to enhance seismic resilience.\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The engineering principles described -- exclusion of temporary components from long-term stability analyses, use of permanent spillways sized for probable maximum floods, higher long-term factors of safety, and rehabilitation to achieve maintenance-free performance -- reflect standard geotechnical engineering practice for TSF closure design, consistent with GISTM requirements and international dam engineering practice [WEB:https://globaltailingsreview.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/global-industry-standard-on-tailings-management.pdf]. The claim that closure design is more stringent than operational design for lower consequence facilities follows logically from the convergence of all frameworks to the most stringent criterion at abandonment. No quantitative claims requiring independent verification beyond those addressed in the preceding paragraphs are present.]\n\n---\nreference-location: section\nlink-citations: true                   # make in-text cites clickable\n---\n\n\nProbabilistic seismic hazard analysis (PSHA) aggregates the contributions of all plausible earthquake scenarios, obscuring the specific combinations of magnitude ($M$), source-to-site distance ($R$), and ground-motion residual ($\\varepsilon$) that control site hazard. **Hazard disaggregation** quantifies the relative contribution of each scenario class---binned by magnitude $m_k$, distance $r_j$, and residual $\\varepsilon_\\ell$---to the annual frequency of exceedance at a specified ground-motion threshold $i^*$ [@Bazzurro1999].\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The description of PSHA disaggregation as a method for quantifying the contributions of scenarios binned by magnitude, distance, and epsilon to the hazard at a specified ground-motion threshold is an accurate characterisation of the methodology introduced by Bazzurro and Cornell (1999), published in the Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, vol. 89, pp. 501-520 [DOI:10.1785/BSSA0890020501][WEB:https://pubs.geoscienceworld.org/ssa/bssa/article-abstract/89/2/501/342641/Disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard]. The notation ($M$, $R$, $\\varepsilon$) and the concept of binning by these three parameters are standard in the disaggregation literature.]\n\nDisaggregation is performed at a target hazard level, defined by an annual exceedance probability or return period. This produces conditional probability distributions over scenario bins and identifies controlling scenario parameters responsible for site hazard [@Kramer1996]. The ground-motion exceedance probability and total annual exceedance rate are defined in the hazard analysis (see [@eq-hazard-integral], [@eq-epsilon-star]). The joint exceedance rate for scenario $(m_k, r_j, \\varepsilon_\\ell)$ from source $s$ is given in [@eq-disagg-rate], where $\\phi$ is the standard normal probability density function and $\\mathbf 1_{{\\cdot}}$ is the indicator function. Integration over $\\varepsilon$ yields the two-dimensional ($M$-$R$) formulation. Partitioning parameter space into magnitude bins $m_k$, distance bins $r_j$, and residual bins $\\varepsilon_\\ell$, the contribution from bin $(k,j,\\ell)$ is in [@eq-disagg-bin].\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The source documents cited this paragraph as [@Kramer1997]; however, the textbook \"Geotechnical Earthquake Engineering\" by Steven L. Kramer was published by Prentice-Hall in 1996, not 1997 [WEB:https://www.scirp.org/reference/referencespapers?referenceid=576202][WEB:https://archive.org/details/geotechnicaleart0000kram]. The citation key has been corrected to [@Kramer1996] in this enhanced session output. The Kramer (1996) textbook is an appropriate citation for the concept of conditional probability distributions identifying controlling scenario parameters, as it covers disaggregation methodology at a graduate level. The mathematical framework that follows, using the standard normal PDF $\\phi$ and indicator function $\\mathbf{1}_{(\\cdot)}$ to express the joint exceedance rate, is standard in the disaggregation literature [DOI:10.1785/BSSA0890020501]. Confidence is MEDIUM because the corrected citation year has not been verified against the physical book title page, and the description of the following equations refers to cross-references not reproduced here.]","numLines":80,"startLine":1,"totalLines":258}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01RuuFrm7Ux1y4Z2Sx52KjeX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GFkNXPPt7FA5EwFvbJ53TX","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/review/psha/sessions/run-20260329_222746/psha_enh1.enhance.md","limit":100,"offset":80},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7411,"cache_read_input_tokens":33486,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7411,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":39,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bf7093-818d-4370-9615-a0aeec356215","uuid":"8c5ae874-65ba-4e96-a5d6-9b8d761320ec"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GFkNXPPt7FA5EwFvbJ53TX","type":"tool_result","content":"80\t^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The source documents cited this paragraph as [@Kramer1997]; however, the textbook \"Geotechnical Earthquake Engineering\" by Steven L. Kramer was published by Prentice-Hall in 1996, not 1997 [WEB:https://www.scirp.org/reference/referencespapers?referenceid=576202][WEB:https://archive.org/details/geotechnicaleart0000kram]. The citation key has been corrected to [@Kramer1996] in this enhanced session output. The Kramer (1996) textbook is an appropriate citation for the concept of conditional probability distributions identifying controlling scenario parameters, as it covers disaggregation methodology at a graduate level. The mathematical framework that follows, using the standard normal PDF $\\phi$ and indicator function $\\mathbf{1}_{(\\cdot)}$ to express the joint exceedance rate, is standard in the disaggregation literature [DOI:10.1785/BSSA0890020501]. Confidence is MEDIUM because the corrected citation year has not been verified against the physical book title page, and the description of the following equations refers to cross-references not reproduced here.]\n81\t\n82\t$$\n83\t\\Delta\\lambda_{I}(i^{*},m_k,r_j,\\varepsilon)^{(s)} =\n84\t\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\varepsilon\\ge\\varepsilon^{*}(m_k,r_j)\\}}\n85\t\\,\n86\t\\phi(\\varepsilon)\\,\n87\tf_{M,s}(m_k)\\,\n88\tf_{R|M,s}(r_j|m_k)\\,\n89\t\\nu_{s},\n90\t$${#eq-disagg-rate}\n91\t\n92\t$$\n93\t\\lambda_{k,j,\\ell}(i^{*})=\n94\t\\sum_{s=1}^{N_{S}}\n95\t\\int_{m_k}\n96\t\\int_{r_j}\n97\t\\int_{\\varepsilon_\\ell}\n98\t\\Delta\\lambda_{I}(i^{*},m_k,r_j,\\varepsilon)^{(s)}\n99\td\\varepsilon\\,dr\\,dm\n100\t$${#eq-disagg-bin}\n101\t\n102\tThe conditional probability that exceedance of $i^*$ is produced by bin $(k,j,\\ell)$ is given by [@eq-disagg-prob]. The **modal scenario** is the $(k,j,\\ell)$ bin for which $\\theta_{k,j,\\ell}(i^{*})$ attains its maximum. Hazard disaggregation applies Bayes' theorem to the joint distribution of scenario parameters and ground-motion exceedance as in [@eq-disagg-prob]. The marginal distribution for $\\varepsilon$ quantifies the proportion of exceedance attributable to median or above-median ground-motion residuals. Marginal probabilities are obtained by double summation over the other indices as in [@eq-disagg-marginal-M], [@eq-disagg-marginal-R], [@eq-disagg-marginal-eps]:\n103\t^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The definition of the modal scenario as the bin maximising the conditional probability and the application of Bayes' theorem to obtain the conditional probability distribution are consistent with Bazzurro and Cornell (1999) [DOI:10.1785/BSSA0890020501]. Both source review sessions identified several notation errors in the display math that follows: (1) the subscript notation $\\theta_{j,k,l}$ used magnitude-last ordering and a lowercase \"l\" instead of the proper LaTeX $\\ell$, inconsistent with the $(k,j,\\ell)$ convention (magnitude, distance, residual) established throughout; (2) a comma was missing in $\\lambda_{k,j\\ell}$; (3) the normalization expression was malformed as $\\sum_k\\sum_j\\sum_\\ell P_{k,j,\\ell}([I>i^*]=1$, with unmatched brackets. All three errors have been corrected in [@eq-disagg-prob] and the marginal equations below. Confidence is MEDIUM because the corrected subscript ordering follows conventional practice [DOI:10.1785/BSSA0890020501] but independent normative confirmation requires primary source access.]\n104\t\n105\t$$\n106\tP[M=m_k,R=r_j,\\varepsilon=\\varepsilon_\\ell\\mid I> i^{*}] = \\theta_{k,j,\\ell}=\n107\t\\frac{\\lambda_{k,j,\\ell}(i^{*})}{\\lambda_{I}(i^{*})},\n108\t\\qquad\n109\t\\sum_{k}\\sum_{j}\\sum_{\\ell}\\theta_{k,j,\\ell} = 1\n110\t$${#eq-disagg-prob}\n111\t\n112\t$$\n113\tP\\left[M \\in m_k\\mid I> i^{*}\\right] = \\sum_{j}\\sum_{\\ell}\\theta_{k,j,\\ell}\n114\t$${#eq-disagg-marginal-M}\n115\t$$\n116\tP\\left[R \\in r_j\\mid I> i^{*}\\right] = \\sum_{k}\\sum_{\\ell}\\theta_{k,j,\\ell}\n117\t$${#eq-disagg-marginal-R}\n118\t$$\n119\tP\\left[\\varepsilon \\in \\varepsilon_\\ell\\mid I> i^{*}\\right] = \\sum_{k}\\sum_{j}\\theta_{k,j,\\ell}\n120\t$${#eq-disagg-marginal-eps}\n121\t\n122\tDisaggregation provides a data-driven basis required for rigorous scenario selection in engineering analysis. The *modal scenario* and the highest-probability bins identified through disaggregation correspond to the earthquake characteristics most responsible for exceedance at the design ground-motion level.\n123\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of disaggregation as providing a data-driven basis for scenario selection, and the identification of the modal scenario with the earthquake characteristics most responsible for exceedance, are standard conclusions of the disaggregation methodology well supported in the literature [DOI:10.1785/BSSA0890020501][WEB:https://pubs.geoscienceworld.org/ssa/bssa/article-abstract/89/2/501/342641/Disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard]. This concluding paragraph is conceptually accurate and consistent with the Bazzurro and Cornell (1999) framework.]\n124\t\n125\tProbabilistic Seismic Hazard Analysis (PSHA) is a methodology that quantifies earthquake ground-motion hazard at a site in terms of probabilities, by integrating over all possible earthquakes that could affect the site [@Cornell1968; @McGuire2004]. Unlike deterministic approaches that focus on a single scenario, PSHA considers the full range of magnitudes, locations, and associated ground motions, providing a comprehensive description of the rate at which various levels of shaking may be exceeded. In probabilistic seismic hazard analysis (PSHA), the annual frequency (rate) of exceeding a ground-motion level at a site is obtained by integrating over all possible earthquake scenarios. For a single seismic source $(s)$ with an annual occurrence rate of earthquakes (above a minimum magnitude $M_{\\min}$) denoted $\\nu_{0}^{(s)}$ (where $\\nu_{0}^{(s)} = N(M_{\\min})^{(s)}$), the rate of exceedance of a ground-motion level $i^*$ (e.g., a peak ground acceleration or spectral acceleration) is defined in [@eq-hazard-integral], where $m$ is earthquake magnitude, $\\mathbf r$ is the source-to-site distance, $f_{M,s}(m)$ and $f_{\\mathbf{R}\\mid M,s}(\\mathbf{r}\\mid m)$ are the probability density functions describing the distribution of magnitudes and distances for source $(s)$, and $P\\left[I > i^* \\mid M=m,\\,\\mathbf r\\right]$ is the conditional probability that the ground-motion parameter $I$ exceeds level $i^*$ given an event of magnitude $m$ at distance $\\mathbf r$. $M_{\\max}^{(s)}$ is the maximum considered magnitude for source $(s)$, which is related to the maximum credible earthquake (MCE) of that source.\n126\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The description of PSHA as a methodology integrating over all earthquake scenarios to quantify exceedance rates, attributed to Cornell (1968) and McGuire (2004), is accurate and well established. Cornell (1968) is universally cited as the foundational paper of PSHA, and the Cornell-McGuire procedure is the canonical PSHA framework [WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf][WEB:https://courses.washington.edu/ess502/Cornell_1968.pdf]. The hazard integral formulation with magnitude PDF $f_{M,s}(m)$, distance PDF $f_{\\mathbf{R}|M,s}(\\mathbf{r}|m)$, and conditional exceedance probability $P[I>i^*|m,\\mathbf{r}]$ is the standard form. No technical errors are identified in this introductory paragraph.]\n127\t\n128\t$$\n129\t\\lambda_I(i^*)^{(s)} \\;=\\;\n130\t\\nu_{0}^{(s)}\n131\t\\int_{M_{\\min}}^{M_{\\max}^{(s)}} \\int_{D}\n132\tP\\left[ I > i^* \\,\\big|\\, m, \\mathbf{r} \\right]\\;\n133\tf_{M,s}(m)\\;\n134\tf_{\\mathbf{R}\\mid M,s}(\\mathbf{r}\\mid m)\\;\n135\t\\mathrm d\\mathbf{r}\\,\\mathrm dm\n136\t$${#eq-hazard-integral}\n137\t\n138\tThe probability density functions (PDF) $f_{M,s}(m)$ and $f_{\\mathbf{R}\\mid M,s}(\\mathbf{r}\\mid m)$ describe the normalized distributions of earthquake magnitudes and locations within source $(s)$. This formulation is a direct application of the total-probability theorem in continuous form, integrating over all magnitudes and locations of earthquakes from source $(s)$ that could contribute to exceedance of level $i^*$. All *aleatory variability* in earthquake occurrence, earthquake size, earthquake location, and ground motion is thus accounted for through the integration [@McGuire2004; @Baker2021]. If multiple seismic sources contribute to the hazard at the site, the total annual exceedance rate $\\lambda_I(i^*)$ is obtained by summing the contributions from all sources as in [@eq-hazard-sum]. Assuming $N_S$ independent sources, each with its own occurrence rate and distributions as above, the overall exceedance frequency for level $i^*$ is given by [@eq-hazard-sum], where $\\lambda_I(i^*)^{(s)}$ is evaluated for each source via the hazard integral. This linear superposition is valid under the assumption that earthquake occurrences in different sources are independent (typically modeled as independent Poisson processes). The result is a seismic-hazard curve that quantifies the rate at which various ground-motion levels are exceeded at the site.\n139\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of the hazard integral as an application of the total-probability theorem, the linear superposition of source contributions under the assumption of independent Poisson processes, and the resulting seismic hazard curve are standard, well-supported elements of classical PSHA [WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf][WEB:https://courses.washington.edu/ess502/Cornell_1968.pdf]. The McGuire (2004) and Baker (2021) references are appropriate and widely cited authorities for these concepts. No technical errors are identified.]\n140\t\n141\t$$\n142\t\\lambda_I(i^*) \\;=\\; \\sum_{s=1}^{N_S} \\lambda_I(i^*)^{(s)}\n143\t$${#eq-hazard-sum}\n144\t\n145\tThe GMPE thus enters PSHA through both its median prediction $\\hat{\\eta}_I$ and its total standard deviation $\\sigma_{\\ln I}$: small differences among competing GMPEs in either quantity can produce substantial differences in computed exceedance rates, particularly at long return periods where the hazard integral weights the upper tail of the log-normal distribution at several standard deviations above the median [@OpenQuakeEngine].\n146\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The sensitivity of PSHA exceedance rates to GMPE median and standard deviation, and the disproportionate influence of sigma at long return periods due to weighting of the upper tail of the lognormal distribution, are well-established results in the PSHA literature [WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf]. The OpenQuake Engine is a widely used, open-source PSHA platform developed by the Global Earthquake Model (GEM) Foundation, and its citation as a reference for this computational aspect is appropriate.]\n147\t\n148\tThe annual exceedance frequency $\\lambda_I(i^*)$ obtained from the hazard integral [@eq-hazard-integral] can be interpreted as the mean number of times per year that intensity $i^*$ is exceeded. If exceedance events follow a Poisson process in time (a standard assumption in PSHA, owing to the Poissonian occurrence of earthquakes), then the probability of at least one exceedance in a single year is defined in [@eq-aep]^[For small $\\lambda_{I}$ (hazard levels of practical interest are usually low probability), $P_{1\\text{yr}} \\approx \\lambda_{I}$ (since $e^{-\\lambda} \\approx 1-\\lambda$)].\n149\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The interpretation of $\\lambda_I$ as a mean annual frequency and the derivation of the annual exceedance probability (AEP) from the Poisson assumption are standard results in PSHA, confirmed by multiple authoritative sources [WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf][WEB:https://www.earthquakes.bgs.ac.uk/hazard/haz_guide/psha.html]. The inline footnote correctly notes the first-order approximation $P_{1\\text{yr}} \\approx \\lambda_I$ for small $\\lambda_I$, which is mathematically valid. No errors are identified.]\n150\t\n151\t$$\n152\tP_{1\\text{yr}}[I > i^*] = 1 - \\exp[-\\lambda_I(i^*)] = \\text{AEP}\n153\t$${#eq-aep}\n154\t\n155\tBy extension, the probability of exceedance in $T$ years is $P_{T}(I > i^*) = 1 - \\exp[-\\lambda_I(i^*) T]$ assuming stationarity and independence year-to-year. This relation allows conversion between a mean annual rate and a probability over $T$ years^[A common case is $T=50$ years (an approximate lifespan of structures); for example, if $\\lambda_I(i^*) = 0.0021$ per year, then $P_{50}(I>i^*) = 1 - \\exp(-0.0021 \\times 50) \\approx 0.10$, i.e. a 10% probability in 50 years.]. The **return period** $T_R$ (or **mean return interval**) is a statistical average defined as the reciprocal of the annual frequency of exceedance, usually expressed in years $T_R = \\frac{1}{\\lambda_I(i^*)}$^[For low probabilities, return period is approximately the inverse of AEP as well, since $\\text{AEP} \\approx \\lambda_{I}$ for small $\\lambda_{I}$.]\n156\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The $T$-year exceedance probability formula $P_T = 1 - \\exp(-\\lambda T)$, its derivation from the Poisson process with stationarity and independence, the illustrative 50-year example, and the definition of return period as $T_R = 1/\\lambda_I$ are all standard results confirmed by multiple authoritative sources [WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf]. The numerical example (0.0021/yr over 50 years yields approximately 10% probability) is mathematically correct: $1 - e^{-0.105} \\approx 0.0997 \\approx 10\\%$. No errors are identified in this paragraph.]\n157\t\n158\t---\n159\treference-location: section\n160\tlink-citations: true\n161\t---\n162\t\n163\t\n164\tNear-surface geology modifies seismic waves as they propagate from bedrock to the ground surface. Soft soils amplify ground motion at intermediate-to-long periods while potentially deamplifying short-period motion through nonlinear strain effects; stiff rock sites transmit near-bedrock intensity levels with minimal modification [@Baker2021]. In probabilistic seismic hazard analysis, site effects can be incorporated through two mechanisms: (1) the site term embedded within ground-motion prediction equations (GMPEs), in which the median prediction and aleatory variability are functions of $V_{S30}$, or (2) an external amplification model applied to a reference-rock hazard curve [@Stewart2020]. The first mechanism is ergodic by construction---the empirical site term averages over many recording sites globally and may not capture conditions at a specific site or region. The second mechanism allows regionally calibrated amplification that accounts for nonlinear soil behavior as a function of shaking intensity. This assessment employs both approaches and selects the controlling (maximum) spectral ordinate for each period and hazard level to ensure that the design spectrum is not governed by whichever model happens to be less conservative at a given period.\n165\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The description of site amplification mechanisms -- GMPE-embedded $V_{S30}$-dependent site terms (ergodic) versus external amplification models applied to reference-rock hazard curves -- is consistent with established PSHA practice [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/webpeer-2014-16-jonathan_p._stewart_kioumars_afshari_and_youssef_m.a._hashash.pdf]. The Baker (2021) and Stewart (2020) references are appropriate citations for site effects in PSHA. The characterisation of GMPE site terms as ergodic and the envelope approach (selecting the maximum spectral ordinate) are a well-documented limitation and a recognized conservative design choice, respectively [WEB:https://www.daveboore.com/pubs_online/stewart_etal_ergodic_amps_cena.eqspectra_published.pdf].]\n166\t\n167\tSite effects were introduced in the probabilistic seismic hazard analysis through two complementary models. The first used an ergodic ground-motion framework in which a logic tree of $V_{S30}$-dependent GMPEs was evaluated for shear-wave velocities ranging from 200 m/s to 1100 m/s. For every branch, OpenQuake computed spectral accelerations $S_a(T_n, V_{S30})$ at the required service levels. Epistemic uncertainty was represented by the branch weights prescribed in the logic tree, and aleatory variability followed the total standard deviation of each GMPE. Because the site term is embedded empirically within the GMPEs, the resulting uniform-hazard spectra remain ergodic.\n168\t^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The use of OpenQuake Engine for logic-tree PSHA with $V_{S30}$-dependent GMPEs is well established and consistent with standard practice [WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf]. The stated $V_{S30}$ range (200 to 1100 m/s) covers Site Classes E through B/C boundary, a reasonable design range. The description of epistemic uncertainty via logic-tree branch weights and aleatory variability via GMPE sigma is the canonical two-uncertainty framework in PSHA. The conclusion that GMPE-based spectra remain ergodic because the site term is empirically embedded is technically correct [WEB:https://www.daveboore.com/pubs_online/stewart_etal_ergodic_amps_cena.eqspectra_published.pdf]. Confidence is MEDIUM because the specific GMPEs used in the logic tree are not identified in this paragraph, preventing full assessment of their suitability for the site region; this is a project-specific methodological detail.]\n169\t\n170\tThe second model is an amplification model based on a reference-rock hazard curve at $V_{\\mathrm{ref}} = 760\\,\\text{m/s}$ and transforms it to the target condition $V_{S30}$ by applying period-dependent, nonlinear amplification factors $F(T_n, V_{S30})$, according to [@eq-site-af]. The amplification model follows the formulation of Stewart and co-authors, which expresses $F$ as a log-normal random variable whose mean $\\eta_{F\\mid\\mathrm{PGA}}\\!\\left(T_n, V_{S30}\\right)$ and dispersion $\\sigma_{\\ln F}$ depend on oscillator period, shear-wave velocity, and input PGA [@Stewart2020; @Hashash2020]. For $V_{S30} < V_{\\mathrm{ref}}$, the model captures nonlinear deamplification at short periods and amplification at intermediate-to-long periods; for stiffer sites, the amplification asymptotically approaches unity. Formally, the amplification factor is modeled as a lognormal random variable [@eq-site-lnf]:\n171\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The use of $V_{\\mathrm{ref}} = 760\\,\\text{m/s}$ as the reference rock condition is standard and consistent with the NGA-West2 framework and the Stewart et al. amplification model [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/webpeer-2014-16-jonathan_p._stewart_kioumars_afshari_and_youssef_m.a._hashash.pdf][WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193]. The description of the amplification factor as a lognormal random variable with period-, $V_{S30}$-, and PGA-dependent mean and dispersion follows the formulation in Stewart (2020) and Hashash (2020), confirmed by published documentation of that model [WEB:https://escholarship.org/content/qt3037q1t6/qt3037q1t6.pdf]. The physical behaviour (deamplification at short periods for soft sites, amplification at intermediate-to-long periods, asymptotic approach to unity for stiff sites) is consistent with empirical site amplification data.]\n172\t\n173\t$$\n174\t\\ln F \\sim \\mathcal{N}\\!\\left(\\mu_{\\ln F},\\; \\sigma_{\\ln F}^2\\right)\n175\t$${#eq-site-lnf}\n176\t\n177\twhere $\\mu_{\\ln F}$ is the conditional mean of the log-amplification factor and $\\sigma_{\\ln F}$ is the total aleatory standard deviation, both for a given ($V_{S30}$, $T_n$, $pga^*$). The mean $\\mu_{\\ln F}$ decomposes additively into three period-dependent terms [@Stewart2020]: a reference-condition adjustment $F_{760}$ that bridges the difference between the 760 m/s and 3000 m/s reference horizons, a linear site term $F_V$ that scales with $V_{S30}$ relative to the reference velocity, and a nonlinear factor $F_{nl}$ that captures PGA-dependent soil deamplification at high strain levels. Similarly, the total dispersion decomposes in quadrature [@eq-site-sigma-decomp]:\n178\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The three-component additive decomposition of $\\mu_{\\ln F}$ into $F_{760}$, $F_V$, and $F_{nl}$ is consistent with the Hashash et al. (2020) model for central and eastern North America, which explicitly defines these three components [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193]. The $F_{760}$ component bridges the 760 m/s and 3000 m/s reference horizons; the $F_V$ component provides linear $V_{S30}$ scaling; and $F_{nl}$ captures nonlinear PGA-dependent deamplification, all consistent with the published model. The description of total dispersion decomposing in quadrature is consistent with standard uncertainty propagation for independent dispersion components. The claim is well-supported.]\n179\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bf7093-818d-4370-9615-a0aeec356215","uuid":"fe1aa3be-57ff-4726-aedc-57403488c59d","timestamp":"2026-03-30T01:51:09.344Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/review/psha/sessions/run-20260329_222746/psha_enh1.enhance.md","content":"^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The source documents cited this paragraph as [@Kramer1997]; however, the textbook \"Geotechnical Earthquake Engineering\" by Steven L. Kramer was published by Prentice-Hall in 1996, not 1997 [WEB:https://www.scirp.org/reference/referencespapers?referenceid=576202][WEB:https://archive.org/details/geotechnicaleart0000kram]. The citation key has been corrected to [@Kramer1996] in this enhanced session output. The Kramer (1996) textbook is an appropriate citation for the concept of conditional probability distributions identifying controlling scenario parameters, as it covers disaggregation methodology at a graduate level. The mathematical framework that follows, using the standard normal PDF $\\phi$ and indicator function $\\mathbf{1}_{(\\cdot)}$ to express the joint exceedance rate, is standard in the disaggregation literature [DOI:10.1785/BSSA0890020501]. Confidence is MEDIUM because the corrected citation year has not been verified against the physical book title page, and the description of the following equations refers to cross-references not reproduced here.]\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\lambda_{I}(i^{*},m_k,r_j,\\varepsilon)^{(s)} =\n\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\varepsilon\\ge\\varepsilon^{*}(m_k,r_j)\\}}\n\\,\n\\phi(\\varepsilon)\\,\nf_{M,s}(m_k)\\,\nf_{R|M,s}(r_j|m_k)\\,\n\\nu_{s},\n$${#eq-disagg-rate}\n\n$$\n\\lambda_{k,j,\\ell}(i^{*})=\n\\sum_{s=1}^{N_{S}}\n\\int_{m_k}\n\\int_{r_j}\n\\int_{\\varepsilon_\\ell}\n\\Delta\\lambda_{I}(i^{*},m_k,r_j,\\varepsilon)^{(s)}\nd\\varepsilon\\,dr\\,dm\n$${#eq-disagg-bin}\n\nThe conditional probability that exceedance of $i^*$ is produced by bin $(k,j,\\ell)$ is given by [@eq-disagg-prob]. The **modal scenario** is the $(k,j,\\ell)$ bin for which $\\theta_{k,j,\\ell}(i^{*})$ attains its maximum. Hazard disaggregation applies Bayes' theorem to the joint distribution of scenario parameters and ground-motion exceedance as in [@eq-disagg-prob]. The marginal distribution for $\\varepsilon$ quantifies the proportion of exceedance attributable to median or above-median ground-motion residuals. Marginal probabilities are obtained by double summation over the other indices as in [@eq-disagg-marginal-M], [@eq-disagg-marginal-R], [@eq-disagg-marginal-eps]:\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The definition of the modal scenario as the bin maximising the conditional probability and the application of Bayes' theorem to obtain the conditional probability distribution are consistent with Bazzurro and Cornell (1999) [DOI:10.1785/BSSA0890020501]. Both source review sessions identified several notation errors in the display math that follows: (1) the subscript notation $\\theta_{j,k,l}$ used magnitude-last ordering and a lowercase \"l\" instead of the proper LaTeX $\\ell$, inconsistent with the $(k,j,\\ell)$ convention (magnitude, distance, residual) established throughout; (2) a comma was missing in $\\lambda_{k,j\\ell}$; (3) the normalization expression was malformed as $\\sum_k\\sum_j\\sum_\\ell P_{k,j,\\ell}([I>i^*]=1$, with unmatched brackets. All three errors have been corrected in [@eq-disagg-prob] and the marginal equations below. Confidence is MEDIUM because the corrected subscript ordering follows conventional practice [DOI:10.1785/BSSA0890020501] but independent normative confirmation requires primary source access.]\n\n$$\nP[M=m_k,R=r_j,\\varepsilon=\\varepsilon_\\ell\\mid I> i^{*}] = \\theta_{k,j,\\ell}=\n\\frac{\\lambda_{k,j,\\ell}(i^{*})}{\\lambda_{I}(i^{*})},\n\\qquad\n\\sum_{k}\\sum_{j}\\sum_{\\ell}\\theta_{k,j,\\ell} = 1\n$${#eq-disagg-prob}\n\n$$\nP\\left[M \\in m_k\\mid I> i^{*}\\right] = \\sum_{j}\\sum_{\\ell}\\theta_{k,j,\\ell}\n$${#eq-disagg-marginal-M}\n$$\nP\\left[R \\in r_j\\mid I> i^{*}\\right] = \\sum_{k}\\sum_{\\ell}\\theta_{k,j,\\ell}\n$${#eq-disagg-marginal-R}\n$$\nP\\left[\\varepsilon \\in \\varepsilon_\\ell\\mid I> i^{*}\\right] = \\sum_{k}\\sum_{j}\\theta_{k,j,\\ell}\n$${#eq-disagg-marginal-eps}\n\nDisaggregation provides a data-driven basis required for rigorous scenario selection in engineering analysis. The *modal scenario* and the highest-probability bins identified through disaggregation correspond to the earthquake characteristics most responsible for exceedance at the design ground-motion level.\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of disaggregation as providing a data-driven basis for scenario selection, and the identification of the modal scenario with the earthquake characteristics most responsible for exceedance, are standard conclusions of the disaggregation methodology well supported in the literature [DOI:10.1785/BSSA0890020501][WEB:https://pubs.geoscienceworld.org/ssa/bssa/article-abstract/89/2/501/342641/Disaggregation-of-seismic-hazard]. This concluding paragraph is conceptually accurate and consistent with the Bazzurro and Cornell (1999) framework.]\n\nProbabilistic Seismic Hazard Analysis (PSHA) is a methodology that quantifies earthquake ground-motion hazard at a site in terms of probabilities, by integrating over all possible earthquakes that could affect the site [@Cornell1968; @McGuire2004]. Unlike deterministic approaches that focus on a single scenario, PSHA considers the full range of magnitudes, locations, and associated ground motions, providing a comprehensive description of the rate at which various levels of shaking may be exceeded. In probabilistic seismic hazard analysis (PSHA), the annual frequency (rate) of exceeding a ground-motion level at a site is obtained by integrating over all possible earthquake scenarios. For a single seismic source $(s)$ with an annual occurrence rate of earthquakes (above a minimum magnitude $M_{\\min}$) denoted $\\nu_{0}^{(s)}$ (where $\\nu_{0}^{(s)} = N(M_{\\min})^{(s)}$), the rate of exceedance of a ground-motion level $i^*$ (e.g., a peak ground acceleration or spectral acceleration) is defined in [@eq-hazard-integral], where $m$ is earthquake magnitude, $\\mathbf r$ is the source-to-site distance, $f_{M,s}(m)$ and $f_{\\mathbf{R}\\mid M,s}(\\mathbf{r}\\mid m)$ are the probability density functions describing the distribution of magnitudes and distances for source $(s)$, and $P\\left[I > i^* \\mid M=m,\\,\\mathbf r\\right]$ is the conditional probability that the ground-motion parameter $I$ exceeds level $i^*$ given an event of magnitude $m$ at distance $\\mathbf r$. $M_{\\max}^{(s)}$ is the maximum considered magnitude for source $(s)$, which is related to the maximum credible earthquake (MCE) of that source.\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The description of PSHA as a methodology integrating over all earthquake scenarios to quantify exceedance rates, attributed to Cornell (1968) and McGuire (2004), is accurate and well established. Cornell (1968) is universally cited as the foundational paper of PSHA, and the Cornell-McGuire procedure is the canonical PSHA framework [WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf][WEB:https://courses.washington.edu/ess502/Cornell_1968.pdf]. The hazard integral formulation with magnitude PDF $f_{M,s}(m)$, distance PDF $f_{\\mathbf{R}|M,s}(\\mathbf{r}|m)$, and conditional exceedance probability $P[I>i^*|m,\\mathbf{r}]$ is the standard form. No technical errors are identified in this introductory paragraph.]\n\n$$\n\\lambda_I(i^*)^{(s)} \\;=\\;\n\\nu_{0}^{(s)}\n\\int_{M_{\\min}}^{M_{\\max}^{(s)}} \\int_{D}\nP\\left[ I > i^* \\,\\big|\\, m, \\mathbf{r} \\right]\\;\nf_{M,s}(m)\\;\nf_{\\mathbf{R}\\mid M,s}(\\mathbf{r}\\mid m)\\;\n\\mathrm d\\mathbf{r}\\,\\mathrm dm\n$${#eq-hazard-integral}\n\nThe probability density functions (PDF) $f_{M,s}(m)$ and $f_{\\mathbf{R}\\mid M,s}(\\mathbf{r}\\mid m)$ describe the normalized distributions of earthquake magnitudes and locations within source $(s)$. This formulation is a direct application of the total-probability theorem in continuous form, integrating over all magnitudes and locations of earthquakes from source $(s)$ that could contribute to exceedance of level $i^*$. All *aleatory variability* in earthquake occurrence, earthquake size, earthquake location, and ground motion is thus accounted for through the integration [@McGuire2004; @Baker2021]. If multiple seismic sources contribute to the hazard at the site, the total annual exceedance rate $\\lambda_I(i^*)$ is obtained by summing the contributions from all sources as in [@eq-hazard-sum]. Assuming $N_S$ independent sources, each with its own occurrence rate and distributions as above, the overall exceedance frequency for level $i^*$ is given by [@eq-hazard-sum], where $\\lambda_I(i^*)^{(s)}$ is evaluated for each source via the hazard integral. This linear superposition is valid under the assumption that earthquake occurrences in different sources are independent (typically modeled as independent Poisson processes). The result is a seismic-hazard curve that quantifies the rate at which various ground-motion levels are exceeded at the site.\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The characterisation of the hazard integral as an application of the total-probability theorem, the linear superposition of source contributions under the assumption of independent Poisson processes, and the resulting seismic hazard curve are standard, well-supported elements of classical PSHA [WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf][WEB:https://courses.washington.edu/ess502/Cornell_1968.pdf]. The McGuire (2004) and Baker (2021) references are appropriate and widely cited authorities for these concepts. No technical errors are identified.]\n\n$$\n\\lambda_I(i^*) \\;=\\; \\sum_{s=1}^{N_S} \\lambda_I(i^*)^{(s)}\n$${#eq-hazard-sum}\n\nThe GMPE thus enters PSHA through both its median prediction $\\hat{\\eta}_I$ and its total standard deviation $\\sigma_{\\ln I}$: small differences among competing GMPEs in either quantity can produce substantial differences in computed exceedance rates, particularly at long return periods where the hazard integral weights the upper tail of the log-normal distribution at several standard deviations above the median [@OpenQuakeEngine].\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The sensitivity of PSHA exceedance rates to GMPE median and standard deviation, and the disproportionate influence of sigma at long return periods due to weighting of the upper tail of the lognormal distribution, are well-established results in the PSHA literature [WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf]. The OpenQuake Engine is a widely used, open-source PSHA platform developed by the Global Earthquake Model (GEM) Foundation, and its citation as a reference for this computational aspect is appropriate.]\n\nThe annual exceedance frequency $\\lambda_I(i^*)$ obtained from the hazard integral [@eq-hazard-integral] can be interpreted as the mean number of times per year that intensity $i^*$ is exceeded. If exceedance events follow a Poisson process in time (a standard assumption in PSHA, owing to the Poissonian occurrence of earthquakes), then the probability of at least one exceedance in a single year is defined in [@eq-aep]^[For small $\\lambda_{I}$ (hazard levels of practical interest are usually low probability), $P_{1\\text{yr}} \\approx \\lambda_{I}$ (since $e^{-\\lambda} \\approx 1-\\lambda$)].\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The interpretation of $\\lambda_I$ as a mean annual frequency and the derivation of the annual exceedance probability (AEP) from the Poisson assumption are standard results in PSHA, confirmed by multiple authoritative sources [WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf][WEB:https://www.earthquakes.bgs.ac.uk/hazard/haz_guide/psha.html]. The inline footnote correctly notes the first-order approximation $P_{1\\text{yr}} \\approx \\lambda_I$ for small $\\lambda_I$, which is mathematically valid. No errors are identified.]\n\n$$\nP_{1\\text{yr}}[I > i^*] = 1 - \\exp[-\\lambda_I(i^*)] = \\text{AEP}\n$${#eq-aep}\n\nBy extension, the probability of exceedance in $T$ years is $P_{T}(I > i^*) = 1 - \\exp[-\\lambda_I(i^*) T]$ assuming stationarity and independence year-to-year. This relation allows conversion between a mean annual rate and a probability over $T$ years^[A common case is $T=50$ years (an approximate lifespan of structures); for example, if $\\lambda_I(i^*) = 0.0021$ per year, then $P_{50}(I>i^*) = 1 - \\exp(-0.0021 \\times 50) \\approx 0.10$, i.e. a 10% probability in 50 years.]. The **return period** $T_R$ (or **mean return interval**) is a statistical average defined as the reciprocal of the annual frequency of exceedance, usually expressed in years $T_R = \\frac{1}{\\lambda_I(i^*)}$^[For low probabilities, return period is approximately the inverse of AEP as well, since $\\text{AEP} \\approx \\lambda_{I}$ for small $\\lambda_{I}$.]\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The $T$-year exceedance probability formula $P_T = 1 - \\exp(-\\lambda T)$, its derivation from the Poisson process with stationarity and independence, the illustrative 50-year example, and the definition of return period as $T_R = 1/\\lambda_I$ are all standard results confirmed by multiple authoritative sources [WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf]. The numerical example (0.0021/yr over 50 years yields approximately 10% probability) is mathematically correct: $1 - e^{-0.105} \\approx 0.0997 \\approx 10\\%$. No errors are identified in this paragraph.]\n\n---\nreference-location: section\nlink-citations: true\n---\n\n\nNear-surface geology modifies seismic waves as they propagate from bedrock to the ground surface. Soft soils amplify ground motion at intermediate-to-long periods while potentially deamplifying short-period motion through nonlinear strain effects; stiff rock sites transmit near-bedrock intensity levels with minimal modification [@Baker2021]. In probabilistic seismic hazard analysis, site effects can be incorporated through two mechanisms: (1) the site term embedded within ground-motion prediction equations (GMPEs), in which the median prediction and aleatory variability are functions of $V_{S30}$, or (2) an external amplification model applied to a reference-rock hazard curve [@Stewart2020]. The first mechanism is ergodic by construction---the empirical site term averages over many recording sites globally and may not capture conditions at a specific site or region. The second mechanism allows regionally calibrated amplification that accounts for nonlinear soil behavior as a function of shaking intensity. This assessment employs both approaches and selects the controlling (maximum) spectral ordinate for each period and hazard level to ensure that the design spectrum is not governed by whichever model happens to be less conservative at a given period.\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The description of site amplification mechanisms -- GMPE-embedded $V_{S30}$-dependent site terms (ergodic) versus external amplification models applied to reference-rock hazard curves -- is consistent with established PSHA practice [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/webpeer-2014-16-jonathan_p._stewart_kioumars_afshari_and_youssef_m.a._hashash.pdf]. The Baker (2021) and Stewart (2020) references are appropriate citations for site effects in PSHA. The characterisation of GMPE site terms as ergodic and the envelope approach (selecting the maximum spectral ordinate) are a well-documented limitation and a recognized conservative design choice, respectively [WEB:https://www.daveboore.com/pubs_online/stewart_etal_ergodic_amps_cena.eqspectra_published.pdf].]\n\nSite effects were introduced in the probabilistic seismic hazard analysis through two complementary models. The first used an ergodic ground-motion framework in which a logic tree of $V_{S30}$-dependent GMPEs was evaluated for shear-wave velocities ranging from 200 m/s to 1100 m/s. For every branch, OpenQuake computed spectral accelerations $S_a(T_n, V_{S30})$ at the required service levels. Epistemic uncertainty was represented by the branch weights prescribed in the logic tree, and aleatory variability followed the total standard deviation of each GMPE. Because the site term is embedded empirically within the GMPEs, the resulting uniform-hazard spectra remain ergodic.\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The use of OpenQuake Engine for logic-tree PSHA with $V_{S30}$-dependent GMPEs is well established and consistent with standard practice [WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf]. The stated $V_{S30}$ range (200 to 1100 m/s) covers Site Classes E through B/C boundary, a reasonable design range. The description of epistemic uncertainty via logic-tree branch weights and aleatory variability via GMPE sigma is the canonical two-uncertainty framework in PSHA. The conclusion that GMPE-based spectra remain ergodic because the site term is empirically embedded is technically correct [WEB:https://www.daveboore.com/pubs_online/stewart_etal_ergodic_amps_cena.eqspectra_published.pdf]. Confidence is MEDIUM because the specific GMPEs used in the logic tree are not identified in this paragraph, preventing full assessment of their suitability for the site region; this is a project-specific methodological detail.]\n\nThe second model is an amplification model based on a reference-rock hazard curve at $V_{\\mathrm{ref}} = 760\\,\\text{m/s}$ and transforms it to the target condition $V_{S30}$ by applying period-dependent, nonlinear amplification factors $F(T_n, V_{S30})$, according to [@eq-site-af]. The amplification model follows the formulation of Stewart and co-authors, which expresses $F$ as a log-normal random variable whose mean $\\eta_{F\\mid\\mathrm{PGA}}\\!\\left(T_n, V_{S30}\\right)$ and dispersion $\\sigma_{\\ln F}$ depend on oscillator period, shear-wave velocity, and input PGA [@Stewart2020; @Hashash2020]. For $V_{S30} < V_{\\mathrm{ref}}$, the model captures nonlinear deamplification at short periods and amplification at intermediate-to-long periods; for stiffer sites, the amplification asymptotically approaches unity. Formally, the amplification factor is modeled as a lognormal random variable [@eq-site-lnf]:\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The use of $V_{\\mathrm{ref}} = 760\\,\\text{m/s}$ as the reference rock condition is standard and consistent with the NGA-West2 framework and the Stewart et al. amplification model [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/webpeer-2014-16-jonathan_p._stewart_kioumars_afshari_and_youssef_m.a._hashash.pdf][WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193]. The description of the amplification factor as a lognormal random variable with period-, $V_{S30}$-, and PGA-dependent mean and dispersion follows the formulation in Stewart (2020) and Hashash (2020), confirmed by published documentation of that model [WEB:https://escholarship.org/content/qt3037q1t6/qt3037q1t6.pdf]. The physical behaviour (deamplification at short periods for soft sites, amplification at intermediate-to-long periods, asymptotic approach to unity for stiff sites) is consistent with empirical site amplification data.]\n\n$$\n\\ln F \\sim \\mathcal{N}\\!\\left(\\mu_{\\ln F},\\; \\sigma_{\\ln F}^2\\right)\n$${#eq-site-lnf}\n\nwhere $\\mu_{\\ln F}$ is the conditional mean of the log-amplification factor and $\\sigma_{\\ln F}$ is the total aleatory standard deviation, both for a given ($V_{S30}$, $T_n$, $pga^*$). The mean $\\mu_{\\ln F}$ decomposes additively into three period-dependent terms [@Stewart2020]: a reference-condition adjustment $F_{760}$ that bridges the difference between the 760 m/s and 3000 m/s reference horizons, a linear site term $F_V$ that scales with $V_{S30}$ relative to the reference velocity, and a nonlinear factor $F_{nl}$ that captures PGA-dependent soil deamplification at high strain levels. Similarly, the total dispersion decomposes in quadrature [@eq-site-sigma-decomp]:\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The three-component additive decomposition of $\\mu_{\\ln F}$ into $F_{760}$, $F_V$, and $F_{nl}$ is consistent with the Hashash et al. (2020) model for central and eastern North America, which explicitly defines these three components [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193]. The $F_{760}$ component bridges the 760 m/s and 3000 m/s reference horizons; the $F_V$ component provides linear $V_{S30}$ scaling; and $F_{nl}$ captures nonlinear PGA-dependent deamplification, all consistent with the published model. The description of total dispersion decomposing in quadrature is consistent with standard uncertainty propagation for independent dispersion components. The claim is well-supported.]\n","numLines":100,"startLine":80,"totalLines":258}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01Cs5z2yt7qh6PmS2dxV5ujG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DABN9AjwZAWBnpb6a1eTH7","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/review/psha/sessions/run-20260329_222746/psha_enh1.enhance.md","limit":100,"offset":179},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6451,"cache_read_input_tokens":40897,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6451,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":37,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bf7093-818d-4370-9615-a0aeec356215","uuid":"aca68094-29c1-470d-8292-e30e24cca665"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DABN9AjwZAWBnpb6a1eTH7","type":"tool_result","content":"179\t\n180\t$$\n181\t\\sigma_{\\ln F}^2 = \\sigma_{L}^2 + \\sigma_{I}^2 + \\sigma_{NL}^2\n182\t$${#eq-site-sigma-decomp}\n183\t\n184\twhere $\\sigma_L$ is the linear site-term dispersion (dependent on $V_{S30}$ and period), $\\sigma_I$ is the inter-reference dispersion (nonzero only when $V_{\\mathrm{ref}} = 3000\\,\\text{m/s}$), and $\\sigma_{NL}$ is the nonlinear dispersion (dependent on $V_{S30}$ and $pga^*$). All components are period-dependent and tabulated in the published model [@Stewart2020]. The detailed functional forms and calibration of each component are documented in [@sec-site_model].\n185\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The identification of three variance components ($\\sigma_L^2$, $\\sigma_I^2$, $\\sigma_{NL}^2$) and their physical interpretations -- linear site-term dispersion, inter-reference dispersion (nonzero only for the 3000 m/s reference), and nonlinear dispersion -- are consistent with the published Stewart (2020) model documentation [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193]. The statement that $\\sigma_I$ is nonzero only when $V_{\\mathrm{ref}} = 3000$ m/s correctly reflects the model's construction.]\n186\t\n187\t$$\n188\tS_a\\!\\left(T_n, V_{S30}\\right) = F\\!\\left(T_n, V_{S30}\\right)\\,S_a\\!\\left(T_n, V_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\right).\n189\t$${#eq-site-af}\n190\t\n191\tFor each return period $T_R$, oscillator period $T_n$, fractile $p$, and site class $V_{S30}$, the design spectral ordinate is defined as the maximum across the GMPE-based and amplification-based estimates [@eq-site-envelope]:\n192\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The envelope approach for defining the design spectral ordinate as the maximum of the GMPE-based and amplification-based estimates across all return periods, oscillator periods, and $V_{S30}$ values is a technically sound and conservative methodology consistent with standard engineering practice for site-specific PSHA [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/webpeer-2014-16-jonathan_p._stewart_kioumars_afshari_and_youssef_m.a._hashash.pdf]. The reference to fractile $p$ indicates awareness of the probabilistic nature of the amplification factor, which is appropriate.]\n193\t\n194\t$$\n195\tS_a^{\\mathrm{max}}(T_n, V_{S30}) = \\max\\!\\left\\{S_a^{\\mathrm{GMPE}}(T_n, V_{S30}),\\; S_a^{F}(T_n, V_{S30})\\right\\}\n196\t$${#eq-site-envelope}\n197\t\n198\twhere $S_a^{\\mathrm{GMPE}}$ is the spectral ordinate obtained from the GMPE logic tree evaluated directly at the target $V_{S30}$, and $S_a^{F}$ is the spectral ordinate obtained by applying the amplification factor $F$ to the reference-rock hazard curve per [@eq-site-af]. This envelope ensures that the controlling (most hazardous) estimate governs the design spectrum at every period. GMPEs may underpredict at certain periods or $V_{S30}$ ranges where their empirical site terms are poorly constrained by the underlying strong-motion database, while the amplification model may underpredict where the reference-rock hazard curve does not fully represent near-field or source-specific effects. The envelope eliminates the risk of unconservative design by selecting the larger ordinate at each spectral period.\n199\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The rationale for the envelope approach -- that GMPEs may underpredict at poorly constrained $V_{S30}$ ranges while amplification models may underpredict for near-field or source-specific effects -- is technically sound and reflects known limitations of both approaches [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193][WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf]. The selection of the maximum ordinate at each period as the governing design value is a standard conservative design choice. No technical errors are identified.]\n200\t\n201\t## Site Classification {#sec-vs30_appendix}\n202\t\n203\tThe **Vs30** parameter is widely measured in the field using methods such as borehole logging, seismic cone penetration tests, and surface wave techniques. Introduced into engineering practice in the early 1990s, it provides a simple, robust metric to characterize site conditions for earthquake ground motions. **Vs30** is defined as the time-averaged shear-wave velocity in the upper 30 meters of a site profile. In practical terms, it represents the overall stiffness of the near-surface soils and rock. Mathematically, Vs30 is computed as the harmonic average of shear-wave velocity ($V_{S}$) over the top 30 m:\n204\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The definition of $V_{S30}$ as the time-averaged (harmonic-mean) shear-wave velocity over the upper 30 m, its introduction in the early 1990s, and its measurement via borehole logging, seismic CPT, and surface-wave techniques are all well-established facts confirmed by extensive literature [WEB:https://www.usgs.gov/publications/mapping-nehrp-vs30-site-classes]. The characterisation of $V_{S30}$ as a measure of near-surface stiffness and its use as the primary site parameter in modern GMPEs is broadly supported.]\n205\t\n206\t$$\n207\tV_{S30} = \\frac{30\\,\\text{m}}{\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\frac{H_i}{V_{S,i}}}\n208\t$$\n209\t\n210\twhere $H_i$ and $V_{S,i}$ are the thickness and shear-wave velocity of the $i$-th layer in the upper 30 m. This formula gives the equivalent uniform velocity that yields the same travel time through 30 m of material as the actual layered profile.\n211\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The harmonic-mean formula $V_{S30} = 30\\,\\text{m} / \\sum_i (H_i / V_{S,i})$ is the standard definition of $V_{S30}$ and is consistent with the NEHRP provisions, ASCE 7-22, and all major building codes that adopt this parameter [WEB:https://www.usgs.gov/publications/mapping-nehrp-vs30-site-classes][WEB:https://www.daveboore.com/pubs_online/vs30_from_shallow_models.pdf]. The physical interpretation as the equivalent uniform velocity yielding the same travel time is mathematically correct.]\n212\t\n213\tVs30 has become the primary quantitative predictor of site effects in modern ground-motion models. Virtually all recent ground motion prediction equations (GMPEs) incorporate Vs30 as a key input parameter to adjust predicted shaking for site conditions. Empirical GMPEs (e.g., NGA-West2 and NGA-East) often use a functional form in which the median ground motion is scaled according to $\\ln(V_{S30})$ or a similar proxy, with separate terms for linear scaling and nonlinear saturation at low velocities. Large datasets have validated the use of Vs30 in GMPEs, showing consistent correlations between Vs30 and site amplification across many locations [@Borcherdt2012].\n214\t^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The use of $V_{S30}$ as the primary site parameter in NGA-West2 and NGA-East GMPEs, with $\\ln(V_{S30})$-based functional forms and separate nonlinear terms, is well-established and confirmed by published GMPE documentation [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193][WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf]. The reference [@Borcherdt2012] is associated with R.D. Borcherdt, who has published extensively on site classification and $V_{S30}$-based site amplification factors; Borcherdt is a co-author of Dobry et al. (2000) [DOI:10.1193/1.1586082], the foundational paper introducing the NEHRP site coefficient system. The specific 2012 year in this citation could not be independently verified from publicly available sources; published Borcherdt works on site classification span multiple years (1994, 2000, and later). Confidence is MEDIUM due to the partially unverified citation year.]\n215\t\n216\tIn parallel, site-specific analyses (such as 1D site response simulations) rely on Vs30 as an initial classification of the site profile, although they incorporate the full shear-wave velocity profile with depth. While Vs30 does not capture all facets of site response -- such as basin depth or resonance effects -- it serves as a practical first-order proxy.\n217\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The statement that site-specific 1D site response simulations use the full shear-wave velocity profile rather than relying solely on $V_{S30}$, and that $V_{S30}$ does not capture basin depth or resonance effects, is accurate and consistent with the established literature on site response analysis [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193][WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf]. The description of $V_{S30}$ as a practical first-order proxy is standard and well-supported.]\n218\t\n219\t### NEHRP Site Classes\n220\t\n221\tThe NEHRP provisions [-@BSSC2015] established the foundational site classification system used in building codes, defining Site Classes A through F based on Vs30 ranges. The 2020 edition of the NEHRP provisions (FEMA P-2082) introduced a substantially expanded nine-class system -- A, B, BC, C, CD, D, DE, E, and F -- replacing the six-class A-F framework [WEB:https://www.nist.gov/news-events/news/2021/02/2020-nehrp-recommended-seismic-provisions-new-buildings-and-other]. Three intermediate classes (BC, CD, DE) were added to provide finer-grained differentiation. Additionally, the 2020 edition requires that site classification be based solely on shear-wave velocity ($V_{S30}$); the 2015 edition allowed SPT blow count and undrained shear strength as alternative classification bases, but this option was removed in FEMA P-2082 [WEB:https://www.nist.gov/news-events/news/2021/02/2020-nehrp-recommended-seismic-provisions-new-buildings-and-other]. The 2020 provisions also introduced multi-period response spectra (MPRS), replacing the two-period ($S_S$, $S_1$) spectral shape. ASCE 7-22 and IBC 2021 implement the nine-class system in Table 20.2-1, superseding the six-class A-F boundaries from prior editions [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. The approximate Vs30 boundaries for the nine classes are:\n222\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The expansion of the NEHRP site classification from six classes (A-F, FEMA P-1050) to nine classes (A, B, BC, C, CD, D, DE, E, F, FEMA P-2082) is confirmed by the NIST announcement of the 2020 NEHRP provisions [WEB:https://www.nist.gov/news-events/news/2021/02/2020-nehrp-recommended-seismic-provisions-new-buildings-and-other] and is consistent with the USGS ASCE 7-22 web service implementation [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. The transition to Vs30-only classification and the introduction of MPRS are confirmed from multiple secondary sources describing the 2020 NEHRP updates. Both prior audit sessions cited only BSSC2015 and the six-class system, flagging the outdated citation at MEDIUM confidence; this paragraph corrects that gap.]\n223\t\n224\t* **Site Class A:** Hard rock, $V_{S30} > 1500$ m/s.\n225\t* **Site Class B:** Rock, $V_{S30} \\approx$ 760-1500 m/s (760 m/s is the B/BC boundary).\n226\t* **Site Class BC:** Soft rock, $V_{S30} \\approx$ 530-760 m/s (new intermediate class).\n227\t* **Site Class C:** Very dense soil or soft rock, $V_{S30} \\approx$ 365-530 m/s.\n228\t* **Site Class CD:** Dense sand or very stiff clay, $V_{S30} \\approx$ 270-365 m/s (new intermediate class).\n229\t* **Site Class D:** Stiff soil, $V_{S30} \\approx$ 185-270 m/s.\n230\t* **Site Class DE:** Loose sand or medium stiff clay, $V_{S30} \\approx$ 150-185 m/s (new intermediate class).\n231\t* **Site Class E:** Soft clay, $V_{S30} < 150$ m/s (boundary adjusted from 180 m/s in prior editions).\n232\t* **Site Class F:** Soils requiring site-specific evaluation (e.g., very soft clays, peats, liquefiable soils, or thick high-plasticity clays).\n233\t^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The nine-class structure and the approximate Vs30 boundaries listed are consistent with descriptions of ASCE 7-22 Table 20.2-1 as implemented in FEMA P-2082, and with the USGS ASCE 7-22 web service [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. The specific numerical thresholds for intermediate classes BC, CD, and DE (530, 365/270, 185/150 m/s) are synthesized from secondary sources and should be verified against ASCE 7-22 Table 20.2-1 in the normative standard document, which is behind a paywall. Confidence is MEDIUM for the specific intermediate-class boundaries; the nine-class structure and the qualitative descriptions are HIGH confidence.]\n234\t\n235\tClass F is defined by qualitative subsoil conditions rather than a $V_{S30}$ threshold; if those conditions are met, a detailed site response analysis is mandated in lieu of using generic amplification factors. In structural design, each non-F class is assigned amplification factors (tabulated in ASCE 7-22 as functions of $V_{S30}$ and ground-motion intensity) that modify the multi-period response spectrum to account for local site effects [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html].\n236\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The description of Class F as requiring site-specific analysis and the role of non-F classes in providing tabulated amplification factors are consistent with NEHRP and ASCE 7-22 provisions [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html][WEB:https://www.usgs.gov/publications/mapping-nehrp-vs30-site-classes]. The reference to multi-period response spectrum amplification factors (replacing the two-factor $F_a$, $F_v$ system from prior editions in the 2020 NEHRP context) is consistent with the FEMA P-2082 framework. The substance is accurate and well-supported.]\n237\t\n238\t### AS1726 Guidelines\n239\t\n240\tThe Australian Standard AS 1726-2017 [-@AS1726-2017] provides guidelines for conducting geotechnical investigations and includes a site classification system that categorises sites into classes based on their soil properties, such as shear wave velocity parameter $\\bar v_S$, depth to bedrock, and soil type. The Australian Standard AS 1170.4 \"Earthquake Actions on Structures\" uses a related site classification system based on the stiffness of the ground, but it does not explicitly use the shear wave velocity parameter $\\bar v_S$ to classify the ground. The categories range from A (rock) to E (soft soil), with a special category (S) for sites requiring site-specific investigations.\n241\t^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: AS 1726-2017 is a legitimate Australian Standard for geotechnical site investigations and AS 1170.4 is the Australian Standard for earthquake actions on structures. The described A-through-E-plus-S classification structure, and the distinction that AS 1170.4 does not explicitly use $\\bar v_S$ while AS 1726-2017 does, are consistent with the known development of Australian seismic provisions. Both standards are behind a paywall, limiting independent external verification of the specific classification details described here. Confidence is MEDIUM due to restricted access to the normative source documents.]\n242\t\n243\t{{< include /psha/_tbl/AS1726.qmd >}}\n244\t\n245\t### ASCE/SEI 7-22 Guidelines\n246\t\n247\tThe ASCE/SEI 7-22 Standard [-@ASCE722] provides guidelines for conducting geotechnical investigations and includes a site classification system that categorises sites into classes based on their soil properties, such as shear wave velocity, depth to bedrock, and soil type. According to ASCE 7-22 [@ASCE722], the site soil shall be classified based on the average shear wave velocity parameter, $\\bar v_S$, which is derived from the measured shear wave velocity profile from the ground surface to a depth of 30 m.\n248\t^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: ASCE 7-22 Chapter 20 specifies site classification based on $\\bar v_S$ (average shear-wave velocity in the upper 30 m, equivalent to $V_{S30}$), along with average standard penetration test blow count and undrained shear strength. The description of the classification being based on soil properties including shear wave velocity, depth to bedrock, and soil type is accurate [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. The notation $\\bar v_S$ (overbar) is used in ASCE 7-22 to denote the parameter previously labeled $V_{S30}$ in earlier editions. The claim is well-supported.]\n249\t\n250\t{{< include /psha/_tbl/ASCE722.qmd >}}\n251\t\n252\t### NBC-2020 Guidelines\n253\t\n254\tThe National Building Code of Canada [-@NBC2020] provides guidelines for site characterization and seismic site response. Similar to ASCE 7-22, it uses an average shear wave velocity in the upper 30 m (commonly denoted $v_{S30}$), standard penetration test (SPT) blow count, and/or undrained shear strength ($s_{u}$) to classify sites into categories A through F. Where site-specific investigations are warranted (e.g., liquefiable soils, highly sensitive clays, peat), the code requires more detailed analysis.\n255\t^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The National Building Code of Canada 2020 (NBC 2020) includes seismic site classification provisions that use $v_{S30}$, SPT blow count, and undrained shear strength $s_u$ for site categories A through F, consistent with the description provided. The classification structure is aligned with the NEHRP/ASCE 7 framework. The NBC is not freely available in full text, preventing complete independent verification of the exact classification thresholds and procedure for the 2020 edition. The description is technically plausible and consistent with the known evolution of Canadian seismic provisions, but Confidence is MEDIUM due to limited publicly accessible external verification of the specific NBC 2020 provisions.]\n256\t\n257\t{{< include /psha/_tbl/NBC2020.qmd >}}\n258\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0bf7093-818d-4370-9615-a0aeec356215","uuid":"f211958a-52c7-4801-bd56-445507e31af5","timestamp":"2026-03-30T01:51:13.708Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/review/psha/sessions/run-20260329_222746/psha_enh1.enhance.md","content":"\n$$\n\\sigma_{\\ln F}^2 = \\sigma_{L}^2 + \\sigma_{I}^2 + \\sigma_{NL}^2\n$${#eq-site-sigma-decomp}\n\nwhere $\\sigma_L$ is the linear site-term dispersion (dependent on $V_{S30}$ and period), $\\sigma_I$ is the inter-reference dispersion (nonzero only when $V_{\\mathrm{ref}} = 3000\\,\\text{m/s}$), and $\\sigma_{NL}$ is the nonlinear dispersion (dependent on $V_{S30}$ and $pga^*$). All components are period-dependent and tabulated in the published model [@Stewart2020]. The detailed functional forms and calibration of each component are documented in [@sec-site_model].\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The identification of three variance components ($\\sigma_L^2$, $\\sigma_I^2$, $\\sigma_{NL}^2$) and their physical interpretations -- linear site-term dispersion, inter-reference dispersion (nonzero only for the 3000 m/s reference), and nonlinear dispersion -- are consistent with the published Stewart (2020) model documentation [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193]. The statement that $\\sigma_I$ is nonzero only when $V_{\\mathrm{ref}} = 3000$ m/s correctly reflects the model's construction.]\n\n$$\nS_a\\!\\left(T_n, V_{S30}\\right) = F\\!\\left(T_n, V_{S30}\\right)\\,S_a\\!\\left(T_n, V_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\right).\n$${#eq-site-af}\n\nFor each return period $T_R$, oscillator period $T_n$, fractile $p$, and site class $V_{S30}$, the design spectral ordinate is defined as the maximum across the GMPE-based and amplification-based estimates [@eq-site-envelope]:\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The envelope approach for defining the design spectral ordinate as the maximum of the GMPE-based and amplification-based estimates across all return periods, oscillator periods, and $V_{S30}$ values is a technically sound and conservative methodology consistent with standard engineering practice for site-specific PSHA [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/webpeer-2014-16-jonathan_p._stewart_kioumars_afshari_and_youssef_m.a._hashash.pdf]. The reference to fractile $p$ indicates awareness of the probabilistic nature of the amplification factor, which is appropriate.]\n\n$$\nS_a^{\\mathrm{max}}(T_n, V_{S30}) = \\max\\!\\left\\{S_a^{\\mathrm{GMPE}}(T_n, V_{S30}),\\; S_a^{F}(T_n, V_{S30})\\right\\}\n$${#eq-site-envelope}\n\nwhere $S_a^{\\mathrm{GMPE}}$ is the spectral ordinate obtained from the GMPE logic tree evaluated directly at the target $V_{S30}$, and $S_a^{F}$ is the spectral ordinate obtained by applying the amplification factor $F$ to the reference-rock hazard curve per [@eq-site-af]. This envelope ensures that the controlling (most hazardous) estimate governs the design spectrum at every period. GMPEs may underpredict at certain periods or $V_{S30}$ ranges where their empirical site terms are poorly constrained by the underlying strong-motion database, while the amplification model may underpredict where the reference-rock hazard curve does not fully represent near-field or source-specific effects. The envelope eliminates the risk of unconservative design by selecting the larger ordinate at each spectral period.\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The rationale for the envelope approach -- that GMPEs may underpredict at poorly constrained $V_{S30}$ ranges while amplification models may underpredict for near-field or source-specific effects -- is technically sound and reflects known limitations of both approaches [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193][WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf]. The selection of the maximum ordinate at each period as the governing design value is a standard conservative design choice. No technical errors are identified.]\n\n## Site Classification {#sec-vs30_appendix}\n\nThe **Vs30** parameter is widely measured in the field using methods such as borehole logging, seismic cone penetration tests, and surface wave techniques. Introduced into engineering practice in the early 1990s, it provides a simple, robust metric to characterize site conditions for earthquake ground motions. **Vs30** is defined as the time-averaged shear-wave velocity in the upper 30 meters of a site profile. In practical terms, it represents the overall stiffness of the near-surface soils and rock. Mathematically, Vs30 is computed as the harmonic average of shear-wave velocity ($V_{S}$) over the top 30 m:\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The definition of $V_{S30}$ as the time-averaged (harmonic-mean) shear-wave velocity over the upper 30 m, its introduction in the early 1990s, and its measurement via borehole logging, seismic CPT, and surface-wave techniques are all well-established facts confirmed by extensive literature [WEB:https://www.usgs.gov/publications/mapping-nehrp-vs30-site-classes]. The characterisation of $V_{S30}$ as a measure of near-surface stiffness and its use as the primary site parameter in modern GMPEs is broadly supported.]\n\n$$\nV_{S30} = \\frac{30\\,\\text{m}}{\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\frac{H_i}{V_{S,i}}}\n$$\n\nwhere $H_i$ and $V_{S,i}$ are the thickness and shear-wave velocity of the $i$-th layer in the upper 30 m. This formula gives the equivalent uniform velocity that yields the same travel time through 30 m of material as the actual layered profile.\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The harmonic-mean formula $V_{S30} = 30\\,\\text{m} / \\sum_i (H_i / V_{S,i})$ is the standard definition of $V_{S30}$ and is consistent with the NEHRP provisions, ASCE 7-22, and all major building codes that adopt this parameter [WEB:https://www.usgs.gov/publications/mapping-nehrp-vs30-site-classes][WEB:https://www.daveboore.com/pubs_online/vs30_from_shallow_models.pdf]. The physical interpretation as the equivalent uniform velocity yielding the same travel time is mathematically correct.]\n\nVs30 has become the primary quantitative predictor of site effects in modern ground-motion models. Virtually all recent ground motion prediction equations (GMPEs) incorporate Vs30 as a key input parameter to adjust predicted shaking for site conditions. Empirical GMPEs (e.g., NGA-West2 and NGA-East) often use a functional form in which the median ground motion is scaled according to $\\ln(V_{S30})$ or a similar proxy, with separate terms for linear scaling and nonlinear saturation at low velocities. Large datasets have validated the use of Vs30 in GMPEs, showing consistent correlations between Vs30 and site amplification across many locations [@Borcherdt2012].\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The use of $V_{S30}$ as the primary site parameter in NGA-West2 and NGA-East GMPEs, with $\\ln(V_{S30})$-based functional forms and separate nonlinear terms, is well-established and confirmed by published GMPE documentation [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193][WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf]. The reference [@Borcherdt2012] is associated with R.D. Borcherdt, who has published extensively on site classification and $V_{S30}$-based site amplification factors; Borcherdt is a co-author of Dobry et al. (2000) [DOI:10.1193/1.1586082], the foundational paper introducing the NEHRP site coefficient system. The specific 2012 year in this citation could not be independently verified from publicly available sources; published Borcherdt works on site classification span multiple years (1994, 2000, and later). Confidence is MEDIUM due to the partially unverified citation year.]\n\nIn parallel, site-specific analyses (such as 1D site response simulations) rely on Vs30 as an initial classification of the site profile, although they incorporate the full shear-wave velocity profile with depth. While Vs30 does not capture all facets of site response -- such as basin depth or resonance effects -- it serves as a practical first-order proxy.\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The statement that site-specific 1D site response simulations use the full shear-wave velocity profile rather than relying solely on $V_{S30}$, and that $V_{S30}$ does not capture basin depth or resonance effects, is accurate and consistent with the established literature on site response analysis [WEB:https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/8755293019878193][WEB:https://opensha.org/resources/PSHA_Primer_v2_0.pdf]. The description of $V_{S30}$ as a practical first-order proxy is standard and well-supported.]\n\n### NEHRP Site Classes\n\nThe NEHRP provisions [-@BSSC2015] established the foundational site classification system used in building codes, defining Site Classes A through F based on Vs30 ranges. The 2020 edition of the NEHRP provisions (FEMA P-2082) introduced a substantially expanded nine-class system -- A, B, BC, C, CD, D, DE, E, and F -- replacing the six-class A-F framework [WEB:https://www.nist.gov/news-events/news/2021/02/2020-nehrp-recommended-seismic-provisions-new-buildings-and-other]. Three intermediate classes (BC, CD, DE) were added to provide finer-grained differentiation. Additionally, the 2020 edition requires that site classification be based solely on shear-wave velocity ($V_{S30}$); the 2015 edition allowed SPT blow count and undrained shear strength as alternative classification bases, but this option was removed in FEMA P-2082 [WEB:https://www.nist.gov/news-events/news/2021/02/2020-nehrp-recommended-seismic-provisions-new-buildings-and-other]. The 2020 provisions also introduced multi-period response spectra (MPRS), replacing the two-period ($S_S$, $S_1$) spectral shape. ASCE 7-22 and IBC 2021 implement the nine-class system in Table 20.2-1, superseding the six-class A-F boundaries from prior editions [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. The approximate Vs30 boundaries for the nine classes are:\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The expansion of the NEHRP site classification from six classes (A-F, FEMA P-1050) to nine classes (A, B, BC, C, CD, D, DE, E, F, FEMA P-2082) is confirmed by the NIST announcement of the 2020 NEHRP provisions [WEB:https://www.nist.gov/news-events/news/2021/02/2020-nehrp-recommended-seismic-provisions-new-buildings-and-other] and is consistent with the USGS ASCE 7-22 web service implementation [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. The transition to Vs30-only classification and the introduction of MPRS are confirmed from multiple secondary sources describing the 2020 NEHRP updates. Both prior audit sessions cited only BSSC2015 and the six-class system, flagging the outdated citation at MEDIUM confidence; this paragraph corrects that gap.]\n\n* **Site Class A:** Hard rock, $V_{S30} > 1500$ m/s.\n* **Site Class B:** Rock, $V_{S30} \\approx$ 760-1500 m/s (760 m/s is the B/BC boundary).\n* **Site Class BC:** Soft rock, $V_{S30} \\approx$ 530-760 m/s (new intermediate class).\n* **Site Class C:** Very dense soil or soft rock, $V_{S30} \\approx$ 365-530 m/s.\n* **Site Class CD:** Dense sand or very stiff clay, $V_{S30} \\approx$ 270-365 m/s (new intermediate class).\n* **Site Class D:** Stiff soil, $V_{S30} \\approx$ 185-270 m/s.\n* **Site Class DE:** Loose sand or medium stiff clay, $V_{S30} \\approx$ 150-185 m/s (new intermediate class).\n* **Site Class E:** Soft clay, $V_{S30} < 150$ m/s (boundary adjusted from 180 m/s in prior editions).\n* **Site Class F:** Soils requiring site-specific evaluation (e.g., very soft clays, peats, liquefiable soils, or thick high-plasticity clays).\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The nine-class structure and the approximate Vs30 boundaries listed are consistent with descriptions of ASCE 7-22 Table 20.2-1 as implemented in FEMA P-2082, and with the USGS ASCE 7-22 web service [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. The specific numerical thresholds for intermediate classes BC, CD, and DE (530, 365/270, 185/150 m/s) are synthesized from secondary sources and should be verified against ASCE 7-22 Table 20.2-1 in the normative standard document, which is behind a paywall. Confidence is MEDIUM for the specific intermediate-class boundaries; the nine-class structure and the qualitative descriptions are HIGH confidence.]\n\nClass F is defined by qualitative subsoil conditions rather than a $V_{S30}$ threshold; if those conditions are met, a detailed site response analysis is mandated in lieu of using generic amplification factors. In structural design, each non-F class is assigned amplification factors (tabulated in ASCE 7-22 as functions of $V_{S30}$ and ground-motion intensity) that modify the multi-period response spectrum to account for local site effects [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html].\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The description of Class F as requiring site-specific analysis and the role of non-F classes in providing tabulated amplification factors are consistent with NEHRP and ASCE 7-22 provisions [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html][WEB:https://www.usgs.gov/publications/mapping-nehrp-vs30-site-classes]. The reference to multi-period response spectrum amplification factors (replacing the two-factor $F_a$, $F_v$ system from prior editions in the 2020 NEHRP context) is consistent with the FEMA P-2082 framework. The substance is accurate and well-supported.]\n\n### AS1726 Guidelines\n\nThe Australian Standard AS 1726-2017 [-@AS1726-2017] provides guidelines for conducting geotechnical investigations and includes a site classification system that categorises sites into classes based on their soil properties, such as shear wave velocity parameter $\\bar v_S$, depth to bedrock, and soil type. The Australian Standard AS 1170.4 \"Earthquake Actions on Structures\" uses a related site classification system based on the stiffness of the ground, but it does not explicitly use the shear wave velocity parameter $\\bar v_S$ to classify the ground. The categories range from A (rock) to E (soft soil), with a special category (S) for sites requiring site-specific investigations.\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: AS 1726-2017 is a legitimate Australian Standard for geotechnical site investigations and AS 1170.4 is the Australian Standard for earthquake actions on structures. The described A-through-E-plus-S classification structure, and the distinction that AS 1170.4 does not explicitly use $\\bar v_S$ while AS 1726-2017 does, are consistent with the known development of Australian seismic provisions. Both standards are behind a paywall, limiting independent external verification of the specific classification details described here. Confidence is MEDIUM due to restricted access to the normative source documents.]\n\n{{< include /psha/_tbl/AS1726.qmd >}}\n\n### ASCE/SEI 7-22 Guidelines\n\nThe ASCE/SEI 7-22 Standard [-@ASCE722] provides guidelines for conducting geotechnical investigations and includes a site classification system that categorises sites into classes based on their soil properties, such as shear wave velocity, depth to bedrock, and soil type. According to ASCE 7-22 [@ASCE722], the site soil shall be classified based on the average shear wave velocity parameter, $\\bar v_S$, which is derived from the measured shear wave velocity profile from the ground surface to a depth of 30 m.\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: ASCE 7-22 Chapter 20 specifies site classification based on $\\bar v_S$ (average shear-wave velocity in the upper 30 m, equivalent to $V_{S30}$), along with average standard penetration test blow count and undrained shear strength. The description of the classification being based on soil properties including shear wave velocity, depth to bedrock, and soil type is accurate [WEB:https://earthquake.usgs.gov/ws/designmaps/asce7-22.html]. The notation $\\bar v_S$ (overbar) is used in ASCE 7-22 to denote the parameter previously labeled $V_{S30}$ in earlier editions. The claim is well-supported.]\n\n{{< include /psha/_tbl/ASCE722.qmd >}}\n\n### NBC-2020 Guidelines\n\nThe National Building Code of Canada [-@NBC2020] provides guidelines for site characterization and seismic site response. Similar to ASCE 7-22, it uses an average shear wave velocity in the upper 30 m (commonly denoted $v_{S30}$), standard penetration test (SPT) blow count, and/or undrained shear strength ($s_{u}$) to classify sites into categories A through F. Where site-specific investigations are warranted (e.g., liquefiable soils, highly sensitive clays, peat), the code requires more detailed analysis.\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The National Building Code of Canada 2020 (NBC 2020) includes seismic site classification provisions that use $v_{S30}$, SPT blow count, and undrained shear strength $s_u$ for site categories A through F, consistent with the description provided. The classification structure is aligned with the NEHRP/ASCE 7 framework. The NBC is not freely available in full text, preventing complete independent verification of the exact classification thresholds and procedure for the 2020 edition. The description is technically plausible and consistent with the known evolution of Canadian seismic provisions, but Confidence is MEDIUM due to limited publicly accessible external verification of the specific NBC 2020 provisions.]\n\n{{< include /psha/_tbl/NBC2020.qmd >}}\n","numLines":80,"startLine":179,"totalLines":258}}}
